for accurate automatic image classification. By training models using existing datasets
\par
of images classified by citizen scientists and subsequent application of such models on
\par
new studies, human effort may be reduced substantially. The goals of this study were
\par
to (a) assess the accuracy of deep learning in classifying camera trap data, (b) investi-
\par
gate how to process datasets with only a few classified images that are generally dif-
\par
ficult to model, and (c) apply a trained model on a live online citizen science project.
\par
2. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) were used to differentiate among images
\par
of different animal species, images of humans or vehicles, and empty images (no
\par
animals, vehicles, or humans). We used four different camera trap datasets featur-
\par
ing a wide variety of species, different habitats, and a varying number of images.
\par
All datasets were labelled by citizen scientists on Zooniverse.
\par
3. Accuracies for identifying empty images across projects ranged between 91.2\% and
\par
98.0\%, whereas accuracies for identifying specific species were between 88.7\% and
\par
92.7\%. Transferring information from CNNs trained on large datasets (``transfer-
\par
learning'') was increasingly beneficial as the size of the training dataset decreased
\par
and raised accuracy by up to 10.3\%. Removing low-confidence predictions increased
\par
model accuracies to the level of citizen scientists. By combining a trained model with
\par
classifications from citizen scientists, human effort was reduced by 43\% while main-
\par
taining overall accuracy for a live experiment running on Zooniverse.
\par
4. Ecology researchers can significantly reduce image classification time and manual
\par
effort by combining citizen scientists and CNNs, enabling faster processing of
\par
data from large camera trap studies.
