1 \textbar{} INTRODUC TION
\par
Ecologists often deploy camera traps to study wildlife populations in
\par
areas of interest (O'Connell, Nichols, \& Karanth, 2011). Such cameras
\par
are remote, independent devices, triggered by motion and infrared
\par
sensors that provide researchers with images of passing animals (e.g.,
\par
see Figure 1). After collection, images have to be classified according
\par
to the study's goals to produce useful ecological data for analysis.
\par
Recent studies encompass millions of images (e.g., Swanson et al.,
\par
2015), which is too many for research teams to manually process and
\par
classify within a reasonable time-\- frame. Citizen science is a valuable
\par
approach in such cases, as it enables harnessing the collective effort
\par
of volunteers from the general population (citizen scientists). Citizen
\par
scientists have become essential contributors to general science,
\par
ecology, and camera trap projects (Dickinson, Zuckerberg, \& Bonter,
\par
2010; Silvertown, 2009; Swanson et al., 2015). Species identification
\par
leveraging the collective labour of thousands of citizen scientists
\par
can result in accurate classifications (Swanson, Kosmala, Lintott, \&
\par
Packer, 2016) while reducing the time to process large datasets.
\par
science platforms, for example, images of humans for privacy rea-
\par
sons or images of rare species to prevent exposing their location to
\par
poachers. Additionally, some camera trap projects contain a large
\par
number of empty images (containing no animals), caused when cam-
\par
era traps are triggered by insignificant objects like overgrown vege-
\par
tation moving in the wind. Research teams often aim at identifying
\par
and removing empty images as efficiently as possible, for example,
\par
Hines, Swanson, Kosmala, and Lintott (2015).
\par
To address these issues, automatic classification of camera trap
\par
images has been a focus of research in computer vision and machine
\par
learning. Recent advances in using techniques from deep learning
\par
have enabled researchers to improve automatic species identifica-
\par
tion significantly (Gomez Villa, Salazar, \& Vargas, 2017; Norouzzadeh
\par
et al., 2018).
\par
1.1 \textbar{} Citizen science and camera trap projects
\par
Zooniverse (www.zooniverse.org) is the largest online citizen
\par
science platform and provides researchers with access to mil-
\par
lions of volunteers (Fortson et al., 2012; Swanson et al., 2015;
\par
Zevin et al., 2016). The platform allows researchers to
\par
build a custom project website (using the ``project builder'',
\par
www.zooniverse.org/lab) and to supply guidelines, additional
\par
information, classification data, and annotation tools for citizen
\par
scientists (see Supporting Information for an example). Research
\par
teams define workflows, which comprise specific tasks the citi-
\par
zen scientists are asked to perform, for example, identifying ani-
\par
mals in camera trap images. Zooniverse has hosted 147 public
\par
projects as of May 2018 of which 29 were camera trap projects.
\par
Collectively, the camera trap projects contributed over 17 mil-
\par
lion identification tasks for which citizen scientists submitted
\par
more than 63 million classifications. The first camera trap pro-
\par
ject (Snapshot Serengeti) was launched in 2012, whereas 12 new
\par
camera trap projects have been published between January and
\par
May 2018 alone. During this time the total number of projects on
\par
Zooniverse has exploded by a factor of 7.4 to 147, whereas the
\par
number of registered volunteers has only grown by a factor of 2.4
\par
to over 1.7 million. This has increased the competition for volun-
\par
teers and is leading to increasing delays between data collection
\par
and subsequent analyses.
\par
Further concerns for some camera trap projects include the
\par
need to remove sensitive images before publishing them on citizen
\par
1.2 \textbar{} Machine learning and image classification
\par
In machine learning, particularly supervised learning, the goal is to
\par
learn how to map inputs (data) to outputs of interest by applying
\par
specific learning algorithms. In the case of image classification, the
\par
aim is to train an algorithm which can process and label images with
\par
classes of interest (e.g., animal species). In recent years, image clas-
\par
sification has been dominated by convolutional neural networks
\par
(CNNs) as demonstrated in recent ImageNet Large Scale Visual
\par
Recognition Challenges (ILSVRC) (Krizhevsky, Sutskever, \& Hinton,
\par
2012; Russakovsky et al., 2015).
\par
CNNs (LeCun et al., 1989) consist of two linked main compo-
\par
nents, a convolutional part which extracts local features from images
\par
and a fully connected part which maps the learned features to out-
\par
puts. Unlike earlier methods, CNNs do not rely on manually defined
\par
features. Instead, the network learns spatial features by updating its
\par
parameters (weights) during model training by the propagation of
\par
errors from the output towards the input. The exact structure of
\par
a CNN (the sequence of operations applied to the data by the net-
\par
work) is known as its architecture. Figure 2 schematically shows the
\par
architecture of a CNN and its main module (a layer) which consists of
\par
filters which are convolved over the input (to measure their spatial
\par
occurrence), activation functions, and pooling (sub-\- sampling) oper-
\par
ations. A layer normally results in smaller feature maps which are
\par
then passed to the next layer. Typically, many such layers are stacked
\par
sequentially allowing for complex feature extraction. The number
\par
of layers in an architecture is referred to as the depth of a network,
\par
thus, deep learning implies neural networks with many layers.
\par
CNNs often are very complex and can contain millions of param-
\par
eters which all have to be learned from training data. The more com-
\par
plex a model, that is, the more parameters it has, the more training
\par
data are usually required to learn suitable parameter values. A lack
