\par
1.3 \textbar{} Automatic animal identification
\par
Initial research in automatic animal identification focused on match-
\par
ing species-\- specific patterns in images and required a substantial
\par
amount of manual preprocessing. Even so, the accuracies that were
\par
achieved, for example, 82\% by Yu et al. (2013), were not comparable
\par
to human accuracies of 96.6\% (Swanson et al., 2016). More recent
\par
studies in automatically identifying animal species using CNNs have
\par
reported accuracies around 90\% with some manual preprocessing
\par
(Gomez Villa et al., 2017) or more complex pipelines involving auto-
\par
matic preprocessing (Giraldo-\- Zuluaga, Salazar, Gomez, \& Diaz-\- Pulido,
\par
2017). The most recent advances by Norouzzadeh et al. (2018) have
\par
reported accuracies of 93.8\% and have matched human accuracy on
\par
over 99\% of all images.
\par
Our study aims at applying and validating the use of CNNs on a
\par
larger variety of camera trap datasets as compared to previous stud-
\par
ies. Norouzzadeh et al. (2018) reported impressive accuracies using
\par
the Snapshot Serengeti dataset which comprises 3.2 million images
\par
(Swanson et al., 2015)---much more than most camera trap datasets
\par
as observed on Zooniverse. Training effective image classification
\par
models often requires large datasets (e.g., the famous ImageNet
\par
dataset contains 1.2 million images) and thus makes that approach
\par
feasible for datasets of the size of Snapshot Serengeti. To investi-
\par
gate the applicability of CNNs in more realistic (smaller) scenarios,
\par
we included several smaller datasets each with significantly fewer
\par
than one million images.
\par
Furthermore, using transfer-learning, our work investigates how
\par
to leverage models trained on large camera trap datasets to smaller
\par
datasets. This technique is well-\- known and was applied in previous
\par
studies (Gomez Villa et al., 2017; Norouzzadeh et al., 2018), how-
\par
ever, by transferring models trained on noncamera trap datasets
\par
(e.g., ImageNet) which is expected to be less effective than trans-
\par
ferring from models trained to accomplish a similar task (i.e., animal
\par
identification). We transferred data from a model trained on a more
\par
recent version of the Snapshot Serengeti dataset comprising 7.3 mil-
\par
lion images. Transfer-\- learning could help citizen science platforms like
\par
Zooniverse to more quickly train and use models for new datasets
\par
with few labelled images.
\par
Finally, our study is the first to demonstrate and evaluate a use
\par
case of how trained models can be applied to classify new camera
\par
trap images by combining human annotations and model classifi-
\par
cations in real time on an online citizen science platform. Previous
\par
studies, for example, Norouzzadeh et al. (2018), estimated the re-
\par
duction in human effort for classifying new images by extrapolat-
\par
ing model performance as measured on historical data and without
\par
combining human annotations with model predictions.
