2.1 \textbar{} Datasets
\par
We used four camera trap datasets in this study (see Table 1 for
\par
overview and Supporting Information for descriptions) which
\par
were collected by different research teams. Each dataset con-
\par
sisted of camera trap images and their annotations provided
\par
by citizen scientists on Zooniverse. The images of three data-
\par
sets were collected in Africa: Snapshot Serengeti (SS), Camera
\par
CATalogue (CC), and Elephant Expedition (EE), whereas images
\par
from Snapshot Wisconsin (SW) were collected in North America.
\par
These datasets differed in aspects such as dataset size, camera
\par
placement, camera configuration, and species coverage which
\par
allows for drawing more general conclusions. Furthermore, the
\par
SS dataset was used in previous machine learning studies, thus
\par
facilitating detailed comparisons of model results.
\par
The datasets featured between nine and 55 species and exhib-
\par
ited significant imbalance in how often different species were photo-
\par
graphed: Pearson's moment coefficient of skewness ranged between
\par
1.58 and 5 (see Supporting Information for species distributions).
\par
Extremely rare species (photographed {$<$}27 times) were excluded
\par
from the study in training the models to ensure images of a specific
\par
