\par
2.3 \textbar{} Model training
\par
2.3.1 \textbar{} Model architecture and parameters
\par
To balance accuracy and computational costs, all models were
\par
trained using the ResNet18 model architecture. ResNet18 is a less
\par
complex version of ResNet152, the latter of which won the ILSVRC
\par
in 2015 (He, Zhang, Ren, \& Sun, 2016). Comparisons of a variety
\par
of state-\- of-\- the art CNN architectures on an earlier version of the
\par
SS dataset by Norouzzadeh et al. (2018) showed that ResNet18 is
\par
only 0.5\% less accurate than the best model evaluated by them. The
\par
model contains over 11 million parameters, has 18 convolutional lay-
\par
ers and is smaller than many other state-\- of-\- the art models, thus re-
\par
ducing training time. To train the models we used Tensorflow (Abadi
\par
et al., 2016), an open-\- source graph computation framework, and
\par
servers hosted on Amazon Web Services (AWS) using one GPU with
\par
12 GB of RAM. Details about parameter choices and image preproc-
\par
essing can be found in the Supporting Information.
\par
2.3.3 \textbar{} Two models: identifying empty
\par
images and species
\par
For each dataset we trained two different models. One model dif-
\par
ferentiated between empty and nonempty images, whereas the sec-
\par
ond model differentiated between species. For the CC dataset the
\par
first model also included vehicles as a separate class. The reasons
\par
for training two different models were: (a) to reduce the likely biases
\par
models would have towards the majority class, which was expected
\par
to be significant as the share of empty images was generally high,
\par
and (b) to more closely resemble the application of the model in the
\par
live experiment (``cascade filtering'') aimed at increasing accuracy by
\par
more closely learning the target task.
\par
The reported results for the postprocessed datasets do not con-
\par
sider applying the two models sequentially. This introduced a pos-
\par
itive bias to the species models as they were evaluated on images
\par
we were certain contained an animal, that is, the model could not
\par
make the mistake of predicting a species when the image was empty.
\par
Instead, the evaluation of the CC live experiment (see section 2.6)
\par
did consider this sequence and thus resembled a realistic scenario.
\par
We did not directly (i.e., without transfer-\- learning) apply models
\par
trained on one dataset to another dataset even if the identification
\par
of empty images is the same task. Due to the substantial differences
\par
in vegetation, study sites, camera placement, and species we did not
\par
expect our models to generalize well to other datasets. We believe,
\par
however, this is an interesting and fruitful area of research.
\par
2.3.2 \textbar{} Data splitting
\par
We trained independent models for each dataset by randomly as-
\par
signing all capture events of a dataset, while preserving class
\par
2.4 \textbar{} Transfer-\- learning
\par
In order to investigate the possibilities of achieving high accuracies
\par
on smaller training datasets, we implemented the transfer-\- learning
\par
technique. Transfer-\- learning (Yosinski, Clune, Bengio, \& Lipson,
\par
2014) involves copying the weights learned on a base model to a
\par
target model. This potentially improves model accuracies, reduces
\par
model training time, and reduces the amount of labelled data re-
\par
quired. To distinguish how we trained our models we refer to train-
\par
ing without transfer-\- learning as training from scratch.
\par
