\par
For transfer-\- learning the base models used in this study were the
\par
models trained on the SS dataset. Those models were trained on the
\par
largest dataset and featured a wide variety of species and thus were
\par
expected to learn features represented in the other projects. We ap-
\par
plied transfer-\- learning by copying and freezing all the weights from
\par
the convolutional layers (feature extraction) and only re-\- learned
\par
the weights of the fully connected layers (see Figure 2). Initial ex-
\par
periments did not show an additional benefit of re-\- learning weights
\par
of convolutional layers (The accuracy of the CC species model was
\par
1.5\% lower when allowing for adaptation of all weights, the accuracy
\par
of the EE species model was 0.2\% lower. We point out that different
\par
model training parameters could influence these results).
\par
2.5 \textbar{} Model output and confidence thresholding
\par
The CNN outputs probabilities over all classes for each image,
\par
that is, if we train a model to distinguish between 20 classes it
\par
will output 20 probabilities for each image. To assign a class to a
\par
particular capture event, we assigned the class with the highest
\par
probability across all images of the capture event. This probability
\par
can be interpreted as a confidence measure in the model's pre-
\par
diction; the higher the predicted probability, the more confident
\par
the prediction. This allowed us to apply confidence thresholding
\par
which ignores the model's opinion if its highest probability was
\par
below a certain threshold. By ignoring low-\- confidence predictions
\par
the overall accuracy of the model can be increased while reducing
\par
coverage (the share of images for which we considered the model
\par
prediction).
\par
2.6 \textbar{} Model application on a live zooniverse project
\par
While the above methods describe how we modelled camera trap
\par
data that has already been recorded and annotated, we also studied
\par
the impact of applying a trained model on newly collected images in
\par
a live project on Zooniverse.
\par
Our goal was to decrease the number of volunteer annotations
\par
required by considering the output of a trained model for a camera
\par
trap project on Zooniverse. We conducted an experiment where ma-
\par
chine predictions were combined with human annotations for a new
\par
batch of CC images, which was not used in the rest of this study.
\par
To evaluate the experiment we assessed the efficiency gain and the
\par
impact on the quality of the final aggregated labels.
\par
We randomly assigned 50\% of all capture events to an experi-
\par
ment group, retaining the rest as a control group, and implemented
\par
new image retirement thresholds for this experiment. In the (non-
\par
experiment) ``cascade filtering'' workflow of CC, capture events in
\par
the ``Empty or Not'' and ``Vehicle or Not'' workflows were retired if
\par
the first two volunteers agreed with each other (more otherwise),
\par
whereas five volunteer annotations were required for the species
\par
workflow, regardless of agreement. For the experiment, we altered
\par
the logic such that if the first volunteer agreed with the model, the
\par
capture event was retired if it was in the ``Empty or Not'' or ``Vehicle
\par
or Not'' workflow. For the ``species'' workflow, an image was retired
\par
Methods in Ecology and Evoluon
\par
\textbar{} 85
\par
if the first two volunteers agreed with the model. In cases where
\par
there was no agreement with the model, the capture event fell back
\par
to the nonexperiment logic. Images were excluded from the exper-
\par
iment where the model's top prediction was a rare species (fewer
\par
than 2,000 images in the dataset), a species with low historical ac-
\par
curacy ({$<$}90\%), or resulted in a model prediction below 85\% (only
\par
for species predictions). After these exclusions 17 species remained
\par
in the dataset (totalling over 88\% of all species images as classified
\par
by the model) plus all predicted empty and vehicle images. To clas-
\par
sify the images, the empty model was applied first and the species
\par
model was subsequently applied to the images classified as con-
\par
taining a species by the first model (see discussion in section 2.3.3).
\par
To evaluate the classification quality we simulated the applica-
\par
tion of the experiment logic on the control group and compared the
\par
labels of the simulation with the effective labels as annotated by
\par
the citizen scientists. To evaluate the efficiency gain we measured
\par
the reduction in annotations until all capture events were classified.
\par
Therefore, the sole utility of the experiment group was to show
\par
the new logic could be implemented technically while still profiting
\par
from efficiency gains on a live project. The control group was used
\par
for the quantitative evaluation. (Due to the random splitting into
\par
control-and experiment group we can reasonably assume that the
\par
results of the control group are representative of the experiment
\par
group)
\par
3 \textbar{} RESULTS
\par
To evaluate our models, we report the accuracy as the percentage
\par
of correct model predictions for camera trap events compared to
\par
the aggregated volunteer opinion (ground truth). To put the reported
\par
accuracies in context: Citizen scientists classifying species images
\par
from Serengeti National Park reached an aggregated accuracy of
\par
96.6\% (Swanson et al., 2016) as compared to expert opinion. See
\par
Supporting Information for results and a discussion regarding statis-
\par
tical uncertainties. Generally, the width of the 95\% confidence inter-
\par
vals ranged between 0.3\% and 2.0\% and were inversely correlated
\par
with the size of the modelled datasets.
\par
3.1 \textbar{} Model accuracies---training from scratch vs.
\par
transfer-\- learning
\par
Model accuracies for all trained models are shown in Figure 3.
\par
Transfer-\- learning outperformed training from scratch on all
\par
datasets for the species models. Even the SW dataset substan-
\par
tially profited from transfer-\- learning, although there was no
\par
species overlap and the vegetation is quite different as com-
\par
pared to the SS dataset. Similarly, the models identifying empty
\par
images profited from transfer-\- learning with the exception of
\par
the CC dataset (0.3\% lower accuracy -\- statistically significant).
\par
Identifying empty images consistently outperformed the spe-
\par
cies models by 2.4\%--5.9\% when trained from scratch and by
\par
0\%--5.4\% when trained with transfer-\- learning.
