3.4 \textbar{} Live experiment
\par
3.4.1 \textbar{} Efficiency gain
\par
To process all experiment-\- eligible capture events, 49\% fewer annotations
\par
by citizen scientists were required. Accounting for the noneligible subjects
\par
for which the model supplied no opinion and thus were processed by the
\par
standard logic, 43\% fewer annotations were needed to retire all images.
\par
3.4.2 \textbar{} Classification quality
\par
Only 0.22\% of all capture events had a different final label due to the
\par
newly defined logic (Accordingly, the accuracy of the human-model
\par
combination was 99.78\%. Using only model predictions would have
\par
resulted in an accuracy of 93.4\%). Visual inspection showed that
\par
most of these cases were either due to difficulty spotting obscured
\par
animals by the first volunteer (and the model), or concurrence of
\par
humans and vehicles in an image, causing confusion by volunteers
\par
whether to choose the human or vehicle class. In total 91.2\% of all
\par
images were retired with fewer annotations because the first volun-
\par
teers agreed with the model. In 8.6\% of the cases, the machine and
\par
the first volunteers disagreed, and the standard logic was applied for
\par
retirement. However, in 47\% of the disagreements, the final plurality
\par
label matched the model label, indicating the first volunteer(s) were
\par
incorrect about half the time when there was a disagreement.
