\par
carefully assess model performance and re-\- train models if needed.
\par
Researchers should also be careful with equating the output of a
\par
CNN to real probabilities. Studies have shown that deep neural net-
\par
works tend to be overly confident in their predictions (Guo, Pleiss,
\par
Sun, \& Weinberger, 2017).
\par
The aim of an ecology project may go beyond simple species
\par
identification. Researchers might want to additionally evaluate an-
\par
imal behaviour, differentiate between adults and young, male or fe-
\par
male individuals, or obtain count estimates of animals in a particular
\par
image. Though some studies used models to localize and count dif-
\par
ferent animals in images, they required datasets with bounding boxes
\par
or other localized annotations in order to train a model (Parham \&
\par
Stewart, 2016; Zhang, He, Cao, \& Cao, 2016). Norouzzadeh et al.
\par
(2018) demonstrated, however, that categorical (nonlocalized) la-
\par
bels can be sufficient to train a model to jointly identify species and
\par
other labels provided by citizen scientists, such as count or animal
\par
behaviour categories.
