\par
using as few as two volunteer classifications. This would enable data
\par
from 900 camera traps to be processed by the same cohort of cit-
\par
izen scientists within a similar time-\- frame. However, it is important
\par
to consider how volunteer experience changes when citizen science
\par
projects incorporate machine predictions and how that affects the
\par
project's success, quality, and number of annotations (see Bowyer,
\par
Maidel, Lintott, Swanson, and Miller (2015) for impact on volunteer
\par
engagement when controlling for the proportion of empty images).
\par
We note that the implementation and usage of such models is not a
\par
trivial task and therefore aim to provide the necessary software and
\par
guidelines to effectively assist ecologists who wish to apply them
\par
(see section 7 for more information). Our results validate the adop-
\par
tion of machine learning as a viable strategy to address the over-
\par
whelming data challenge facing ecologists and strongly motivate the
\par
requisite investment of time and effort. Moreover, we demonstrate
\par
the great potential of powerful machine learning algorithms to rein-
\par
force citizen scientist analysis of large camera trap studies.
\par
4.3 \textbar{} Outlook
\par
A next step for citizen science platforms like Zooniverse or research
\par
teams could be to automate camera trap classification. Integrating
\par
pretrained models into their data-\- processing pipelines and combin-
\par
ing them with annotations from experts or citizen scientists as they
\par
become available (referred to as online-\- learning) could further re-
\par
duce processing times. Ideally, such a process would produce proba-
\par
bilistic estimates of classifications to make better decisions in how
\par
to handle them, for example, whether to have them reviewed by an
\par
expert. Ideas on how to aggregate annotations from several, inde-
\par
pendent citizen scientists and combine them with model predictions
\par
is an active area of research (e.g., Branson, Horn, \& Perona, 2017;
\par
Simpson, Roberts, Psorakis, \& Smith, 2013). Ideally, the output of
\par
a model should represent probability distributions over all classes,
\par
which is an active area of research in Bayesian neural networks (e.g.,
\par
Gal, 2016).
\par
With increasing dataset sizes, computational capabilities, and
\par
improved model architectures, we expect accuracies of deep
\par
learning models to further increase. A promising field of research
\par
aims to reduce the number of training labels required, for example,
\par
Gal and Ghahramani (2015), which would be particularly beneficial
\par
for rare species. Furthermore, parameter tuning and model ensem-
\par
bling usually yield better results, for example, Norouzzadeh et al.
\par
(2018). Also, for projects with multiple images per capture event,
\par
motion patterns between subsequent images can contain valu-
\par
able information to improve the detection of empty images (see
\par
Supporting Information for a case study) or to additionally classify
\par
animal behaviour.
\par
Combining deep learning with the effort of citizen scientists
\par
may enable large camera trap studies to substantially expand their
\par
networks. For example the SS study operated over 200 camera
\par
traps in 2013 and required nine volunteer classifications per capture
\par
event to annotate those data. Using the approach described in this
