Abstract
\par
Background and Objective Precision (stratified or personalised) medicine is underpinned by the premise that it is feasible
\par
to identify known heterogeneity using a specific test or algorithm in patient populations and to use this information to guide
\par
patient care to improve health and well-being. This study aimed to understand if, and how, previous economic evaluations
\par
of precision medicine had taken account of the impact of capacity constraints.
\par
Methods A meta-review was conducted of published systematic reviews of economic evaluations of precision medicine
\par
(test--treat interventions) and individual studies included in these reviews. Due to the volume of studies identified, a sample
\par
of papers published from 2007 to 2015 was collated. A narrative analysis identified whether potential capacity constraints
\par
were discussed qualitatively in the studies and, if relevant, which quantitative methods were used to account for capacity
\par
constraints.
\par
Results A total of 45 systematic reviews of economic evaluations of precision medicine were identified, from which 222
\par
studies focusing on test--treat interventions, published between 2007 and 2015, were extracted. Of these studies, 33 (15\%)
\par
qualitatively discussed the potential impact of capacity constraints, including budget constraints; quality of tests and the
\par
testing process; ease of use of tests in clinical practice; and decision uncertainty. Quantitative methods (nine studies) to
\par
account for capacity constraints included static methods such as capturing inefficiencies in trials or models and sensitivity
\par
analysis around model parameters; and dynamic methods, which allow the impact of capacity constraints on cost effective-
\par
ness to change over time.
\par
Conclusions Understanding the cost effectiveness of precision medicine is necessary, but not sufficient, evidence for its
\par
successful implementation. There are currently few examples of evaluations that have quantified the impact of capacity
\par
constraints, which suggests an area of focus for future research.
