Vol.:(0123456789)1012 1 Introduction
\par
Precision (stratified or personalised) medicine is under-
\par
pinned by the premise that it is feasible to identify known
\par
heterogeneity using a specific test or algorithm in patient
\par
populations to guide patient care to improve health and
\par
well-being [1]. There is no unified definition of precision
\par
(stratified or personalised) medicine, but in practice to date
\par
it refers to using some test--treat combination to target an
\par
intervention [1, 2]. A variety of mechanisms are under-
\par
going development to identify such heterogeneity in the
\par
outcomes of interventions and progression of disease in
\par
populations of patients informed by genomic, proteomic,
\par
transcriptomic and metabolomic strategies [2]. The ability
\par
to determine which patients might be more likely to benefit
\par
from a treatment, avoid adverse effects or experience more
\par
severe disease has driven the theoretical arguments that
\par
precision medicine is a cost-effective use of healthcare
\par
resources [3, 4].
\par
Determining the incremental cost effectiveness of the
\par
strategies to deliver precision medicine (hereafter short-
\par
ened to precision medicine) is important because diverting
\par
funding to such newer interventions will involve the real-
\par
location of resources from other areas of medicine. The
\par
reallocation of funding may affect the health outcomes for
\par
relevant populations of patients, representing the oppor-
\par
tunity cost of the new intervention. Economic evaluations
\par
provide a structured framework to provide evidence sup-
\par
porting whether the introduction of precision medicine is
\par
an effective use of healthcare resources.
\par
There is some economic evidence supporting exem-
\par
plars of precision medicine [5]. However, even with such
\par
economic evidence, the introduction of precision medi-
\par
cine into health systems has been slower than anticipated,
\par
potentially due to the volume of eligible patients. There
\par
is emerging anecdotal, and some empirical, evidence
\par
of factors limiting the uptake of precision medicine. In
\par
2015, the charity Cancer Research UK (CRUK) published
\par
a report highlighting the significant delays in providing
\par
genetic mutation testing to patients [6] and estimated that
\par
in the previous year approximately 3500 patients may
\par
have missed out on receiving a medicine that may have
\par
improved their quality and length of life because of the
\par
absence of relevant mutation testing. Questions about
\par
the ability of the UK National Health Service (NHS) to
\par
implement one of the case study examples of precision
\par
medicine, EGFR mutation testing and gefitinib, a spe-
\par
cific epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) inhibitor
\par
licenced for patients with EGFR mutation-positive non-
\par
small cell lung cancer, without ``substantial investment in
\par
time and resources'' have been raised. It was believed that
\par
the NHS did have capacity to introduce such testing in
\par
S. J. Wright et al.
\par
the short timeframe [7]. However, this proved not to be
\par
the case, with the CRUK report suggesting that only 52\%
\par
of patients received EGFR mutation testing even 4 years
\par
after the approval of gefitinib by the National Institute for
\par
health and Care Excellence (NICE) [6]. This case study
\par
introduces the potential for capacity constraints to be a
\par
key barrier to the introduction of precision medicine, even
\par
when these have been shown to be cost effective in clini-
\par
cal practice.
\par
No consensus definition of what constitutes a capacity
\par
constraint in the context of healthcare interventions exists in
\par
the literature. The Oxford English Dictionary (OED) defines
\par
capacity as ``the amount that something can produce'' [8].
\par
This definition implies that it is necessary to be clear what is
\par
being produced. The capacity of a healthcare system could
\par
therefore be defined as its ability to produce some defined
\par
output. In keeping with the extra-welfarist normative under-
\par
pinning, assumed by decision-making bodies such as NICE,
\par
the relevant output of a healthcare system has been defined
\par
as `health status' measured using the quality-adjusted life-
\par
year (QALY). The definition of a constraint is ``something
\par
that controls what you do by keeping you within particu-
\par
lar limits'' [9]. Combining the definitions of capacity and
\par
constraint can be used to propose a working definition of
\par
a capacity constraint in a healthcare system which has the
\par
goal of maximising health status: `Any factor which impedes
\par
or limits the amount of health status produced for a popula-
\par
tion of patients receiving specified interventions, or policies,
\par
provided by the healthcare system'.
\par
The introduction of any healthcare intervention may be
\par
impeded by capacity constraints in a healthcare system,
\par
which may be particularly extensive and significant for
\par
examples of precision medicine due to their nature as com-
\par
plex interventions involving both a test and treatment ele-
\par
ment [10]. There is some qualitative evidence describing the
\par
type of capacity constraints directly relevant to the uptake of
\par
precision medicine into practice, including a lack of labo-
\par
ratories providing tests, poor logistics resulting in slow test
\par
turnaround, a lack of training for clinicians, and insufficient
\par
funding for testing or treatments [11--13]. The impact of such
\par
capacity constraints on the incremental cost effectiveness of
\par
precision medicine has not been well-described. This study
\par
aimed to identify a sample of published economic evalua-
\par
tions of precision medicine and describe if, and how, these
\par
economic evaluations had qualitatively discussed and quan-
\par
titatively accounted for capacity constraints in the analysis.
\par
2 Methods
\par
This study used a two-stage systematic review conducted
\par
and reported in accordance with published guidelines and
\par
reporting criteria [14]. A published search of PubMed byAccounting for Capacity Constraints in Economic Evaluations of Precision Medicine
\par
1013
\par
Payne and colleagues [5] suggested that there were a sub-
\par
stantive number of previously published systematic reviews
\par
of economic evaluations of precision medicine and related
\par
areas such as personalised medicine, pharmacogenetics and
\par
pharmacogenomics. Therefore, a de novo systematic review
\par
that aimed to identify all previous economic evaluations of
\par
precision medicine would overlap significantly with this
\par
previous body of work, requiring significant resources in
\par
terms of researcher time but unlikely to yield substantively
\par
different findings. This study therefore used a strategy to
\par
identify and collate published systematic reviews of eco-
\par
nomic evaluations of precision medicine. From these sys-
\par
tematic reviews a sample of economic evaluations of test
\par
and treatment-based examples of precision medicine were
\par
identified. The identified sample of economic evaluations
\par
were published between 2007, when the use of terms related
\par
to precision medicine began to occur regularly in the litera-
\par
ture [2], and February 2017.
\par
For the purpose of this review precision medicine was
\par
defined as an intervention that uses, for example, a test to
\par
``identify subgroups of patients with distinct mechanisms of
\par
disease, or particular responses to treatments'' [1]. Related
\par
areas included were therefore as follows: precision medi-
\par
cine; stratified medicine; individualised medicine; genetic
\par
medicine; genomic medicine; personalised medicine; and
\par
targeted medicine [15]. This systematic collation of system-
\par
atic reviews published up to February 2017 was then used to
\par
identify an exemplar sample of individual economic evalua-
\par
tions and this sample was used to identify if, and how, capac-
\par
ity constraints had been included in the published analyses.
\par
The review involved two stages. Stage one involved the sys-
\par
tematic collation of a sample of systematic reviews of eco-
\par
nomic evaluations of precision medicine. Stage two involved
\par
creating a list of relevant individual economic evaluations
\par
of precision medicine.
\par
2.1 Stage One
\par
Table 1 summarises the inclusion criteria used to guide
\par
the relevance of published systematic reviews of economic
\par
evaluations of precision medicine. To be classified as a
\par
systematic review, the published study must have used a
\par
systematic approach to search databases of published litera-
\par
ture with the aim of identifying all studies that addressed a
\par
specified research question.
\par
2.1.1 Search Strategy
\par
The MEDLINE (inception year: 1946) and EMBASE
\par
(inception year: 1980) databases were searched from data-
\par
base inception to February 2017, using an electronic search
\par
strategy, to identify all systematic reviews of economic
\par
evaluations of precision medicine as defined by this review.
\par
The electronic search strategy for this systematic review
\par
was based on a published economic evaluation search fil-
\par
ter developed by the University Of York-based Centre for
\par
Reviews and Dissemination (CRD) [16] and combined with
\par
terms relevant to precision medicine and a systematic review
\par
search filter, which were informed by a published strategy
\par
[5] (see Electronic Supplementary Material (ESM) Appen-
\par
dix 1). In addition, a selection of lead or senior (last) authors
\par
of published systematic reviews of economic evaluations of
\par
precision medicine were contacted by e-mail to determine
\par
whether they knew of other published systematic reviews
\par
not identified in the initial search relevant to the selected
\par
time period.
\par
2.1.2 Selection Process
\par
The abstracts identified in the electronic literature search
\par
were screened for relevance and inclusion in the review
\par
by a team of three reviewers at the Manchester Centre for
\par
Health Economics (SJW, Hunter Moore and Sean Gavan).
\par
Each abstract was screened by two of the three reviewers.
\par
Disagreements on whether a study should be included were
\par
resolved by a fourth researcher (Niall Davison).
\par
2.1.3 Data Extraction and Analysis
\par
The number of systematic reviews of economics evaluations
\par
of precision medicine was recorded and their key details
\par
summarised using a table (ESM Appendix 2). The identified
