\par
2.2.1 Search Strategy
\par
A list of individual economic evaluations of precision medi-
\par
cine were identified from the reference lists of the systematic
\par
reviews identified in stage one. Grey literature studies that
\par
had been identified in the systematic reviews were retained
\par
if they met the inclusion criteria for individual studies. This
\par
involved a manual search facilitated using a database created
\par
in Microsoft \- Excel\textregistered{} 2010 [17].
\par
2.2.2 Selection Process
\par
To determine the relevance of the individual studies and
\par
inclusion of primary economic evaluations of test and treat-
\par
ment strategies, the abstracts of the identified studies were
\par
imported into the bespoke database in \- Excel\textregistered{} and then
\par
double screened by two reviewers (SJW and Martin Eden,
\par
Manchester Centre for Health Economics), with disagree-
\par
ments resolved by a third reviewer (KP).
\par
To identify studies that had discussed capacity issues, a
\par
manual keyword search of the PDF for each included study
\par
was conducted. To identify relevant keywords, a search of
\par
the title and abstracts of relevant theoretical papers was con-
\par
ducted using the Mendeley reference management software
\par
[18--33]. The identified terms were capacity, barrier*, con-
\par
strain*, restrict*, short (for short-run or short-term), imple-
\par
ment*, learn*, inefficien*, bottleneck, scale, utilis*, utiliz*.
\par
2.2.3 Data Extraction and Analysis
\par
The individual studies were initially collated into one of
\par
four categories according to the aim of the parent systematic
\par
review (as outlined in the stage one data extraction). It was
\par
clear, however, that some systematic reviews had identified
\par
some studies as test--treat when they were test-only strate-
\par
gies (and vice versa). Therefore, after review of the titles and
\par
abstracts of the individual studies, they were then reclassi-
\par
fied into one of three categories: (1) economic evaluations
\par
of test and treatment interventions; (2) economic evaluations
\par
of test-only interventions; and (3) other studies that did not
\par
meet the eligibility criteria for this systematic review. Stud-
\par
ies classified into the second and third categories were then
\par
excluded from this review.
\par
The total number of papers identified and their charac-
\par
teristics are summarised in ESM Appendix 3. Data were
\par
extracted by one reviewer (SJW) using a data extraction
\par
