3.3 Inclusion of Capacity Constraints
\par
Of the 222 individual economic evaluations included in
\par
this review, 33 mentioned the potential impact of capac-
\par
ity constraints on the costs and consequences of precision
\par
medicine in a qualitative sense and nine of these 33 studies
\par
went further and attempted to quantify the impact of capac-
\par
ity constraints in the analysis (Table 3). ESM Appendix 4
\par
summarises the 33 included studies. These 33 studies raised
\par
key elements related to capacity constraints and how these
\par
may impact on the cost effectiveness of precision medicine.
\par
These capacity constraints were grouped into four themes:
\par
budget constraints; quality of the testing process; ease of test
\par
use in clinical practice; and the need for economic evidence
\par
to reduce decision uncertainty.
\par
3.3.1 Budget Constraints
\par
A key concern relevant to capacity constraints was the total
\par
impact of precision medicine on healthcare budgets (budget
\par
impact). Budget impact was mentioned in terms of the spe-
\par
cific treatment or associated testing and how this may poten-
\par
tially inhibit the uptake and use of these interventions in
\par
clinical practice. Three studies focused on the budget impact
\par
in broad terms [41--43], but a further ten studies specified
\par
the discussion about the financial impact of testing or treat-
\par
ment [42, 44--52].
\par
Kondo et al. [47] stressed the need for concern regarding
\par
the financial impact of implementing 12,000 new 21-gene
\par
assays for breast cancer per year. The considerably larger
\par
eligible patient populations who could benefit from interven-
\par
tions such as warfarin dosing tests were suggested to pose
\par
more substantial problems in terms of financial impact. Pat-
\par
rick et al. [53] identified that up to 10 million people could
\par
benefit from tests costing between US\$400 and US\$550 per
\par
test.
\par
When evaluating the implementation of the more
\par
advanced fluorescent in situ hybridisation (FISH) testing
\par
for reflex testing human EGFR 2 (HER2) amplifications in
\par
breast cancer, Garrison et al. [45] stated that it was unclear
\par
whether payers would finance the more rigorous test over
\par
the commonly used immunohistochemistry test (IHC).
\par
Ret\`el et al. [54] assessed that there was a 75\% chance that a
\par
70-gene signature test for breast cancer would not be imme-
\par
diately reimbursed by payers, thereby limiting the availabil-
\par
ity of testing to patients.
\par
The introduction of genetic testing for breast cancer was
\par
seen by the Medical Advisory Secretariat of the Ministry
\par
of Health and Long-Term Care, in Ontario, Canada, as an
\par
action that could either release health system resources by
\par
reducing the number of women receiving unnecessary chem-
\par
otherapy, or place an additional burden through additional
\par
test costs [51]. This report highlighted potential economic
\par
incentives that may impact the level of usage of testing,
\par
including pharmaceutical companies' desire to maintain
\par
profits in providing the drug to wider population and pay-
\par
ers' desire to reduce drug expenditure.
\par
The cost of specific treatments was also commonly high-
\par
lighted as a potential barrier to their full implementation. Ito
\par
et al. [46] suggested that the out-of-pocket costs of access-
\par
ing aromatase inhibitors for breast cancer may cause under-
\par
utilisation of the potentially health-improving treatment in
\par
the USA. It was determined that improving coverage of these
\par
drugs by Medicare, a government health insurance scheme,
\par
would improve health outcomes while reducing healthcare
\par
resource use.
\par
Lidgren et al. [49] suggested that individual clinicians
\par
may decide not to prescribe treatments due their high cost.
\par
The authors also suggested that if different healthcare clin-
\par
ics have different financial budgets then provision of trastu-
\par
zumab for breast cancer may be variable and this could lead
\par
to inequitable access to treatment.
\par
