\par
feasible. In a survey conducted as input into the analysis
\par
of an economic evaluation, Barone et al. [59], an Italian
\par
research team, found that 75\% of a sample of oncologists,
\par
pathologists, molecular biologists and surgeons believed a
\par
delay in receiving KRAS mutation testing result for colorec-
\par
tal cancer patients would affect their treatment choice and
\par
that 25\% experienced such delays in actual practice.
\par
3.3.3 Ease of Test Use in Clinical Practice
\par
To effectively introduce a new test into clinical practice,
\par
the relevant health professionals must be aware of the test
\par
and have sufficient training to offer it and interpret and
\par
appropriately use the results in their decision-making to
\par
guide clinical practice. Insufficient human capital can
\par
therefore act as a capacity constraint in moving precision
\par
medicine into practice. When tests and treatments are
\par
1020 S. J. Wright et al.Accounting for Capacity Constraints in Economic Evaluations of Precision Medicine
\par
1021
\par
assessed in trials and economic evaluation the assump-
\par
tion is that they are used in an optimal, or near optimal,
\par
way, and with full adherence to their recommended use
\par
by clinicians. However, clinical practice is complex and
\par
the way in which a test fits into the current care pathway
\par
is not always clear. Nine studies highlighted issues with
\par
the transition of tests from research to clinical settings
\par
[41, 42, 54, 55, 60--64]. For example, Breijer et al. [61]
\par
developed and evaluated two multivariable models to pro-
\par
duce an algorithm to predict the risk of endometrial cancer
\par
based on patients' characteristics. While this analysis sug-
\par
gested a reduction in the cost of diagnosing the cancer,
\par
these authors proposed that due to the minimal expected
\par
value per patient, the algorithm would have to be made
\par
very easy to use by clinicians for it to be implemented in
\par
clinical practice.
\par
The potential reluctance of clinicians to change their
\par
prescribing behaviour was shown in a UK study of thio-
\par
purine-methyl transferase (TMPT) genotyping to inform
\par
the use of azathioprine and the appropriate dosing strategy
\par
[62]. Even though the test could be used to better predict
\par
which patients would experience profound neutropenia as
\par
a reaction from azathioprine, clinicians did not use the
\par
test to change the dosing of azathioprine. The authors
\par
hypothesised that this is because the clinicians chose to
\par
remain conservative with azathioprine prescription due
\par
to the other potential adverse reactions that the medicine
\par
could cause.
\par
Ret\`el et al. [54] predicted that the use of a 70-gene signa-
\par
ture in breast cancer patients would be delayed by ``hesitant
\par
adopters'' who would not use the results of the assay in deci-
\par
sion-making. However, these authors included a scenario
\par
in their analysis that assumed an increase in the ease of use
\par
of the 70-gene signature would subsequently increase the
\par
use of test results in clinical decision-making, resulting in
\par
improved cost effectiveness of the intervention [54].
\par
Two studies suggested that the willingness of clinicians
\par
to use new tests and their ability to effectively use results
\par
in decision-making may evolve over time as a result of a
\par
learning process [63, 65]. Klang et al. [63] found that clini-
\par
cians using the Oncotype DX panel did not register treatment
\par
decisions for the first 55 of 368 patients as the clinicians
\par
were ``learning about the technology and how to interpret
\par
the results''. The authors of a US study investigating the
\par
use of a multi-gene assay in breast cancer explicitly high-
\par
lighted that previous analyses of Oncotype DX were based
\par
on the assumption that the test would be used as dictated
\par
by guidelines [65]. They further stated they believe policy
\par
makers were interested in ``learning how the assay affected
\par
outcomes and costs compared with actual practice and after
\par
some period of experience with the assay''. Both of these
\par
studies indicated that a lower utilisation of testing by health
\par
professionals may impact on the incremental cost effective-
\par
ness of precision medicine in clinical practice.
\par
3.3.4 Decision Uncertainty
\par
When a new precision medicine is introduced, decision
\par
makers, such as individual clinicians, local hospital trusts
\par
or national health technology assessment (HTA) agencies,
\par
must decide under conditions of uncertainty whether to pro-
\par
vide the intervention to patients (implementation). There is
\par
a significant cost associated with making the wrong deci-
\par
sion related to implementation, which could include reduced
\par
patient outcomes at an individual level or societal loss of
\par
health from funding cost-ineffective interventions. While
\par
commonly used methods such as deterministic and proba-
\par
bilistic sensitivity analysis allow for uncertainty in model
\par
parameters to be visualised, a wide range of other uncertain-
\par
ties such as methodological and structural uncertainty may
\par
be present in the evaluation of precision medicine [66, 67].
\par
Decision uncertainty around implementation can therefore
\par
act as a significant capacity constraint to the introduction of
\par
new examples of precision medicine.
\par
As a rationale for conducting economic evaluations, many
\par
studies highlighted that evidence of the cost effectiveness of
\par
an intervention was a requirement for implementation in a
\par
clinical setting [68, 69]. As such, a lack of such evidence on
\par
incremental cost effectiveness could inhibit the use of pre-
\par
cision medicine in clinical practice. In Canada, the Ontario
\par
Medical Advisory Secretariat suggested that evidence on the
\par
net resource implications of HER2 testing for breast cancer
\par
was required before the intervention could be adopted into
\par
``dynamic health systems'' [51].
\par
The published evidence also recognised that economic
\par
evaluations are conducted at a certain stage in the develop-
\par
ment of the intervention to deliver precision medicine and
\par
that the use and cost effectiveness may change in subsequent
\par
clinical practice. Ret\`el et al. [54, 70] highlighted that their
\par
results were based on the assumption of full implementa-
\par
tion and that an evaluation using real-life scenarios was in
\par
progress at the time of publishing this study.
\par
In theory, the availability of economic evidence can help
\par
to fuel increased implementation of precision medicine but,
\par
in addition, increased implementation can itself provide
\par
economic benefits. Rubinstein et al. [71] identified that if
\par
decision makers relied on a passive diffusion of BRCA 1/2
\par
genetic testing into the healthcare system, then potentially
\par
the economies of scale which could have been generated
\par
from a more managed implementation strategy could be
\par
forgone. These authors also suggested that increased imple-
\par
mentation of an alternative intervention magnetic resonance
\par
imaging in breast cancer may create additional costs and
\par
benefits if the imaging becomes a part of the cancer man-
\par
agement pathway alongside BRCA 1/2 genetic testing. This1022 synergistic effect is known as an economy of scope, whereby
\par
the same resource can be used to produce or improve multi-
\par
ple services to provide better outcomes.
\par
3.4 Quantifying the Impact of Capacity Constraints
\par
in Economic Evaluations
\par
Nine (see Table 3) of the identified 222 economic evalu-
\par
ations of precision medicine included in this systematic
\par
review used, or suggested, techniques that explicitly quan-
\par
tified the impact of capacity constraints [44, 50, 54, 57,
\par
72--76]. All of these studies used decision-analytic models
\par
that allowed for sub-perfect implementation of technologies,
\par
limiting their potential benefit to society. In other words,
\par
for various reasons, fewer than 100\% of the eligible patient
\par
population were assumed to receive the intervention deliver-
\par
ing precision medicine or the intervention was not given in
\par
the optimal way, resulting in higher costs or lower benefits
\par
than were potentially achievable. In some cases this less than
\par
perfect implementation was due to capacity issues such as
\par
budget constraints [54], regulation barriers [54] and long test
\par
turnaround times [57]. In two other instances, the imperfect
\par
implementation was associated with low uptake of the test
\par
or treatment [72, 73]. While adherence to medications may
\par
appear to be a demand-side problem, it is in fact a complex
\par
issue and ``the attributes of the health-care system and ser-
\par
vice delivery may also influence adherence'' [73]. For exam-
\par
ple, if clinicians have limited time to spend with patients,
\par
the opportunity to provide effective information about the
\par
benefits of adherence and approaches to coping with adverse
\par
effects may be limited. The methods used to account for
\par
capacity constraints can be categorised as static or dynamic
\par
and are described in Sects. 3.4.1 and 3.4.2.
\par
3.4.1 Static Methods to Account for Capacity Constraints
\par
Static methods refer to methods used to produce a single
\par
cost-effectiveness estimate which takes account of imperfect
\par
implementation for one cohort of patients. This estimate of
\par
the incremental cost effectiveness may differ from the esti-
\par
mated incremental costs and consequences for a perfectly
\par
implemented precision medicine. For example, Delea et al.
\par
[74, 75] accounted for drug wastage in their model-based
\par
economic evaluation of lapatinib and letrozole for women
\par
with HER2-positive breast cancer. This analysis identified
\par
that reducing wastage of trastuzumab from 15 to 0\% resulted
\par
in lapatinib and letrozole being cost effective at \pounds 22,895 per
\par
QALY gained when compared with trastuzumab, which had
\par
been a cost-saving intervention in the base-case analysis.
\par
Romanus et al. [57] accounted for specific capacity con-
\par
straints to multiplexed biomarker testing for non-small cell
\par
lung cancer in a sensitivity analysis. When turnaround time
\par
increased by a factor of 1.5, the most cost-effective approach
\par
S. J. Wright et al.
\par
changed from ``test and treat'' to ``empiric therapy'', in which
\par
patients began treatment with a general chemotherapy agent
\par
while waiting for test results. Reducing the proportion of
\par
patients being tested from 100\% to 5.7\% did not impact the
\par
rank ordering of the incremental cost effectiveness of the
\par
interventions. Likewise, in a study by Vanderlaan and col-
\par
leagues [72] assumptions about the differences in the uptake
\par
of a 21-gene assay for breast cancer did not impact on the
\par
incremental cost effectiveness of the intervention. In two
\par
further studies, assumptions about the cost of the test or
\par
re-biopsy did not affect the incremental cost effectiveness
\par
of a genotype assay for drug selection in patients with HIV
\par
[50] or EML4 (echinoderm microtubule associated protein-
\par
like 4)--ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) testing for
\par
patients with non-small cell lung cancer [44].
\par
McCowan et al. [73] found that adherence to tamoxifen
\par
in women with breast cancer significantly impacted the
\par
incremental cost effectiveness. On average, patients with an
\par
adherence of less than 80\% to the treatment were expected to
\par
experience 1.12 fewer QALYs than those patients assumed
\par
to have over 80\% adherence. Furthermore, such patients
\par
were expected to experience significantly higher medical
\par
costs (\pounds 5970, 95\% confidence interval 4644--7372).
\par
3.4.2 Dynamic Methods to Account for Capacity
\par
Constraints
\par
Dynamic methods refer to methods that account for capacity
\par
constraints that allow the impact of barriers or constraints
\par
to change over time and/or in multiple patient cohorts. As a
\par
result, the cost effectiveness of the technology also poten-
\par
tially changes over time. The most comprehensive investi-
\par
gation of capacity constraints and impact on implementa-
\par
tion in an economic evaluation using dynamic methods was
\par
conducted by Ret\`el and colleagues [54] who investigated
\par
the future potential uptake of a 70-gene signature test in
\par
breast cancer. This study used an analytical approach called
\par
scenario drafting to identify potential barriers and facilita-
\par
tors to the implementation of the technology. These authors
\par
constructed different sets of model parameters to reflect the
\par
implications of these scenarios, accounting for their per-
\par
ceived likelihood of occurring in reality. These scenarios
\par
comprised potential capacity issues such as a lack of reim-
\par
bursement of testing, regulation issues, uncertainty in the
\par
clinical utility of the test, and a lack of use by clinicians
\par
due to the difficulty interpreting the tests. The authors also
\par
investigated the potential impact of using the test at different
\par
stages of the clinical pathway.
\par
The incremental cost effectiveness of the 70-gene sig-
\par
nature test was evaluated at different timepoints and imple-
\par
mentation levels for three key scenarios: reducing technical
\par
failure rates of the test over time; decreasing non-compli-
\par
ance with discordant test results; and increasing financialAccounting for Capacity Constraints in Economic Evaluations of Precision Medicine
\par
1023
\par
reimbursement and clinicians' uptake of testing. The cost
\par
and consequences achieved by testing an additional patient
\par
was allowed to vary. As each scenario involved the inter-
\par
vention diffusing into clinical practice at different rates, the
\par
costs, health outcomes and cost effectiveness of the inter-
\par
vention differed by year and scenario. A key finding of the
\par
study was that the intervention would only be cost effec-
\par
tive if use of the test results by clinicians improved over
\par
time. At the initial time point, 2005, the intervention was
\par
always cost ineffective with an incremental cost-effective-
\par
ness ratio (ICER) of \texteuro 1.9 million. If uptake did not improve,
\par
then the ICER only improved to \texteuro 1.5 million in the best-
\par
case scenario. In the scenario where other factors remained
\par
equal and uptake improve from 3\% in 2005 to 50\% in 2010
\par
and 92\% in 2010, the ICER reduced from \texteuro 1.9 million to
\par
\texteuro 26,145 and \texteuro 11,123, respectively. Failing to overcome
\par
the capacity constraints of low financial reimbursement of
\par
the test and a lack of uptake by clinicians would therefore
\par
mean a potentially cost-effective intervention should not be
\par
provided as it would result in a societal loss of health to
\par
the population. Removal of the capacity constraints makes
\par
the intervention cost effective, but the cost of such capac-
\par
ity investments would also need to be accounted for in an
\par
economic evaluation.
\par
4 Discussion
\par
Precision medicine is often reported to offer a cost-effective
\par
approach for the management of a selection of different dis-
\par
eases by using a stratification mechanism to identify which
\par
patients may accrue more benefits in terms of response or
\par
avoidance of adverse events. Some economic evidence sup-
\par
porting this premise is provided by the growing number of
\par
economic evaluations conducted in the past 10 years. This
\par
review identified 45 systematic reviews of economic evalu-
\par
ations of precision medicine, which summarised 367 unique
\par
individual economic evaluations. Between 2007 and 2015,
\par
some 222 economic evaluations were conducted to identify
\par
the costs and consequences of test and treat interventions.
\par
The first necessary condition for the adoption of an exam-
\par
ple of precision medicine is demonstration of whether it is
\par
a cost-effective use of healthcare resources using appropri-
\par
ate methods of economic evaluation to identify incremental
\par
costs and consequences. However, capacity constraints in a
\par
healthcare system have the potential to impact on the esti-
\par
mated incremental cost effectiveness of examples of preci-
\par
sion medicine in practice. Only 33 studies of a sample of
\par
222 identified economic evaluations qualitatively discussed
\par
the potential impact of capacity constraints for the introduc-
\par
tion of examples of precision medicine. The core capacity
\par
constraints outlined in these papers included budget con-
\par
straints; quality of tests and testing processes; ease of test
\par
use in clinical practice; and decision uncertainty. Even when
\par
interventions appeared cost effective, it was suggested they
\par
can pose significant financial burdens on payers who have
\par
to rapidly provide access to testing services and potentially
\par
expensive treatments.
\par
Despite the potential for capacity constraints to affect the
\par
incremental cost effectiveness of precision medicine, only
\par
nine (4\%) economic evaluations from the sample of 222
\par
sought to quantify the effects of limited capacity. All of the
\par
nine studies that quantified the effects of limited capacity
\par
were based on decision-analytic models rather than trials,
\par
and a wide range of model types including decision trees,
\par
Markov models and discrete event simulation were used.
\par
The presence of these commonly used models in this review
\par
suggests that it would be possible to incorporate capacity
\par
constraints in many economic evaluations.
\par
While the way in which capacity constraints are included
\par
in models will depend on the model type, the methods used
\par
could be categorised as either a static or dynamic approach.
\par
The static methods included real-world cost-effectiveness
\par
analysis and sensitivity analysis in decision-analytic mod-
\par
els. Static approaches to incorporating capacity constraints
\par
only give a representation of how the constraints impact the
\par
cost effectiveness of the intervention for a single cohort of
\par
patients at a single point in time. Dynamic approaches to
\par
quantifying capacity constraints allow for the fact that health
\par
system capacity can change over time and this can have a
\par
changing impact on the cost effectiveness of the interven-
\par
tion. The example of Ret\`el et al. [54] focused on how the
\par
level of uptake impacts on the cost effectiveness of the inter-
\par
vention. However, the method used could also be applied to
\par
capacity constraints. If the ICER is non-linear and depends
\par
on the level of implementation, then any factor that impedes
\par
implementation can in theory render the intervention cost
\par
ineffective. For example, if numerous repeat EGFR muta-
\par
tion tests are required due to insufficient samples at the start
\par
of implementation, this will raise the cost of testing and
\par
reduce patient benefits due to delays in receiving treatment.
\par
With greater communication regarding biopsy requirements
\par
and learning by clinicians, better samples could be obtained,
\par
reducing costs and improving benefits. Therefore, the ICER
\par
will be dependent on the extent to which the intervention
\par
is being effectively implemented and the impact of testing
\par
knowledge as a capacity constraint. This study shows the
\par
key interaction between barriers that impede the use of an
\par
intervention and the potential for marginal costs and benefits
\par
to vary depending on the level of implementation. The result
\par
is situations where capacity constraints and other barriers to
\par
implementation cause the intervention to become cost inef-
\par
fective. Implementing the precision medicine at this level
\par
will result in healthcare resources being diverted away from
\par
other areas where they could be put to better use, lowering
\par
the overall health of patients in the healthcare system.1024 The Ret\`el et al. [54] study also raised an important con-
\par
sideration in that actions can be taken to overcome capacity
\par
constraints and to improve the incremental cost effectiveness
\par
of precision medicine. For example, testing guidelines could
\par
be introduced or education programmes used to improve the
\par
quality of biopsy samples for testing. Such an intervention
\par
itself would have a cost but would also provide benefits in
\par
improving the benefits for patients and making the interven-
\par
tion more cost effective. The evaluation of such strategies
\par
to improve the implementation of interventions is known
\par
as value of implementation analysis [27]. This systematic
\par
review did not identify any value of implementation analy-
\par
ses investigating the implementation of precision medicine.
\par
The use of dynamic, multi-cohort decision-analytic mod-
\par
els and the value of an implementation approach may pro-
\par
vide better evidence than static methods to decision mak-
\par
ers in allowing them to understand how best to implement
\par
new examples of precision medicine in a cost-effective way
\par
by investing in improving health system capacity. In order
\par
to forecast the potential capacity constraints before the
\par
approval of a precision medicine by an HTA body, the use of
\par
qualitative methods such as interviews or focus groups may
\par
prove useful. For example, Ret\`el et al. [54] used the Delphi
\par
method with a group of clinicians to identify potential bar-
\par
riers to implementing the MammaPrint test.
\par
4.1 Limitations
\par
The economic evaluation of precision medicine is an expand-
\par
ing research area, and due to the size of the literature base,
\par
some restrictions to the inclusion of papers in this study
\par
were required. The use of a search strategy that identified
\par
previous systematic reviews reduced the number of papers
\par
for abstract screening. Collating these studies provided a
\par
comprehensive set of primary studies and theoretically all
\par
papers previously published in this area. However, due to the
\par
length of time required to conduct a systematic review, it is
\par
possible that economic evaluations of precision medicine
\par
have been published in the time since the literature searches
\par
of the most recent systematic reviews were conducted. This
\par
means there may be a gap in the studies identified between
\par
2015 and 2017. This could cause bias in the results of this
\par
study if methods to include capacity constraints in economic
\par
evaluations have been recently developed and applied. The
\par
authors of this review are not aware of any recently pub-
\par
lished novel methods for accounting for capacity constraints
\par
in economic evaluations of precision medicine.
\par
The focus on studies published in the last 10 years and
\par
evaluations that focused on test and treatment strategies
\par
could also feasibly have excluded studies that discussed
\par
capacity. While studies published before 2007 may have
\par
discussed capacity due to the novelty of precision medicine,
\par
it is unlikely that early economic evaluations of precision
\par
S. J. Wright et al.
\par
treatments would have incorporated complex methodologi-
\par
cal adjustments for capacity. Capacity could be a significant
\par
issue in the provision of precision treatments which only
\par
provide diagnostic information. Such studies were excluded
\par
from this review.
\par
This review has taken a broad definition of a capacity
\par
constraint as any factor which impedes the full benefits of an
\par
intervention from being realised. While this includes factors
\par
such as finite budgets and the quality of the testing process,
\par
it also includes more abstract concepts such as low usage of
\par
tests due to a lack of knowledge of the technology amongst
\par
clinicians. Some of the included studies also investigated
\par
adherence to medicine and uptake of treatment. While low
\par
adherence could be due to capacity constraints in a lack of
\par
patient education about the benefits of treatment, it may also
\par
be due to underlying patient preferences for the treatment or
\par
adverse effect profile. Therefore, while full implementation
\par
of a precision medicine will rely on a lack of capacity con-
\par
straints on the supply side, it could also be impeded by low
\par
demand for the treatment by patients. This could be the case
\par
if a new treatment had a greater risk of more adverse effects,
\par
more severe adverse effects or a different range of adverse
\par
effects. There may, therefore, be a limit to the level of imple-
\par
mentation that can be achieved by investing in capacity.
\par
While this review has focused on the impact of capacity
\par
constraints for the economic evaluation of precision medi-
\par
cine, such constraints in the healthcare system may have a
\par
significant impact on the cost effectiveness of interventions
\par
in other medical areas. For example, Jahn et al. [28] explored
\par
the cost effectiveness of drug-eluting stents in the presence
\par
of capacity constraints using a discrete event simulation
\par
model. Capacity constraints may also be particularly sig-
\par
nificant for the cost effectiveness of organ transplants where
\par
there is a limited availability of donors [77]. In addition,
\par
capacity constraints and their impact may be larger in coun-
\par
tries with developing healthcare systems [78].
\par
5 Conclusions
\par
The results of this systematic review suggest that a wide
\par
variety of capacity constraints could have implications for
\par
the cost effectiveness of precision medicine in clinical prac-
\par
tice, but the majority of economic evaluations of precision
\par
medicine do not account for such constraints. In the studies
\par
that did account for limited health system capacity, a variety
\par
of methods had been used, with most relying on static com-
\par
parisons of the cost effectiveness of examples of precision
\par
medicine for a single cohort with or without health system
\par
capacity constraints.
\par
Studies should account for changing health system capac-
\par
ity, which may have implications for the cost effectiveness
\par
of interventions across multiple cohorts in different years.Accounting for Capacity Constraints in Economic Evaluations of Precision Medicine
\par
1025
\par
This is because when combined with varying marginal costs
\par
and benefits, implementation-limiting capacity constraints
\par
can result in interventions that are cost effective at the pop-
\par
ulation level becoming inefficient in the short-run. Health
\par
economists should endeavour to forecast potential barriers
\par
to implementing precision medicine and to evaluate potential
\par
strategies to invest in capacity. A number of studies have
\par
reported qualitative investigations of such barriers [11--13]
\par
and combining these approaches with dynamic methods for
\par
quantifying such barriers may help to provide decision mak-
\par
ers with more robust evidence as to how to cost-effectively
\par
implement such interventions and take resource and capacity
\par
constraints into account.
