\par
\textbf{The problem of evaluating automated large-scale}
\par
\textbf{evidence aggregators}
\par
\textbf{Nicolas W\"uthrich1}
\par
{$\cdot$} \textbf{Katie Steele2}
\par
Received: 7 November 2016 / Accepted: 13 November 2017 / Published online: 28 November 2017
\par
\copyright{} The Author(s) 2017. This article is an open access publication
\par
\textbf{Abstract }In the biomedical context, policy makers face a large amount of potentially
\par
discordant evidence from different sources. This prompts the question of how this
\par
evidence should be aggregated in the interests of best-informed policy recommenda-
\par
tions. The starting point of our discussion is Hunter and Williams' recent work on an
\par
automated aggregation method for medical evidence. Our negative claim is that it is far
\par
from clear what the relevant criteria for evaluating an evidence aggregator of this sort
\par
are. What is the appropriate balance between explicitly coded algorithms and implicit
\par
reasoning involved, for instance, in the packaging of input evidence? In short: What
\par
is the optimal degree of `automation'? On the positive side: We propose the ability
\par
to perform an adequate robustness analysis (which depends on the nature of the input
\par
variables and parameters of the aggregator) as the focal criterion, primarily because
\par
it directs efforts to what is most important, namely, the structure of the algorithm and
\par
the appropriate extent of automation. Moreover, where there are resource constraints
\par
on the aggregation process, one must also consider what balance between volume of
\par
evidence and accuracy in the treatment of individual evidence best facilitates infer-
\par
ence. There is no prerogative to aggregate the total evidence available if this would in
\par
fact reduce overall accuracy.
\par
B Nicolas W\"uthrich
\par
N.Wuethrich@lse.ac.uk
\par
Katie Steele
\par
katie.steele@anu.edu.au
\par
1 Department for Philosophy, Logic, and Scientific Method, London School of Economics and
\par
Political Science, Houghton Street, London WC2A 2AE, UK
\par
2 School of Philosophy, Australian National University, HC Coombs Building, Canberra 0200,
\par
Australia
\par
1233084 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
\textbf{Keywords }Evidence aggregation{$\cdot$} Evidence-based medicine{$\cdot$} Statistical meta-
\par
analysis{$\cdot$} Robustness analysis
\par
\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
It is now a commonplace that policy-making should be evidence-based (Cartwright and
\par
Hardie 2012; Montuschi 2009): for example, class size regulations, housing policies,
\par
and dosage recommendations regarding new substances, all should be designed, as far
\par
as practicable, in light of the best available evidence. Policy makers demand evidence
\par
for many reasons, including assessing the potential impact of planned interventions
\par
and providing greater accountability to relevant stakeholders. However, in these social
\par
and biomedical contexts, policy makers are facing an enormous amount of evidence
\par
(Stegenga 2011, p. 497; Krimsky 2005, p. 129; Weed 2005, p. 1545). This prompts
\par
the question of how these large sets of diverse and potentially conflicting evidence
\par
should, if at all, be aggregated to facilitate best-informed policy recommendations.
\par
In this paper, we focus on this problem in the medical context. Evidence aggregation
\par
in medicine can involve a host of data from different sources, such as observational
\par
studies, randomized controlled trials, meta-analyses, and expert judgment, which often
\par
confirm conflicting hypotheses. Throughout this paper we refer to aggregation pro-
\par
cedures involving evidential input of this sort, i.e., high volume, highly diverse and
\par
potentially conflicting evidence, as `large-scale' evidence aggregation.
\par
In a series of recent papers, Hunter and Williams (2010, 2012, 2013) have developed
\par
a comprehensive decision support tool for medical policy makers. Their framework
\par
motivates our discussion here, because it is ambitious and striking and thus brings
\par
to light a number of general questions. First, their approach is large-scale and highly
\par
computational---it involves an explicit algorithm, such that, once initiated, relatively
\par
little user input is required. (Throughout the paper, we refer to their approach as `highly
\par
automated'.) Second, they provide a full account of the steps to choosing a (medical
\par
or treatment) policy. That is, their framework or algorithm involves both an epistemic
\par
component (assessing hypotheses concerning the various causal effects of the relevant
\par
treatments) and a preference-driven component (weighing the treatments in light of
\par
these hypotheses). This paper focuses on the epistemic component, which is in line
\par
with what many consider to be the problem of `evidence aggregation', but we will also
\par
briefly address how this component fits into the bigger treatment-selection picture. As
\par
regards the epistemology alone, Hunter and Williams' proposal invites reflection about
\par
relatively under-appreciated issues: Does it make sense to deliver a highly automated
\par
large-scale evidence aggregator? How do we assess the optimal extent of automation
\par
for any given type of evidence aggregation task?
\par
Let us be clear what we take automation to consist in and what the alternative
\par
to automation is. We propose that evidence aggregators are automated to the extent
\par
that they explicitly encode procedures or algorithms that are fixed, transparent, and
\par
dependent on inputs characterised in a particular way. (Ideally these procedures would
\par
also be encoded in such a way that they can be run on a computer at high speed, and
\par
also so that values for key parameters can be easily changed. Here we will assume that
\par
`explicit algorithms' are implemented wisely so that they allow for these benefits. In
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3085
\par
this sense, automation implies computability in the discussion that follows.) The non-
\par
automated aspects of aggregation involve implicit reasoning that affords flexibility,
\par
in the sense that how inputs are interpreted and weighed may be determined `on
\par
the fly'. It is effectively expert judgment that may or may not be capable of explicit
\par
articulation. So, the main contrast is between reasoning that is made explicit or public,
\par
and reasoning that is `behind the scenes', a `black box' aspect of the procedure, so
\par
to speak. The more the packaging of the input evidence relies on `behind the scenes'
\par
reasoning, for instance, the less automated the aggregator.
\par
We defend two claims. First, a negative claim: We argue that the above questions
\par
are non-trivial. It is very difficult to specify criteria for determining the appropri-
\par
ate extent of automation for a large-scale evidence aggregator, such as Hunter and
\par
Williams', even when it comes to assessing a single, well-specified causal hypothe-
\par
sis. This problem deserves more philosophical attention. Second, on a more positive
\par
note: Despite this lack of clarity, we defend \emph{one }criterion for assessing the reliability
\par
of large-scale evidence aggregators: the capacity, given the nature of the input vari-
\par
ables and parameters of the aggregator, to perform an \emph{adequate }robustness analysis. In
\par
effect, this criterion directs attention to the question that matters: Does the aggregation
\par
function have appropriate parameters and associated input variables (even if there is
\par
uncertainty about the precise parameter values)? We go on to argue that resource con-
\par
straints complicate the assessment of what algorithm structure enhances reliability or
\par
sound inference. In this case volume of evidence competes with accuracy in processing
\par
individual evidence. There is no prerogative to process the total evidence available via
\par
a relatively crude algorithm, if this would in fact reduce overall accuracy.
\par
The paper is structured as follows. First, we sketch the general problem of evidence-
\par
based comparison of policy (or treatment) options, our focus being the epistemic or
\par
evidential component. The goal here is to see how far standard logic(s) of inference
\par
constrain or guide the task of constructing a large-scale evidence aggregator (Sect. 2).
\par
While formal logic does assist in framing the aggregation problem, it does not settle the
\par
substantial questions of interest to us concerning the optimal extent of automation. This
\par
should be even more apparent from the description of Hunter and Williams' approach
\par
(Sect. 3). So we need to develop further criteria for assessing a large-scale evidence
\par
aggregator. We go on to introduce the ability to perform an adequate robustness analysis
\par
as at least one such evaluation criterion, and we compare the case of unlimited resources
\par
(e.g. computation time) to the case of a constrained resource budget (Sect. 4). Finally,
\par
we defend our criterion against some objections (Sect. 5).
\par
\textbf{2 Evidence-based decisions: separating the key components}
\par
Hunter and Williams aim to provide answers to what is ultimately of interest in prac-
\par
tical medical contexts: their decision support tool outputs claims like `treatment 1 is
\par
medically better than treatment 2 for patients of type X'. While such claims appear to
\par
concern only medical `facts', they are ultimately claims about preferences---whether
\par
treatment 1 is \emph{preferred}, on all-things-considered medical grounds, to treatment 2.1
\par
1 Of course, non-medical considerations (prominently, monetary cost) may also play a role in advice
\par
relating to the comparison of medical treatments, but we leave such considerations aside.
\par
1233086 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
\textbf{Table 1 }A simplified decision
\par
problem between treatments 1
\par
and 2 in light of the two criteria
\par
`pain relief' and `blood pressure'
\par
Pain relief Blood pressure
\par
Treatment 1 Pain 1 Blood 1
\par
Treatment 2 Pain 2 Blood 2
\par
Thus these sorts of claims are \emph{deceptively }simple; the first thing to appreciate is that
\par
they involve both a factual and an evaluative element.
\par
An example helps to illustrate what is at stake: Consider a doctor deciding what
\par
treatment to recommend a patient. Assume there are just two treatments. (Note that, for
\par
the most part, we will assume, for simplicity, that there are just two options to choose
\par
between.) We will further simplify the problem by assuming that the doctor has a
\par
good idea already of the characteristics of the patient. That is, we are not considering
\par
the problem of diagnosis, which is a tricky problem in its own right. Assume that
\par
in the case at hand both the impact on pain relief and the impact on blood pressure
\par
matter. Representing the situation in terms of a `multi-criteria' decision problem, as
\par
per Table 1, helps to illuminate the dimensions of the problem.
\par
Recall that we ultimately want to assess the claim: `treatment 1 is more choice-
\par
worthy (i.e., medically better, with respect to pain relief and blood pressure), relative to
\par
treatment 2, for patients of type X'. Table 1 makes explicit that this involves comparing
\par
the treatments with respect to both pain relief and blood pressure, and then ultimately
\par
ranking the treatments, depending on their performance in these respects, and the
\par
relative importance of the two criteria. So the decision problem at hand involves both
\par
facts (the causal effects of the treatment options with respect to pain and blood pressure)
\par
and values (the relative importance of differences in pain relief and blood pressure).
\par
Appreciation of the basic decision model is the first step in analysing Hunter and
\par
Williams' proposal, which we outline in Sect. 3. It allows us to distinguish the various
\par
components of their decision support tool. The aim in the remainder of this section
\par
is to consider how much further standard logic can take us in analysing Hunter and
\par
Williams' (and other comparable) proposal(s). Our particular interest is the \emph{evidence}
\par
\emph{aggregation problem }(in s2.1--s2.2 below); we mention the literature on multi-criteria
\par
decision-making only briefly at the end of the section (in s2.3). One might hope
\par
that standard logic of inference is the key to assessing a highly automated evidence
\par
aggregator such as devised by Hunter and Williams. We suggest, however, that one
\par
can construe the logic of evidence aggregation in a variety of ways; in any case, the
\par
bare logic does not in itself settle the more substantial questions concerning optimal
\par
design. These questions are taken up in later sections.
\par
\textbf{2.1 The evidence aggregation problem}
\par
Recall that, for simplicity, we restrict our attention to the comparison of just two treat-
\par
ments: In such a case, the key empirical questions are whether (and, ideally, by how
\par
much) one treatment is better than the other on each respective medical dimension
\par
(e.g. pain relief). That is, we want to know the sign and ideally also the magnitude
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3087
\par
of the difference in the relevant \emph{effect size(s) }for the two treatments.2 This question
\par
can be translated into a set of hypotheses: either very fine-grained hypotheses con-
\par
cerning the precise difference in effect size between the two treatments, or else more
\par
coarse-grained hypotheses concerning simply whether or not treatment 1 is better than
\par
treatment 2 along the dimension in question.
\par
The problem of evidence aggregation is about what the total evidence at hand says
\par
about any given target partition of hypotheses. For the Bayesian, there is a straight-
\par
forward, albeit abstract, answer to this question: The \emph{impact }of the evidence on the
\par
relative credibility (measured in terms of probabilities) of two hypotheses depends on
\par
the relative \emph{likelihoods }for the hypotheses, or in other words, the relative probability of
\par
the total evidence conditional on the respective hypotheses. That is, evidential impact
\par
depends on the \emph{likelihood ratio}, stated here in terms of the probability function \emph{Pr },
\par
for two hypotheses \emph{H }and \emph{H }{$\prime$} given total evidence amounting to the conjunction of
\par
evidence propositions \emph{E}1, . . . , \emph{En }:
\par
Pr(\emph{E}1, \emph{E}2, . . . , \emph{En }\textbar\emph{H })
\par
Pr(\emph{E}1, \emph{E}2, . . . , \emph{En }\textbar\emph{H }{$\prime$})
\par
In the case that \emph{E}1, \emph{E}2,..., \emph{En }are conditionally independent with respect to \emph{H }, then
\par
the likelihood for the total evidence is just the product of their separate likelihoods:
\par
Pr (\emph{E}1, \emph{E}2, . . . , \emph{En }\textbar\emph{H })= Pr (\emph{E}1\textbar\emph{H }) \texttimes{} Pr (\emph{E}2\textbar\emph{H }) \texttimes{} ... \texttimes{} Pr (\emph{En }\textbar\emph{H }). In the case of
\par
dependencies between the evidence propositions, however, the likelihoods cannot be
\par
thus decomposed.
\par
Bayesian logic does not itself dictate the content of the evidence propositions,
\par
denoted here \emph{E}1, . . . , \emph{En }. But constraints assist with tractability. Many applications
\par
(including those of interest here) are suited to the following `witness model' constraint:
\par
The individual `pieces of evidence', \emph{E}1, . . . , \emph{En }, take the form of \emph{witness reports}, that
\par
is, direct reports on the truth of the hypotheses of interest (whether a probability of
\par
truth or a binary assessment of truth). The witness reports are furthermore taken to
\par
be independent.3 The likelihood ratio associated with any such report is effectively
\par
a measure of the reliability of the witness; it is the ratio of the probability that the
\par
witness reports that the hypothesis is true given that it is in fact true to the probability
\par
that the witness reports that the hypothesis is true given that it is false. The challenge
\par
of applying the witness schema in any given case is to identify what are the separate
\par
pieces of evidence that can count as independent witness reports. These reports must
\par
be subsequently assessed for reliability.
\par
The Bayesian witness model has much in common with models for aggregating
\par
individual opinions to arrive at a collective opinion (see List 2012 for an overview of
\par
the aggregation of a set of logically connected binary opinions and Genest and Zidek
\par
1986 for an overview of the aggregation of probabilistic opinions). Both kinds of
\par
2 The pain relief effect size, for instance, might be characterised in terms of the proportion of subjects who
\par
report significant decrease in pain after treatment.
\par
3 The independence requirement of these models is generally only approximately respected in practice.
\par
Salient witness reports are simply treated as independent, whether or not there are doubts about this. We
\par
tend to go along with this stance in what follows. While assuming independence without warrant may
\par
compromise one's inferences/judgments, this is not our focus in this paper.
\par
1233088 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
model involve assessments of the \emph{reliability }of the individual `witness' opinions, and
\par
they assume that (or are more tractable to the extent that) the individual opinions are
\par
independent of each other. The collective aggregation approach is more straightforward
\par
in that the final assessment of the hypotheses is a direct aggregate of the actual witness
\par
reports.4 Indeed the latter model is perhaps the best way to interpret Hunter and
\par
Williams' proposal in the next section. Either way, we will see below (and also in the
\par
next section) that the bare logic of a `witness model' does not greatly constrain the
\par
evidence aggregation task.
\par
\textbf{2.2 The literature on evidence aggregation vis-\`a-vis the schema}
\par
The existing literature on aggregating evidence for causal hypotheses accords well with
\par
the witness schema. One might interpret the key questions as follows: How should
\par
individual `witness reports' be delineated to (at least approximately) preserve \emph{inde-}
\par
\emph{pendence }and how should the reliability of these reports be assessed? What algorithm
\par
should be employed for aggregating the reported causal conclusions? Consider an
\par
example report amounting to a statistical reject/accept result concerning some causal
\par
claim. This is effectively a witness report that has some degree of reliability. Indeed,
\par
there is a preoccupation in the literature (in particular, the `hierarchy of evidence' lit-
\par
erature associated with the `evidence-based medicine/policy' movement; see Brendan
\par
et al. 2014 for an overview) with how to assess the reliability of study results given the
\par
design in question (the sample size, the controls for bias, etc.), and furthermore with
\par
how to aggregate the results, for instance, whether supposedly more reliable study
\par
types should `trump' others or whether they should all play a role in determining the
\par
final causal conclusions.5
\par
Most evidence hierarchies place \emph{statistical meta-analyses }near the top.6 One might
\par
worry that the witness model does not account for this kind of conglomerate evidence,
\par
where the effect size measures from different studies (perhaps the difference in efficacy
\par
of two treatments) are combined in sophisticated ways. By way of response: There
\par
are two ways one might reconcile statistical meta-analysis with the witness schema.
\par
The first is to see meta-analysis as an instance of the general schema, with a particular
\par
choice of algorithm for aggregating the witness reports, and limited to a certain kind
