\par
assessing a causal hypothesis.8
\par
In general, the above discussion underscores a point made above, namely that the
\par
witness schema, while offering some useful constraints, is nonetheless a very flexible
\par
model for evidence aggregation. Clearly a lot depends on how the evidence is divided
\par
into independent witnesses and assessed for reliability. That is, it is apparent that a
\par
lot of the difficult questions in negotiating evidence are shifted to the `pre-processing'
\par
of the evidence, and away from the final aggregation task. So, the general dictates of
\par
logic---here spelled out in terms of a witness model---can provide a basic framework
\par
for evidence aggregation, but further substantial aspects of the process are inevitably
\par
left unspecified. That is not to say that these further details of identifying, weighing and
\par
aggregating evidence cannot in large part be automated via an explicit algorithm. How
\par
much automation is a good idea is of course the question that remains unanswered.
\par
\textbf{2.3 Return to the wider multi-criteria decision problem}
\par
As noted earlier, in order to finally make a choice of treatment, one not only has to tackle
\par
the above evidence aggregation problems for the relevant empirical hypotheses, but
\par
also the \emph{problem of combining, according to decision theoretic principles, the various}
\par
\emph{empirical and evaluative claims that are pertinent to the final choice of option}. This is a
\par
hugely contested issue and falls under the literature of `multi-criteria decision analysis'
\par
(for an overview see Keeney and Raiffa 1993). There are a host of suggested methods
\par
for making a choice in the face of options that are ranked differently according to
\par
different criteria which themselves have differing importance for the decision-maker.
\par
An important specification of these methods is whether the ranking of options for
\par
each dimension can be represented in cardinal terms or rather only in ordinal terms:
\par
i.e., can one specify how much better/worse treatment 1 is on pain relief compared
\par
to treatment 2 for patient type X or can one only say which is better/worse? When
\par
cardinal information is plausibly available and the criteria are comparable in cardinal
\par
terms, the decision analysis can follow standard Bayesian principles in the form of
\par
