\textbf{3 A striking proposal: Hunter and Williams' highly automated}
\par
\textbf{aggregator}
\par
Hunter and Williams claim to offer ``a new framework for representing and synthe-
\par
sizing knowledge from clinical trials involving multiple outcome indicators.'' (Hunter
\par
and Williams 2012, p. 1) We summarize what we take to be the key tenets of their
\par
account. The upshot is that their procedure illustrates the main claim made in the
\par
previous section: to determine the optimal extent of automation, further criteria are
\par
needed over and above the information provided by referencing standard logic(s) of
\par
inference.9 The goal of Hunter and Williams' approach is to come up with an ordinal
\par
ranking of two treatment options for a specific patient (2013, p. 16).10 For example: Is
\par
the treatment of contraceptive pill or no treatment better, when we look at pregnancy,
\par
ovarian cancer, and breast cancer as outcome indicators for patient \emph{Y }? (ibid., p. 13).11
\par
Their approach contains two aggregation elements:
\par
1. The aggregation of evidence for each outcome indicator, which itself involves (a)
\par
delineating evidence, (b) assessing reliability of evidence, and (c) an aggregation
\par
rule.
\par
2. The overall multi-criteria problem, taking into account all relevant outcome indi-
\par
cators.
\par
Given the focus of our discussion, we explain the first of these two aggregation steps
\par
in more detail. We briefly sketch their approach to the second step for completeness.
\par
To aggregate the available evidence regarding the various outcome indicators,
\par
Hunter and Williams introduce an evidence table. This table delineates evidence in
\par
terms of different studies. For each study, a number of key input variables are filled in
\par
or evaluated (ibid., pp. 6--7):
\par
(a) the pair of treatments (e.g. contraceptive pill, no treatment),
\par
(b) the outcome indicator denoting the dimension along which the treatments are
\par
compared (e.g. breast cancer),
\par
(c) the value of the outcome indicator given a particular measure. If one adopts the
\par
relative risk measure, then the ratio between the portion of people displaying the
\par
outcome given treatment 1 (e.g. breast cancer given contraceptive pill) and the
\par
portion of people displaying the outcome given treatment 2 (e.g. breast cancer
\par
given no treatment) is calculated,
\par
