\par
whether the outcome indicator is desirable for a patient class. For example, given
\par
a value of 1.04 (assuming the above specified measure of relative risk and the
\par
outcome indicator `breast cancer'), contraceptive pill is inferior to no treatment
\par
(ibid., p. 13),
\par
(e) whether the measured value of the outcome indicator is statistically significant,
\par
and
\par
(f) the evidence type (e.g. RCT study, cohort study, meta-analysis, or network anal-
\par
ysis).12
\par
These input variables effectively inform what the evidence says about the treatments
\par
and how reliable it is. The information in the evidence table is effectively the total
\par
available input for the automated aggregation procedure.
\par
Now, the different studies in the evidence table are treated as independent witnesses.
\par
For each witness, its reliability is determined via so-called \emph{meta-arguments}. Hunter
\par
and Williams consider the following meta-arguments (for identifying unreliable evi-
\par
dence): `the evidence contains flawed RCTs', `the evidence contains results that are not
\par
statistically significant', `the evidence is from trials that are for a very narrow patient
\par
class', `the evidence has outcomes that are not consistent'. These meta-arguments
\par
return binary `in or out' results (meaning that a study either feeds into the aggregation
\par
function or it does not) for computational ease and theoretical simplicity (Hunter and
\par
Williams 2012, pp. 4, 9, 20).
\par
With these elements in place, we can discuss Hunter and Williams' aggregation rule
\par
for any given outcome indicator. It is based on the notion of an \emph{inductive argument},
\par
where this is a pair (\emph{X},{$\epsilon$})\emph{i }, with \emph{X }being a subset of evidence and {$\epsilon$} the claim that either
\par
\emph{T}1 is superior to \emph{T}2, \emph{T}1 is inferior to \emph{T}2, or \emph{T}1 is equal to \emph{T}2 (ibid. pp. 3--4, 11) with
\par
respect to outcome indicator \emph{i}.
\par
13 The set of inductive arguments is constructed in the
\par
following way. To start, the available evidence (concerning \emph{T}1 and \emph{T}2) is divided into
\par
three subsets SUPERIOR, INFERIOR, and EQUIVALENT. The subset SUPERIOR
\par
contains all rows of the evidence table (i.e. evidence pieces) for which \emph{T}1 was shown
\par
to be superior to \emph{T}2. The subsets INFERIOR and EQUITABLE are defined via the
\par
inferiority and equivalence relations, respectively (ibid., pp. 10--11). The inductive
\par
arguments are determined by permissible inference rules from the set of evidence.
\par
Hunter and Williams propose the following three simple inference rules that apply to
\par
cases where evidence is not conflicting (assuming that \emph{X }is a subset of evidence, ibid.
\par
11):
\par
1. If \emph{X }{$\subseteq$} SUPERIOR, then \emph{T}1 {$>$} \emph{T}2
\par
2. If \emph{X }{$\subseteq$} INFERIOR, then \emph{T}1 {$<$} \emph{T}2
\par
3. If \emph{X }{$\subseteq$} EQUITABLE, then \emph{T}1 {$\sim$} \emph{T}2
