\par
argument with the broadest set of evidence for the respective superiority, inferiority,
\par
or equitability claim for an outcome indicator.
\par
With the notion of an inductive argument in place, we can specify the aggrega-
\par
tion rule for a single outcome indicator. Consider the example of two studies with
\par
contradictory claims about the effects of contraceptive pills. Let us assume that two
\par
inductive arguments can be generated for the outcome indicator `ovarian cancer':
\par
(\textbraceleft\emph{e}1\textbraceright, \emph{T}1 {$<$} \emph{T}2)\emph{k }and (\textbraceleft\emph{e}2\textbraceright, \emph{T}1 {$>$} \emph{T}2)\emph{k }, where \emph{T}1: contraceptive pill and \emph{T}2: no treat-
\par
ment. Hunter and Williams propose two options to aggregate this information.14 First,
\par
they suggest performing a statistical meta-analysis which aggregates the effect sizes
\par
of the two studies. If the effect sizes are aggregated into one, then one can read off
\par
the binary superiority relation. This amounts to generating a new inductive argument
\par
(while deleting the two other ones) with the evidence basis \textbraceleft\emph{e}3\textbraceright{} being the meta-
\par
analysis: (\textbraceleft\emph{e}3\textbraceright, \emph{T}1 {$<$} \emph{T}2)\emph{k }. In this way, the conflicting evidence has been removed
\par
from the evidence table. Second, the reliability criterion could be contradicting one
\par
(or both) of the inductive arguments and thereby resolve the conflict between the two
\par
arguments. This strategy appears a bit hopeful. It seems that Hunter and Williams
\par
assume that all (or certainly the largest part) of the studies will give the same order-
\par
ing for the treatments for any particular indicator. Note that they do not propose an
\par
alternative aggregation rule which is salient: a majority (or supermajority) rule could
\par
be used in assessing the relevant majority proportions between numbers of studies
\par
indicating that \emph{T}1 is superior (inferior) to \emph{T}2.
\par
So far, we have described the first aggregation step. Now, we briefly turn to their
\par
\emph{treatment of the overall multi-criteria problem}: What is the best treatment option
\par
taking into account all relevant outcome indicators (e.g. ovarian cancer, breast cancer,
\par
and pregnancy)? Hunter and Williams suggest a pairwise comparison of the inductive
\par
arguments involving different outcome indicators (e.g. the comparison of two inductive
\par
arguments; one stating that \emph{T}1 is superior to \emph{T}2 with respect to ovarian cancer, the other
\par
stating that \emph{T}2 is superior to \emph{T}1 with respect to pregnancy) (Hunter and Williams 2012,
\par
p. 15). This pairwise comparison is based on the importance of the involved outcome
\par
indicators as well as the risk of developing the outcome indicators (ibid.). This process
\par
effectively identifies the winning inductive argument, which states as its conclusion
\par
an ordering of the treatments (ibid., p. 19).
\par
Let us briefly take stock. Hunter and Williams propose a decision support tool
\par
that has the components outlined in Sect. 2. In particular, the evidence aggregation
\par
component can be seen to accord with a witness model. So the right building blocks
\par
are present in Hunter and Williams' aggregator, but clearly there are many more
\par
design choices one might query. Indeed, Hunter and Williams' evidence aggregator
