\par
\textbf{4 A new criterion for determining the optimal degree of automation:}
\par
\textbf{capacity for robustness analysis}
\par
Like other approaches to evidence aggregation in the literature, Hunter and Williams'
\par
proposal can be seen to accord with a witness model, but this in itself does not go
\par
far in terms of settling the quality of the inferences. We now consider in more detail
\par
whether their procedure is justified or fit for purpose. We address this issue not solely
\par
for Hunter and Williams' proposal but with respect to automated evidence aggre-
\par
gation procedures more generally: What is the appropriate extent of automation for
\par
an evidence aggregator to help facilitate policy recommendations in, say, a medical
\par
context?
\par
The large question that is left open by the `witness schema' (i.e., that goes beyond
\par
the bare inferential framework), is how to delineate the independent `witnesses', and
\par
assess the nature and reliability of `their' findings. Reliability, for instance, is a matter
\par
of \emph{both }the quality and relevance of the experiment or `witness' given the hypotheses at
\par
hand. One must determine what features of individual pieces of evidence should inform
\par
this complex reliability assessment and how exactly reliability should be determined
\par
on the basis of these features. Assessing the reliability of the witnesses is one of the key
\par
things that is automated in automated evidence aggregators. This can be automated to
\par
varying extent. Recall that Hunter and Williams automate the reliability assessment
\par
via meta-arguments.
\par
In Sect. 4.1 below we consider this question first from an ideal perspective, free
\par
from any constraints on computational resources. In effect, we consider better and
\par
worse treatments of (or inferences based on) a fixed amount of evidence (the more the
\par
better). Here we introduce our key idea of ability to perform an adequate robustness
\par
analysis. Then in Sect. 4.2 we go on to consider the more realistic scenario where there
\par
may be constraints on computational resources. We depict this as a \emph{further }problem of
\par
a trade-off between nuanced analysis of evidence and volume of evidence. We argue
\par
that it may \emph{not }be the case that aggregators that can handle more evidence are better.
\par
In fact, that very consideration is pivotal in assessing and comparing aggregators. We
\par
will argue that there is no prerogative to aggregate the total evidence available if this
\par
would in fact reduce overall accuracy.
\par
15 Admittedly, Hunter and Williams leave the question open as to whether aspects of their algorithm, in
\par
particular, the reliability assessments, should be more detailed.
\par
1233094 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
\textbf{4.1 Assessing aggregators in an ideal setting: no computational constraints}
\par
Absent any computational constraints, the goodness of an automated evidence aggre-
\par
gator is all about ideal performance---the idea is to make the best or wisest inferences
\par
possible for a class of cases (i.e., the type of evidence aggregation problem at hand),
\par
given all the available evidence. So we want the reasoning process to be as nuanced as
\par
possible, where the more of this nuance that can be captured by an explicit algorithm
\par
and so automated, the better. A higher degree of automation is desirable, all else being
\par
equal, because it allows transparency, removes computational error, enhances speed,
\par
and facilitates analysis of the sensitivity of results to choices of parameter values, i.e.,
\par
robustness analysis. In the ideal setting then, any part of the reasoning process that can
\par
be made explicit in advance of seeing the particular evidence at hand, should indeed
\par
be made explicit. The only reason to leave some aspects of the reasoning process as
\par
a black box is if there are aspects of the procedure where it is less advantageous to
\par
try to specify the reasoning in advance; better to leave it to experts (assuming they
\par
are experts of average competence relative to the appropriate group) to interpret and
\par
weigh the particular evidence when it arises.
\par
It is one thing to state the goal of automating all reasoning that can be made explicit
\par
without sacrifice in nuanced analysis, but of course it is another thing to make these
\par
extremely difficult judgments. We cannot hope to provide detailed advice on how to
\par
make such judgments in this paper. For one thing, much of the detail will depend on
\par
the type of policy task at hand and the kind of evidence available. Instead, what we
\par
seek are \emph{strategies }that a practitioner may employ to approach or frame the question in
\par
a way that may assist, in a modest way, in arriving at an answer. To put it differently:
\par
What general criterion provides the best avenue for assessing optimal automation?
\par
The main strategy or criterion we propose for assessing the degree of automation of
\par
an evidence aggregator can be stated as follows: Does the automated aggregator allow
\par
conducting a robustness analysis that would yield a thorough and compelling survey
\par
of the possibility space? Of course, robustness analysis has already been mentioned
\par
above as a useful by-product of automation. We initially elaborate this point (4.1.1).
\par
Our novel proposal, however, is that this consequence of automation should serve
\par
as a focal point in the algorithm design (4.1.2). This is not just a matter of wise
\par
implementation; as mentioned, we are already assuming the implementation of the
\par
algorithm in question allows parameter values to be changed by the user. It is rather
\par
a matter of settling on the explicit algorithm structure with an eye to whether the
\par
subsequent robustness analysis will serve as a reasonable survey of the possibility
\par
space for the type of aggregation problem at hand. In short, focussing on the ability
\par
to conduct an adequate robustness analysis serves to direct one's priorities to what
\par
really matters---away from the precise `dial settings' of an aggregator, so to speak,
\par
and towards whether we have the right dials to begin with.
\par
Before proceeding, let us first clarify a couple of terms concerning the structure of
\par
an automated evidence aggregator that will be central to our discussion in this section.
\par
First, there are \emph{input variables }describing the evidence. Recall, for instance, Hunter and
\par
Williams' evidence table: the columns are the input variables accounting for relevant
\par
features of the evidence, and each row---an individual piece of evidence---is effectively
\par
a vector of values for these input variables.
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3095
\par
Second, there are the \emph{parameters }of the aggregation function that dictate how the
\par
values of the input variables bear on the assessment of each piece of evidence and
\par
ultimately on the overall aggregation or final inference concerning the hypotheses in
\par
question. For instance, the aggregation function might include a parameter `threshold
\par
sample size' which is used to measure the quality of a piece of evidence with respect to
\par
sample size. These parameters are associated with a range of possible \emph{values}. Thanks
\par
to programming design, the parameters can be set to any value within this range/set,
\par
depending on initial user input.
\par
\emph{4.1.1 Robustness analysis in its traditional role: as a useful upshot of automation}
\par
As a general characterisation, robustness analysis involves determining the stability
\par
of a result given changes in underlying assumptions. Two forms of robustness analy-
\par
sis are widely distinguished in the literature: \emph{Derivational (or inferential) robustness}
\par
\emph{analysis }looks at the stability of model derivations (or inferences) given changes in
\par
the model (or background) assumptions (Kuorikoski et al. 2010, p. 542).16 \emph{Measure-}
\par
\emph{ment robustness analysis }looks at the stability of empirical results given changes in
\par
empirical modes of determination (such as different types of experiments) (Wimsatt
\par
1981, p. 128).
\par
Here, we focus on derivational robustness analysis (DR) since it is a form of error
\par
analysis, i.e. a way of exploring the sensitivity of results to choices of parameter values.
\par
This role of DR is also called \emph{heuristic function }as it allows a transparent and traceable
\par
way of dealing with unavoidable idiosyncratic choices in the construction of a model
\par
(here, evidence aggregators), due to uncertainty and/or reasonable disagreement. For
\par
DR allows determining the relative importance of various components of a model with
\par
respect to the output variable of interest (Kuorikoski et al. 2010, p. 543).
\par
Automated evidence aggregators, given our assumption about wise implementation,
\par
are well set up for this kind of error analysis. This point has been brought up by Hunter
\par
and Williams in relation to the meta-arguments in their aggregator. They note that their
\par
procedure allows ``a form of sensitivity analysis'' (Hunter and Williams 2012, p. 25) by
\par
including different meta-arguments. By including different meta-arguments, in effect,
\par
the reliability of the evidence can be assessed in a range of ways, and the impact of
\par
these differing assessments on the resulting inferences can be monitored.
\par
In other contexts too, sensitivity analysis is recommended as a way to keep track and
\par
explore the implications of model choices that are subject to uncertainty/reasonable
\par
disagreement. For instance, Stegenga (2011, p. 498) points out that there are many
\par
choices of this kind in statistical meta-analysis:
\par
Meta-analysis fails to constrain intersubjective assessments of hypotheses
\par
because numerous decisions must be made when performing a meta-analysis
\par
which allow wide latitude for subjective idiosyncrasies to influence the results
\par
of a meta-analysis. (ibid.)
\par
16 This is also frequently called sensitivity analysis (Raerinne 2013, pp. 287--288).
\par
1233096 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
\emph{4.1.2 Robustness analysis in its new role: as a central design criterion}
\par
For all the good of robustness analysis, one might regard it a secondary issue when
\par
it comes to assessing the degree of automation of an evidence aggregator. Surely
\par
the primary issue is whether the aggregator facilitates roughly the best inferences
\par
possible given the available evidence; error analysis is a matter of extra detail. As
\par
suggested above, robustness is indeed typically considered an ex post analysis or a
\par
way to check what confidence one should have in a model result. Here we defend,
\par
however, a more central role for robustness analysis in the construction and assessment
\par
of an evidence aggregator. In short, the \emph{prospect }of what robustness analysis can be
\par
performed focuses one's attention on what really matters---the functional form and
\par
possible inputs to the evidence aggregator, rather than the precise parameter values
\par
featuring in the aggregator. In other words, the prospect of robustness analysis helps
\par
one to assess what parts of the inference process can be made explicit and transpar-
\par
ent.
\par
There are at least two reasons why focussing on the capacity for robust-
\par
ness analysis is helpful in making these judgments about algorithm design: (a)
\par
it allows one to recognise that certain types of uncertainty/error (regarding pre-
\par
cise parameter values) \emph{do not }compromise automation, since the impact of these
\par
uncertainties can be explored via the robustness analysis, and (b) it allows one
\par
to recognise that other types of uncertainty/error \emph{do }in fact compromise automa-
\par
tion; cases where there is not only low confidence in the `best-guess' estimates,
\par
but in fact there is low confidence in the entire possibility space that would be
\par
afforded by robustness analysis. In this case, it is not clear whether the remedy
\par
is more or less automation, but robustness analysis can guide the deliberation pro-
\par
cess.
\par
Let us clarify the latter deliberation process by appeal to an example. It helps to
\par
imagine the incremental development of an aggregator. The starting structure might be
\par
a very basic one, where the pieces of evidence are described in terms of two input vari-
\par
ables, say, `type of study', with possible values `randomised controlled trial (RCT)'
\par
and `observational study', and also `statistical significance', where possible values
\par
are simply `yes' and `no'. The logic of the reliability assessments might be along the
\par
following lines: Only studies that are statistically significant have positive reliability
\par
(such that they are included in the aggregation), and amongst those, the RCTs are
\par
given more weight according to a parameter \emph{beta}, specifically, RCTs are given \emph{beta}
\par
times the reliability weighting of observational studies. (Note that Hunter and Williams
\par
introduce a crude reliability judgment of this sort by considering meta-arguments that
\par
include/exclude evidence pieces based on whether results are statistically significant
\par
or not.) Now one might reflect on the prospective robustness analysis afforded by this
\par
aggregator. The possibility space will include inferences based on a range of values
\par
for \emph{beta}. But this might be regarded too limited and misleading a set of possibili-
\par
ties.
\par
For the above example aggregator, the possibility space afforded by robustness
\par
analysis might be deemed more adequate if the algorithm for making reliability judg-
\par
ments were either more or less detailed. A relatively straightforward innovation is to
\par
convert judgments that are currently implicit but need not be to explicit aspects of
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3097
\par
the algorithm. For instance, with regard to our example, the judgments of statistical
\par
significance could be spelled out more explicitly; this would involve substituting the
\par
\emph{p value }of the study as the input variable, and then deriving whether the study is sta-
\par
tistically significant according to the parameter \emph{alpha}, such that if the \emph{p value }is less
\par
than \emph{alpha }the study is deemed statistically significant. The corresponding robustness
\par
analysis would then produce a possibility space that includes a range of values for
\par
\emph{alpha}, which would presumably be more thorough.17
\par
So much for the low hanging fruit. The more difficult judgments concern aspects
\par
of the reasoning process where it is not clear whether more or less detail in the explicit
\par
algorithm would be better. Adding detail to the explicit algorithm is a good thing pro-
\par
vided this is tracking genuine nuance of reasoning; the alternative scenario, however,
\par
is when extra detail in the explicit algorithm systematically distorts the reasoning
\par
process, making it more rigid in a way that is not rectified by robustness analysis.
\par
Returning to our example, a key reason why the initial robustness analysis might be
\par
deemed inadequate is that the reliability weightings depend purely on `study type'
\par
(amongst those that are statistically significant), and yet it might be thought that this
\par
is \emph{not }the most pertinent grouping as far as quality of evidence is concerned. One
\par
possibility is to add further dimensions to this grouping: perhaps `sample size' and a
\par
measure of the `relevance of experimental subject', i.e., the closeness of the experi-
\par
mental group to the patient class at hand, could also be included as input variables,
\par
and treated in the reliability function with reference to appropriate parameters. In this
\par
case, the robustness analysis would effectively survey the possibilities associated with
\par
changing the relative weights of these more fine-grained study groupings, which would
\par
potentially be a more adequate representation of the real space of possibilities.18 On
\par
the other hand, it might be thought that this extra detail in the explicit algorithm would
\par
only make matters worse; that the corresponding robustness analysis would yield a
\par
possibility space that is even more misleading and would not include reasonable infer-
\par
ences. Perhaps the reliability assessments should be shifted entirely to implicit `on
\par
the fly' expert reasoning rather than explicitly coded. In this case, the evidence input
\par
variable would simply be `reliability of study'. Of course, this means one could not
\par
explore the possibility space so readily, but one might at least be happier with the `best
\par
guess' estimate.
\par
This example of course raises a lot of further substantial questions. But we hope
\par
the central point is clear: that it is far from obvious what aspects of an evidence
\par
aggregation process are best made explicit; focussing on the prospects for robustness
\par
analysis provides some help, however modest, in settling this question.
\par
17 We are not hereby endorsing statistical significance as important for determining whether a study
\par
result ought to be included in evidence aggregation. There are reasons to worry about this interpretation
\par
of statistical results. Our claim is simply that \emph{if }this property were to play such a role, better to make the
\par
reasoning as explicit as possible, and enable the exploration of changes in the chosen level of significance.
\par
18 For example, we can define for the input variable `sample size' the parameter, \emph{gamma}, such that the
\par
reliability assessment of a study correlates not linearly to the sample size but increases in a step-wise fashion.
\par
For the input variable `relevance of experimental subject', a parameter \emph{delta }could be introduced in relation
\par
to the similarity measure between the experimental and patient group.
\par
1233098 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
\textbf{4.2 Handling computational constraints: when is greater volume of evidence}
\par
\textbf{better?}
\par
Now we turn to the scenario where there are constraints on (or costs associated with)
\par
computational and other resources. In practice, this is always the case, and no doubt
\par
Hunter and Williams have resource constraints in mind. To start, the on-going main-
\par
tenance of the database of evidential inputs demands a lot of person hours---for
\par
identifying and recording the relevant features of each study or piece of evidence.
\par
(Moreover, it may be that only some characteristics of a study are available in the first
\par
place.) Then when it comes to running the aggregator, once the database is in hand,
\par
the algorithms for selecting, assessing the reliability and aggregating relevant pieces
\par
of evidence to arrive at a conclusion about the hypotheses in question all require pro-
\par
cessing time. In addition, there may be costs associated with the end-user interpreting
\par
the methodology/ inferential output of the aggregator (an important aspect of trans-
\par
parency). In short, evidence aggregation typically involves a variety of costs; most
\par
importantly with respect to person hours and computer processing time.
\par
One might suppose that the assessment of evidence aggregators when resources are
\par
costly is a very messy business. It seems that it calls for some trade-off between infer-
\par
ential accuracy and efficiency. And epistemology alone cannot answer the question:
\par
To what extent should we `cut corners' in assessing the total body of evidence so as
\par
to arrive at conclusions more quickly or with less expenditure of other resources?
\par
We want to stress here, however, that this is not quite the right way to think about
\par
assessing the performance of an evidence aggregator that is subject to resource con-
\par
straints. There is no getting around the difficult trade-off between reducing resource
\par
costs and increasing inferential accuracy.19 But one must be careful in thinking about
\par
how to spend any given resource budget to best achieve accuracy. The above subtly
\par
presupposes that all the available evidence \emph{mus}t be taken into account. Of course, it
\par
is natural to think that all the evidence should be considered and a way to meet the
\par
resource constraints should be found in the way the evidence is analysed, the reason
\par
being one of the core tenets of evidential logic, i.e., the view that inference should be
\par
based on \emph{all available evidence}.
\par
20 But that tenet refers to an ideal setting where all
\par
the available evidence is taken into account in a fully appropriate and nuanced way.
\par
There is no similar logical demand to attend to all available evidence in a practical
\par
setting where, due to resource constraints, this evidence cannot all be assessed in full
\par
detail. That would indeed be an odd requirement on an evidence aggregator---that
\par
just because some apparently relevant evidence has been tabled, it must influence the
\par
inference at hand, even if in a necessarily crude fashion.21
\par
19 Ideally such a tradeoff would be modeled as a `value of information' decision problem and thus settled
\par
in a justified way.
\par
20 This is referred to as the \emph{Principle of Total Evidence }(Carnap 1947).
\par
21 To clarify: Our target here is the mistaken idea that all the tabled evidence must be taken into account
\par
\emph{in a similarly detailed way }(however much detail that may involve) and have \emph{similar potential for influence}
\par
\emph{on the inference at hand}. Of course, there is another sense in which all the tabled evidence must be taken
\par
into account---even if only a subset of the tabled evidence is examined in detail, there must be a way of
\par
selecting this subset that involves searching through \emph{all }the tabled evidence. But in this case the tabled
\par
evidence would not all be treated similarly.
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3099
\par
Our claim is the following: The assessment of the optimal degree of automation for
\par
an evidence aggregator under circumstances of resource constraints (once the budget
\par
has been settled) is not so different from the assessment of an aggregator that is free
\par
from resource constraints. In both cases it is performance, i.e., quality of inference
\par
regarding the hypotheses at hand, that matters, and this is best assessed by focussing
\par
on the capacity for robustness analysis. In the context of resource constraints, the
\par
`principle of total evidence' may be better honoured by processing a subset of evidence
\par
in more detail rather than a greater amount of evidence in lesser detail. That is the
\par
underlying balancing act, in any case: Are the available resources spent in the best way
\par
possible? Should the resource budget(s) be spent on greater nuance in the description
\par
and analysis of evidence, or rather on a greater volume of evidence?
\par
We do not deny that this adds yet a further dimension to the question of what is the
\par
optimal extent of automation for an aggregator. In the ideal case, it was simply a matter
\par
of what aspects of the reasoning process could sensibly be made explicit---a difficult
\par
enough judgment in itself. Resource constraints introduce a new complication: it may
\par
be that less-than-ideal treatment of evidence enables more volume of evidence to be
\par
processed, in the interests of accuracy. This is a further balancing act, but one that may
\par
also be informed by the capacity of an evidence aggregator for robustness analysis.
\par
Let us simply note an extreme scenario that may shed light on the more difficult non-
\par
extreme cases. The worst case, so to speak, is an evidence aggregator for which we
\par
are not confident that \emph{any }of the input evidence contributes to higher quality inference
\par
about the hypotheses at hand, regardless of the precise values of key parameters. Here
\par
it is not even the case that, conditional on using the aggregator at hand, inferences
\par
based on more evidence (requiring more resources) are better. In the more ordinary
\par
and difficult cases, by contrast, all the aggregators under assessment will be ones
\par
for which more evidence (requiring more resources) permits better inferences using
\par
\emph{that }aggregator. (Here the possibility space associated with each aggregator, owing to
\par
robustness analysis, is at least deemed adequate.) The further question in this case is
\par
which aggregator allows for the best quality inferences given the resource budget at
\par
hand, where some aggregators process less evidence with greater nuance while others
\par
process more evidence with lesser nuance.
\par
\textbf{5 Potential objections to our criterion}
\par
We consider here potential criticisms of our approach to determining the optimal extent
\par
of automation in cases without and with resource constraints. On the one side of the
\par
spectrum, it might be objected that we rely too much on intuitive reasoning or expert
\par
judgement, when it would be better to appeal to objective assessments of the track
\par
record of evidence aggregators. On the opposite side, it might be objected that there
\par
is no plausible alternative to implicit expert reasoning when it comes to aggregating
\par
diverse evidence.
\par
The main thing to be said in response to the first position is that it is not obvious
\par
what an objective assessment of the track record of an aggregator consists in. A clear
\par
criterion for an aggregator having produced a verified result would be an instance in
\par
which the aggregator predicts an event and the event in fact takes place. However, the
\par
1233100 Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102
\par
kinds of hypotheses considered in this paper (e.g. `treatment 1 is better than treatment 2
\par
with respect to breast cancer rates') are generally not of this nature. Medical hypotheses
\par
tend to be stochastic (and moreover, associated predictions often depend on a number
\par
of auxiliary conditions); thus there is no definitive point in the future at which evidence
\par
is received that settles such matters.
\par
The second position might go like this: The automation of evidence amalgamation
\par
is doomed to fail because it is inherently resistant to explicit algorithms. To start, one
\par
might argue that judgment is required on a very basic level, namely for the application
\par
of any (reliability) criterion to a particular experimental finding. Even if the reliability
\par
criterion is expressed in a functional form in terms of input variables and parameters,
\par
this objection goes, the value assigned to the input variables in any concrete case
\par
requires expert judgement. Stegenga (2014, p. 203) cites empirical studies showing
\par
that applying the \emph{same }quality assessment tools (QATs) to the \emph{same }medical finding
\par
leads to widely diverging quality assessment of this medical evidence, presumably
\par
because the finding itself is perceived differently by different investigators. By way
\par
of response, we accept the importance of expert judgment in the process of evidence
\par
aggregation. Yet we want to stress that there is no reason to deny that at least some
\par
of the reasoning involved in evidence aggregation/inference can be broken down and
\par
made explicit via an algorithm. Indeed, our question in this paper concerns the optimal
\par
\emph{extent }of automation.
\par
One might otherwise argue that evidence amalgamation is resistant to explicit algo-
\par
rithms since every case of amalgamation is distinct, and, hence, an algorithm, which is
\par
necessarily more general, misses the distinct features of the particular case. We do not
\par
find this objection convincing. Surely, there is a sufficient degree of similarity between
\par
classes of evidence aggregation problems that allow formulating explicit aggregation
\par
rules that are broadly applicable. The QATs mentioned by Stegenga are a case in
\par
point.
\par
\textbf{6 Concluding remarks}
\par
This paper addressed the question of what is the optimal degree of automation for
\par
a given type of evidence aggregation problem. We looked at this question in the
\par
context of medical policy-making which involves large and rather diverse sets of evi-
\par
dence. We argued that general inferential logic, such as the witness schema, helps
\par
to frame the evidence aggregation task but does not in itself settle the further sub-
\par
stantial question about the optimal degree of automation. We illustrated this point
\par
with the help of our case study---Hunter and Williams' highly automated aggrega-
\par
tor.
\par
We hope that our discussion has highlighted the difficulties in answering this ques-
\par
tion. Designing a large-scale evidence aggregator is non-trivial, to say the least, and
\par
this paper leaves a host of important questions unanswered. Of course, much will
\par
depend on the details of the type of policy task at hand; our modest aim in this paper
\par
was to provide a general strategy/evaluation criterion that could provide a focal point
\par
for making the requisite assessments. We have proposed the ability to perform an
\par
adequate robustness analysis (which depends on the nature of the input variables and
\par
123Synthese (2019) 196:3083--3102 3101
\par
parameters of the aggregator) as the focal criterion, primarily because it directs efforts
\par
to what is most important, namely, the structure of the algorithm and thus the appro-
\par
priate extent of automation. Moreover, where there are resource constraints on the
\par
aggregation process, one must also consider what balance between volume of evi-
\par
dence and accuracy in the treatment of individual evidence best facilitates inference.
\par
Again, concentrating on robustness analysis helps here, but there are further trade-offs
\par
between nuanced analysis of evidence and volume of evidence that must be taken into
\par
account. There is no prerogative to aggregate the total evidence available if this would
\par
in fact reduce overall accuracy. This trade-off between nuanced analysis of evidence
\par
and volume of evidence is in our view a promising line of further research.
\par
