\par
The field of fire science evolved over approximately 100 years from early descriptive studies
\par
(e.g., Plummer 1912) to the development of complex models of fire spread and other physical
\par
processes (e.g., Linn et al. 2007). Research has followed two streams: basic research to enhance
\par
understanding of wildfire as an Earth system and ecological process, and applied research to in-
\par
form fire management decision making. Contemporary fire management organizations follow
\par
the four pillars of emergency management: prevention and mitigation, planning and prepared-
\par
ness, response, and recovery. Thus, to inform preparedness and response actions, wildfire man-
\par
agers would like to know, at a daily to weekly scale, how many fires will likely occur, whether
\par
and how fast they will spread,how intense and how large they will grow,and how long they will
\par
last. To inform prevention and mitigation activities, they would also like to know the long-term
\par
likelihood of a vegetated area burning. Because of the close connection between weather and
\par
