2.3. Changes in Fire Occurrence
\par
Whether and where fire occurrence is changing with climate is of considerable interest to fire
\par
managers.Forexample,fireoccurrencehasbeenshowntobeincreasingunderawarmingclimate
\par
in the western United States (e.g.,Westerling et al.2006).Observed increases in fire occurrence
\par
have been found to be associated with anomalies in fire weather indices (Woolford et al. 2014).
\par
The fire season as measured by fire occurrence probability has been getting longer (Woolford
\par
et al. 2010, Albert-Green et al. 2013), and, based on results from studies using global climate
\par
model data under various scenarios, fire occurrence probability is predicted to increase under a
\par
warming climate,(e.g.,Krawchuk et al.2009a,b; Wotton et al.2003,2010).
\par
As commented on by Taylor et al. (2013), difficulties with historical analyses (e.g., Woolford
\par
et al.2010,Albert-Green et al.2013) arise because fire detection system effectiveness can change
\par
overtime,leadingtopotentialconfoundingwithanyclimatechangeeffect.Woolfordetal.(2010)
\par
foundthatthemediansizeatdetectionforlightning-causedfireshadbeendecreasingoverthe42
\par
years of their study, suggesting that the detection system may have become more effective over
\par
time,under the assumption that a fire would continue to grow after ignition.
\par
Woolford et al. (2010) noticed three dominant characteristics in the lightning-caused fire
\par
occurrence records they analyzed, namely, regular seasonal patterns (as commonly quantified
\par
in other fire occurrence work such as Martell et al. 1989; Brillinger et al. 2003; Preisler
\par
et al. 2004; Woolford et al. 2009, 2011); large deviations from these patterns, including zero-
\par
heavy behavior where no fires were observed even though fires were typically observed around
\par
such a period; and extreme behavior where many more fires were observed than what is
\par
typical.
\par
The mixture framework of Woolford et al. (2014) identified significant increases to the
\par
lightning-caused fire occurrence probability that were associated with temperature and fire-
\par
weather index anomalies. Their study monitored long-term trends in a set of historical fire
\par
records for the period 1963--2009 for a region of northwestern Ontario, Canada, using a three-
\par
component mixture of logistic GAMs with the component densities representing seasonal,zero-
\par
heavy, and extreme behavior as discussed above. They noted that potential confounders, such as
\par
improved wildland fire detection systems, make it difficult to tease out climate change trends
\par
and that longer than a half-century of historical records is required to have strong confidence
\par
in correctly concluding significance in such a study monitoring temporal changes to fire occur-
\par
rence. They also noted that determining power to detect trends in fire occurrence probability
\par
as a function of the number of years in the historical records was a ``key, yet commonly over-
\par
looked,pointinmanyquantitativescientificinvestigationsoftrendsthatmayberelatedtoclimate
\par
change'' (Woolford et al. 2014, p. 407). This power evaluation offers an opportunity to consider
\par
how many years of records are required to detect changes in environmental effects with high
\par
confidence.
\par
Regardless of the underlying model and study, goodness-of-fit checking is a key step in any
\par
model-building framework. Typically, the goodness-of-fit logistic-based models can be assessed
\par
by comparing observed counts versus those expected under the model where such counts are
\par
aggregated on various scales.Ideally,cross-validation (Wood 2006,James et al.2013) is also used
