\par
It is challenging to incorporate estimates of uncertainty in fire management strategies,in part
\par
because it is a highly dynamic and multiscalar decision environment. Although advances have
\par
been made in developing stochastic models of characteristics such as fire occurrence, medium-
\par
term fire spread, and burn probability, few studies have connected hazard measures, including
\par
uncertainty, with damage functions and impacts (e.g., Preisler et al. 2011). Implementation of
\par
new models within a fire management decision environment presents special challenges at the
\par
interface between data analytics and human factors (that are not unique to the fire community).
\par
