journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/jhydrol
\par
Research papers
\par
Real-time reservoir operation using recurrent neural networks and in ow
\par
forecast from a distributed hydrological model
\par
Shuyu Yanga, Dawen Yanga,⁎, Jinsong Chenb, Baoxu Zhaoa
\par
a State Key Laboratory of Hydroscience and Engineering, Department of Hydraulic Engineering, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China
\par
b Earth and Environmental Sciences Area, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA, USA
\par
T
\par
A R T I C L E I N F O
\par
A B S T R A C T
\par
This manuscript was handled by G. Syme,
\par
Editor-in-Chief, with the assistance of Shuo
\par
Wang, Associate Editor
\par
Keywords:
\par
Recurrent neural networks
\par
Real-time reservoir operation
\par
In ow forecast by distributed hydrological model
\par
The upper Chao Phraya River basin
\par
Large-scale reservoirs play an essential role in water resources management for agriculture irrigation, water
\par
supply and ood controls. However, we need robust reservoir operation systems under both normal ow and
\par
extreme ow conditions. In this study, we applied recurrent neural networks (RNN) to simulate the operation of
\par
three multi-purpose reservoirs located in the upper Chao Phraya River basin. Two reservoirs have the function of
\par
multiannual ow regulation and one has the function of incomplete annual regulation. The goal of this study is
\par
to explore the applicability of RNN models for operation of reservoirs with multiannual ow regulation under
\par
di erent ow regimes, especially under extreme oods and droughts. We used three RNNs, namely nonlinear
\par
autoregressive models with exogenous input (NARX), long short-term memory (LSTM) and genetic algorithm
\par
based NAXR (GA-NAXR) for reservoir operation based on historical data. For real-time water resources man-
\par
agement, an accurate in ow forecast is required to provide a real-time reservoir out ow, and thus we also
\par
carried out a real-time reservoir operation using the RNN and the in ow forecast by a distributed hydrological
\par
model. Results show that (1) GA-NARX has the highest accuracy among three RNNs and is more stable than the
\par
original NARX by optimizing the initial conditions, although it takes longer training time than NARX and LSTM;
\par
(2) GA-NARX-based operation model is e ective under extreme oods and droughts; and (3) the real-time op-
\par
eration system combining the GA-NARX and the distributed hydrological model has reasonable accuracy in both
\par
wet season and dry season. RNN-based operation model developed in this study has potential applicability in
\par
practical water management, and the model combining the hydrological prediction is specially useful for real-
\par
time reservoir operation.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
As an important infrastructure for water resources management and
\par
utilization, reservoirs provide us many bene ts, such as controlling
\par
ood, mitigating drought, irrigating agriculture, generating hydro-
\par
power and supplying water resources (Chang and Chang, 2006; Lehner
\par
et al., 2011; Zhang et al., 2018a). Real-time reservoir operation in-
\par
cluding accurate real-time in ow forecast is required for realizing the
\par
above functions (Coulibaly et al., 2000; Lin et al., 2009; Louks and
\par
Sigvaldason, 1981; Valipour et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2018a).
\par
Previous studies on real-time reservoir operations showed that si-
\par
mulation models can provide more detailed and realistic representation
\par
of reservoir operations with lower computational demands and are
\par
more readily accepted by operators in practice (Lund and Guzman,
\par
1999; Uysal et al., 2016; Yang et al., 2016). Conceptual or physical-
\par
based models are widely used to simulate the controlled out ow and
\par
water level of single or multiple-reservoir systems. For example,
\par
Sigvaldson (1976) developed a mathematical reservoir simulation
\par
model for a multipurpose multiple-reservoir system based on the con-
\par
cept of priority. Georgakakos and Marks (1987) proposed an e cient
\par
method for the real-time operation by using a set of stochastic di er-
\par
ential equations to describe the dynamics in reservoirs. With the in-
\par
creasing development in river dynamics and computer technology,
\par
many advanced and complex simulation models have been developed
\par
and widely used in practice, such as the HEC-ResSim model (Klipsch
\par
and Hurst, 2007), the WEAP model (Purkey, 2005) and the CalSim
\par
model (Draper et al., 2004). These physical-based models provide solid
\par
foundations for estimating reservoir out ow and storage (Zhang et al.,
\par
2018a) through combing operation patterns described mathematically
\par
and empirical operation rule curves based on historical hydrological
\par
data. Since the process of reservoir operation is transparent and inter-
\par
pretable in physical-based models, it is easier for operators to
