However, simulation models are e ective when the prede ned op-
\par
eration policies established in the model re ect the actual operation
\par
process (Johnson et al., 1991; Yang et al., 2016). In actual reservoir
\par
operations, it is widely acknowledged that operators often add their
\par
own judgment to adapt to speci c conditions, objectives, or constraints
\par
existing at various situations. Operation curves are often used as a re-
\par
ference or guideline (Oliveira and Loucks, 1997), and the applicability
\par
of the physical model based on pre-established rule curves is in-
\par
su cient for reservoir operation in real time, especially during the
\par
extreme ood events (Chang and Chang, 2001; Saavedra Valeriano
\par
et al., 2010). An actual reservoir operation process sometimes deviates
\par
from the operational plan due to the operators' judgement or knowl-
\par
edge for managing emergencies such as oods and droughts. This
\par
makes the physical models di cult to accurately simulate real-time
\par
reservoir operation (Johnson et al., 1991; Oliveira and Loucks, 1997;
\par
Zhang et al., 2018a). Therefore, it needs to develop an e cient and
\par
easy-to-use reservoir simulation model based on the reservoir input and
\par
output data, which can take the operators' historical experience into
\par
account without the requirement of huge data for water resources
\par
management in the downstream.
\par
To overcome the limitations of physical models, hydrologists tried
\par
data-driven models to simulate the hydrological processes. Recently,
\par
machine learning (ML) techniques have been applied in hydrological
\par
time series forecast (Bai et al., 2016; Chen et al., 2013; Hung et al.,
\par
2009; Nasseri et al., 2008; Yu et al., 2017). As for the application in
\par
reservoir operation, the black-box nature of most ML models could
\par
make it di cult for operators to understand the process. However,
\par
since machine learning methods have super abilities to t complex
\par
high-dimensional relationships, they have been increasingly used for
\par
extracting the actual operation rules based on the historical records of
\par
in ow and out ow, which contains the empirical knowledge for
\par
dealing with the extreme ows and time-varying demands from the
\par
downstream (Corani et al., 2009; Rasouli et al., 2012; Wang et al.,
\par
2010; Yang et al., 2016; Zhang et al., 2018a).
\par
Among many machine learning models, multilayer perceptron
\par
models (MLPs), support vector machine and regression (SVM and SVR)
\par
and classi cation and regression tree (CART) have been applied for
\par
reservoir operation simulation. With the back-propagation (BP) algo-
\par
rithm and time-delayed input variables, MLP models were successfully
\par
used to simulate the out ow of reservoirs (Chaves and Chang, 2008;
\par
Jain et al., 1999). However, MLPs have several limitations, such as local
\par
optimal solutions and gradient disappearance to practical applications
\par
(Zhang et al., 2019). Based on statistical learning theory, SVM or SVR is
\par
widely used in the eld of reservoir operation (Aboutalebi et al., 2015;
\par
Ji et al., 2014; Lin et al., 2006) and reported to have better prediction
\par
accuracy of reservoir discharge over traditional arti cial neural net-
\par
works (Lin et al., 2006). SVM has the advantages of fast training speed
\par
and provides the best solutions, but it still has several limitations, such
\par
as the sensitivity to the choice of the kernel function and no guarantee
\par
to learn exact parameters for the best solutions (Patle and Chouhan,
\par
2013; Yan et al., 2003). As a type of decision tree models, CART was
\par
used to extract the actual reservoir operation pattern combined with a
\par
shu ed cross-validation scheme, and the controlled release and storage
\par
trajectories of nine major reservoirs in California were simulated (Yang
\par
et al., 2016). However, most of the previous studies tested the ML
\par
models for a short period, which may not su cient to demonstrate their
\par
applicabilities in di erent ow regimes, especially in extreme ood and
\par
drought conditions.
\par
With the feedback on both time-delayed input and output, RNN can
\par
preserve and remember the short-past and long-past information, and
\par
therefore, it is preferred for a complex dynamic timing and sequential
\par
problem (Adi et al., 2017; Chung et al., 2016; Koutnik et al., 2014;
\par
Zhang et al., 2019). Although the RNN still su ers from limitations of
\par
longer time consumption, the gradient vanishing, and exploding pro-
\par
blems, it is preferred for simulation of reservoir operation with dynamic
\par
Journal of Hydrology 579 (2019) 124229
\par
process and high dependence on the historical information. Zhang et al.
\par
(2018a) compared three machine learning models, namely the arti cial
\par
neural network, support vector regression and long short-time memory
\par
(LSTM) in simulations of reservoir operation at the Gezhouba Dam and
\par
reported that LSTM was a type of RNN which had the best overall
\par
performance. However, Gezhouba Dam is a hydropower plant without
\par
storage (Zhang, 2004) and has very limited ow regulation capacities
\par
(Sharma and Singh, 2013). In this case, in ow and out ow are highly
\par
correlated. By using three recurrent neural network models, Zhang
\par
et al. (2019) simulated Xiluodu Reservoir operation, which has an in-
\par
complete annual regulation ability (Chen, 2015). Further studies are
\par
desired to verify e ectiveness of RNN for the large reservoirs with
\par
multiannual ow regulation capacities, especially a focus on simulating
\par
reservoir operation in the extreme hydrological conditions ( oods and
\par
droughts) is required.
\par
Nonlinear AutoRegressive model with eXogenous input (NARX) (Lin
\par
et al., 1996; Menezes Jr and Barreto, 2008) and Long Short-Term
\par
Memory (LSTM) model (Gre et al., 2017; Hochreiter and
\par
Schmidhuber, 1997) are two widely used RNNs with di erent model
\par
structures in hydrological time series forecast. Compared with con-
\par
ventional neural networks, NARX adds loops to feed the previous input
\par
and output back to the network and pass the information from one step
\par
to the next, and the feedback delays, including input delays and output
\par
delays, represent the lag time of input and output. With input vectors
\par
built through tapped-delay lines, NARX model can be e ciently applied
\par
to long-term prediction of nonlinear time series (Chang et al., 2016;
\par
Menezes Jr and Barreto, 2008). With chain like modules similar to
\par
NARX, LSTM network adopts memory blocks that are composed of an
\par
input gate, an output gate, a memory cell and a forget gate (Gers,
\par
2002). These components are recurrently connected to each other and
\par
located between the input and the output layers to replace hidden
\par
layers so as to lter out nonessential information while preserving
\par
useful information from the long past (Shen, 2018). The two models
\par
have been applied for sewer over ow monitoring and groundwater
\par
level prediction (Chang et al., 2016; Shen, 2018; Wunsch et al., 2018;
\par
Zhang et al., 2018b), but there are few applications for operation si-
\par
mulation of reservoirs with multiannual ow regulation, especially
\par
under extreme ood and drought conditions.
\par
Physically based hydrological models have been widely used for
\par
hydrological simulation and forecast (Mateo et al., 2015). Among them,
\par
the Geomorphology-based hydrological model (GBHM) (Yang et al.,
\par
1998; Yang et al., 2002) has been successfully used in many catchments
\par
of China and Asia, such as Yangtze River (Li et al., 2015a), Mekong
\par
River (Wang et al., 2016) and Jiulong River (Lu et al., 2018). To pro-
\par
vide downstream water managers an out ow forecast with one day in
\par
advance at least, it is desired to test the GBHM for in ow forecast in a
\par
real-time reservoir operation. Chao Phraya River Basin covers about
\par
30\% of the total territory of Thailand, and a large amount of farmland
\par
and several big cities (such as Chiang Mai, Nakhon Sawan, Bangkok,
\par
etc.) are within the basin. The agriculture, economics and life of this
\par
basin are seriously a ected by the frequent oods and droughts
\par
(Ben eld, 2011; Promchote et al., 2016), and the Thailand government
\par
highly relies on reservoirs in the upstream for water resources man-
\par
agement (Hungspreug et al., 2000). With the challenge of climate
\par
change and population growth, reservoir operation is under great
\par
pressure, and operators need a high-e ciency reservoir operation
\par
model as a decision-supporting tool for coping with rapidly changing
\par
environments. In addition, an e cient real-time out ow forecast for
\par
managing the oods and droughts in the downstream regions is also
\par
required. Therefore, the reservoir operation tools need to be improved
\par
due to the rapidly changing environments, and development of the RNN
\par
o ers an opportunity in the reservoir operations in the upstream of
\par
Chao Phraya River in Thailand.
\par
In this study, we use RNNs to extract the reservoir operation rules
\par
from the historical operation data, and use GBHM to carry out the re-
\par
servoir in ow forecast. Combining the RNN-based reservoir operation
