4. Results
\par
In this section, we compared simulations of the reservoir operation
\par
by three RNNs, and presented the results of reservoir in ow simulation
\par
by GBHM model and the accuracy of real-time reservoir operations.
\par
3.5. Model performance evaluation
\par
We used root mean square errors (RMSE) and Pearson's correlation
\par
coe cients (r) to evaluate the performance of the models. Percent bias
\par
(PBIAS) and the Nash-Sutcli e e ciency (NSE) (Nash and Sutcli e,
\par
1970), which are both widely used to evaluate the accuracy of the
\par
hydrological model, are also chose to describe the statistical properties
\par
of the models in this study (Moriasi et al., 2015). The r and NSE are two
\par
normalized statistical indexes, and a value closer to 1 indicates a better
\par
simulation. As recommended by previous studies, the model perfor-
\par
mance can be judged``satisfactory'' for ow simulations if daily,
\par
monthly, or annual r greater than 0.84, NSE greater than 0.50,
\par
- {$\leq$} {$\leq$} \emph{PBIAS }15\% 15\% and the RMSE is less than half of the standard
\par
deviation of the observed data for watershed-scale models (Moriasi
\par
et al., 2007; Yang et al., 2016; Zhang et al., 2018a). In addition, we also
\par
adopt the di erence of the occurrence day of the annual maximum
\par
ood (DAMFD) to represent the timing error of the models, and a po-
\par
sitive value means that the simulated ood peak time lags behind the
\par
measured value. Since only the largest ood in a year is identi ed in the
\par
annual maximum ood series (AMF) and several large oods are
\par
usually ignored, this study used the peaks-over-threshold method (POT)
\par
to overcome this drawback. A threshold that leads to three events per
\par
year is used and the details of the AMF and POT can be found in Lang
\par
et al. (1999) and Mediero et al. (2014).
\par
4.1. Model performance comparison in reservoir operation simulation
\par
This section compares the simulated reservoir out ow by three
\par
RNNs to evaluate their performances.
\par
As for NARX and GA-NARX, the results of hyper-parameter selection
\par
show that the models with 8-time delays and 5 hidden neurons for the
\par
Bhumibol Reservoir, 10-time delays and 8 hidden neurons for the Sirikit
\par
Reservoir and 11-time delays and 9 hidden neurons for the Khwae Noi
\par
Bamrung Dan Reservoir have the most stable and accurate perfor-
\par
mance. As for LSTM, the results of hyper-parameter selection indicate
\par
that the models with the learning rate of 0.025 and 64 hidden nodes for
\par
the Bhumibol Reservoir, the learning rate of 0.002 and 32 hidden nodes
\par
for the Sirikit Reservoir and the learning rate of 0.0005 and 16 hidden
\par
nodes for the Khwae Noi Bamrung Dan Reservoir have the best per-
\par
formance.
\par
Table 1 summarizes the performances of three RNNs of three re-
\par
servoirs. Fig. 6 shows the simulation results of the Bhumibol Reservoir,
\par
and the results of other two reservoirs can be found in the supple-
\par
mentary materials. In Fig. 6, the results during the test period are
\par
plotted with a logarithmic scale to clear the model performance for low
\par
ows. We can see that three RNNs can simulate the out ow of three
\par
reservoirs satisfactorily with high NSE (larger than 0.65) and low ab-
\par
solute PBIAS value (less than 10\%) and GA-NARX is the best one among
\par
three RNNs. The results also indicate that the RNNs can simulate the
\par
operation of both multiannual regulation reservoir and incomplete
\par
annual regulation reservoir.
\par
To better evaluate the RNN-based reservoir operation models, we
\par
compare the performance of the models under di erent ow regimes
\par
and the results of the Bhumibol Reservoir are shown in Fig. 6. For low
\par
ow simulation, RNNs greatly overestimate the out ow of all three
\par
reservoirs with a positive PBIAS (larger than 20\%) and the LSTM model
\par
has the lowest accuracy. For intermediate ow, all RNNs have a slight
\par
underestimation or overestimation with a small PBIAS (less than 10\%).
\par
For high ow simulation, the RNNs all underestimate the high ow
\par
with a negative PBIAS (less than 20\%), and the GA-NARX model is
\par
obviously more accurate than other two models. The overestimation of
\par
low ow and the underestimation of high ow may be caused by the
\par
sigmoidal activation function (Sudheer et al., 2003). For sigmoidal
\par
functions, the slope gradually tends to 0 as the absolute value of in-
\par
dependent variable increasing, which indicates that the high ow will
