\par
2010 and 2011 as typical years and the results are shown in Fig. 8a. In
\par
the 2010 drought, the simulated out ow by the GA-NARX model
\par
matches the observed out ow with the NSE of 0.93 and the r of 0.96,
\par
and there is no obvious overestimation or underestimation with the
\par
PBIAS of-0.94\%. In the 2011 oods, the model also has good per-
\par
formances with the NSE of 0.79 and the r of 0.90, and slight
\par
underestimation of the out ow (PBIAS of-1.65\%). This shows that the
\par
operation model based on RNN can provide a reasonable estimation of
\par
the reservoir out ow during the extreme hydrological events, and the
\par
GA-NARX model is suitable for reservoir operation during the extreme
\par
droughts and oods. In addition, we also compare the daily storage
\par
trajectories (Fig. 8b) by GA-NARX under 2010 drought and 2011 ood
\par
with observed storage and operation curves. The results show that GA-
\par
NARX can better simulate the storage trajectories under extreme hy-
\par
drological events. Besides, the results show that the reservoir extended
\par
the release time from July to August to relieve the drought in 2010, and
\par
emptied its ood control storage in April ahead of schedule to mitigate
\par
the impact of ood in 2011. This indicates the operation rules extracted
\par
by GA-NARX can predict ood control and drought mitigation well.
