\par
5. Discussions
\par
5.1. Impact of inflow forecast error and operator's bias on the operation
\par
model
\par
To explore the in uence of in ow forecast errors on the RNN-based
\par
operation model, the in ow forecast errors are assumed to be random
\par
and can be tted to a normal distribution with a mean value of 0 and a
\par
standard deviation determined based on the observed in ow data
\par
(Baker, 1977; Datta and Burges, 1984; Sankarasubramanian et al.,
\par
2008; Zhao et al., 2011). We use Monte Carlo methods to generate 2000
\par
samples of the in ow from the distribution. Previous study has shown
\par
that the uncertainties of base ow simulation are smaller than that of
\par
peak ow simulation by GBHM and the range of the uncertainties is
\par
12S. Yang, et al. Journal of Hydrology 579 (2019) 124229
\par
Fig. 12. The results of the out ow forecast with the forecast period of 3 day (a), 5 days (b) and 7 days (c).
\par
about 10--20\% (Li et al., 2012). In this study, the standard deviations of
\par
the in ow forecast errors are given as 10\% and 20\%. Fig. 11 shows the
\par
results, and it can be found that the uncertainties of the simulated re-
\par
servoir out ow increase as the error of in ow forecast increases. The
\par
majority of the observed out ow is within the light grey bound (with
\par
95\% con dence interval), and the mean values of forecast out ow
\par
follow the observed values well, which also can be inferred that the GA-
\par
NARX model is robust for real time reservoir operation.
\par
Since the biases of the dam operators implicit in the historical op-
\par
eration data may a ect the future forecast, we discuss possible impacts
\par
under di erent situations. In general, since the historical operation
\par
rules contain not only the successful experience of engineers but also
\par
the lessons of their failures, these operation rules are not necessarily the
\par
best and may not achieve all the functions of the reservoir in the future.
\par
Operated by these rules in the future, the out ow may also fail to meet
\par
the requirement of the water management in some situations. For
\par
normal years, the reservoir operation according to the pre-established
\par
rules can generally meet the water demand of the downstream, which
\par
does not highly depend on the experience and judgment of operators.
\par
Therefore, the out ow forecast may be less a ected by those deviations.
\par
However, in case of extreme oods and droughts or other emergency
\par
situations, it is not enough to only use of the pre-established operation
\par
rules, and the operation process may highly depend on the experience
\par
of operators and the feedback of the downstream (Johnson et al., 1991;
\par
Oliveira and Loucks, 1997; Zhang et al., 2018a). Since the historical
\par
experience may not meet the requirements in those emergency situa-
\par
tions, the out ow forecast may also not meet the downstream water
\par
demand. To reduce the impacts of the historical biases, the operation
\par
model should be retrained every 5 to 10 years (Ahmad et al., 2014).
\par
This is an area that we need to further study in the future.
\par
5.2. Potential applications of the reservoir operation system
\par
The reservoir operation system established in this study has the
\par
potential for improving long-term water resources planning and real-
\par
time ood risk management.
\par
For water resources planning under a fast-changing environment,
\par
such as climate change and land use changes, long-term simulation of
\par
reservoir operation can provide references for the decision makers. By
\par
using the future meteorological data output from the General
\par
Circulation Models (GCMs), GBHM model can provide a long-term in-
\par
ow prediction based on the GCMs outputs and subsequently the RNN-
\par
based operation model can carry a long-term simulation of reservoir
\par
operation for future water resources planning.
\par
Real-time ood and drought risk management requires forecast of
\par
reservoir in ow and out ow with a few days leading time at least.
\par
Reservoir out ow forecast with input variables updated every three
\par
days, ve days and seven days are calculated and compared in Fig. 12.
\par
The results show that the reservoir operation system can provide a
\par
robust out ow forecast with a leading time up to 5 days.
\par
13S. Yang, et al. Journal of Hydrology 579 (2019) 124229
\par
In many cases, cascade reservoirs in a river basin always have hy-
\par
draulic connections and storage compensations and work together to
\par
meet the water demand (Zhou et al., 2014). Therefore, the connectivity
\par
of reservoirs should be quanti ed mathematically and used as a type of
\par
predictor variables in the operation model in future study. Besides, the
\par
meteorological forecast is not used in the in ow forecast, and thus,
\par
future study also need to address the applicability of weather forecast in
\par
real time reservoir operation.
\par
6. Conclusions
\par
This study investigated the applicability of three RNN models
\par
(NARX, LSTM and GA-NARX) for simulating the reservoir operation,
\par
and developed a real-time operation system for the Bhumibol Reservoir
\par
by combining a distributed hydrological model (GBHM) for in ow
\par
forecast. The performance of RNN-based reservoir operation models
\par
was evaluated under di erent ow regimes and extreme hydrological
\par
events. We discussed the applicability of GA-NARX and the impacts of
\par
the GA on the optimization of the NARX. According to the results, we
\par
found:
\par
(1) RNNs are capable of capturing the actual reservoir operation rules
\par
from the historical data and simulating the reservoir out ow
\par
properly. On the other hand, RNNs underestimate high ow and
\par
overestimate low ow due to the use of the sigmoidal activation
\par
function and the limited high and low ow data in the historical
\par
time series.
\par
(2) By optimizing the initial conditions of NARX, GA can greatly im-
\par
prove the prediction accuracy comparing with the traditional
\par
NARX. GA-NARX also overcomes the drawback of high sensitivity
\par
of NARX to the initial conditions.
\par
(3) GA-NARX-based operation model is e ective under extreme oods
\par
and droughts and can simulate the storage trajectories better than
\par
traditional methods, which implies that the GA-NARX-based op-
\par
eration model captures the engineer's judgements during the ex-
\par
treme ow conditions based on the feedback from downstream.
\par
(4) Coupling GBHM for the in ow forecast, the real-time operation
\par
system of the Bhumibol Reservoir developed in this study can
\par
provide robust reservoir out ow forecast with a one-day leading
\par
time. This is useful in real water resources management in the Chao
\par
Phraya River Basin.
