\par
Modeling biodiversity benchmarks in variable environments
\par
JIAN D. L. YEN ,
\par
1,2,5 JOSH DORROUGH ,
\par
3 IAN OLIVER ,
\par
3 MICHAEL SOMERVILLE,
\par
3 MEGAN J. MCNELLIE ,
\par
3,4
\par
CHRISTOPHER J. WATSON ,
\par
3 AND PETER A. VESK 1,2
\par
1School of BioSciences, The University of Melbourne, Parkville, VIC 3010, Australia
\par
2ARC Centre of Excellence for Environmental Decisions, The University of Melbourne, Parkville, VIC 3010, Australia 3Office of Environment and Heritage, GPO Box 39, Sydney, NSW 2001, Australia
\par
4Fenner School of Environment and Society, Frank Fenner Building, Building 141 Linnaeus Way, The Australian National University,
\par
Acton, ACT 2601, Australia
\par
Citation: Yen, J. D. L., J. Dorrough, I. Oliver, M. Somerville, M. J. McNellie, C. J. Watson, and
\par
P. A. Vesk. 2019. Modeling biodiversity benchmarks in variable environments. Ecological
\par
Applications 29(7):e01970. 10.1002/eap.1970
\par
Abstract. Effective environmental assessment and management requires quantifiable bio-
\par
diversity targets. Biodiversity benchmarks define these targets by focusing on specific biodiver-
\par
sity metrics, such as species richness. However, setting fixed targets can be challenging because
\par
many biodiversity metrics are highly variable, both spatially and temporally. We present a mul-
\par
tivariate, hierarchical Bayesian method to estimate biodiversity benchmarks based on the spe-
\par
cies richness and cover of native terrestrial vegetation growth forms. This approach uses
\par
existing data to quantify the empirical distributions of species richness and cover within
\par
growth forms, and we use the upper quantiles of these distributions to estimate contemporary,
\par
``best-on-offer'' biodiversity benchmarks. Importantly, we allow benchmarks to differ among
\par
vegetation types, regions, and seasons, and with changes in recent rainfall. We apply our
\par
method to data collected over 30 yr at\textasciitilde 35,000 floristic plots in southeastern Australia. Our
\par
estimated benchmarks were broadly consistent with existing expert-elicited benchmarks, avail-
\par
able for a small subset of vegetation types. However, in comparison with expert-elicited bench-
\par
marks, our data-driven approach is transparent, repeatable, and updatable; accommodates
\par
important spatial and temporal variation; aligns modeled benchmarks directly with field data
\par
and the concept of best-on-offer benchmarks; and, where many benchmarks are required, is
\par
likely to be more efficient. Our approach is general and could be used broadly to estimate bio-
\par
diversity targets from existing data in highly variable environments, which is especially relevant
\par
given rapid changes in global environmental conditions.
\par
Key words: Australia; best-on-offer benchmarks, biodiversity offsets; indicators; reference conditions;
\par
species richness; vegetation restoration.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Biodiversity benchmarks (hereafter, benchmarks) are
\par
routinely used in a range of assessment and management
\par
applications as the quantitative estimates of desirable
\par
biodiversity states (e.g., restoration ecology [Hobbs and
\par
Harris 2001], offsetting schemes [Bull et al. 2014a, b]).
\par
Despite debate on how best to apply these benchmarks
\par
(Suding 2011, Maron et al. 2012), it is agreed that natu-
\par
ral resource managers require transparent and repeat-
\par
able methods to quantify desirable and undesirable
\par
biodiversity states (Oliver et al. 2002, 2007, Quetier and
\par
Lavorel 2011, Pardo et al. 2012, Bull et al. 2014a, b).
\par
Deviations from benchmarks provide an estimate of the
\par
quality of a site and indicate the potential for improve-
\par
ments in biodiversity at a site (Sinclair et al. 2002).
\par
Manuscript received 4 July 2018; revised 31 May 2019;
\par
accepted 14 June 2019. Corresponding Editor: David S. Schi-
\par
mel.
\par
5 E-mail: Jian.Yen@unimelb.edu.au
\par
Benchmarks often represent quantitative estimates of
\par
``historical'' (e.g., pre-intensive agriculture, presettlement;
\par
Stoddard et al. 2006),``long undisturbed'' (Parkes et al.
\par
2003, Stoddard et al. 2006), or unmodified (Gibbons
\par
et al. 2008) reference states. However, it can be challeng-
\par
ing to define long-undisturbed or historical reference
\par
states because biodiversity records rarely predate human
\par
disturbances (Hobbs et al. 2010, Balaguer et al. 2014)
\par
and patterns and intensities of historical disturbances are
\par
poorly quantified. Identifying long-undisturbed reference
\par
states is further complicated by the need to specify the
\par
time period over which baseline levels of disturbance are
\par
defined. In addition, even where long-undisturbed refer-
\par
ence states are known, these states may be unattainable in
\par
contemporary and future systems due to ongoing changes
\par
in species composition, climate, and landscape configura-
\par
tion (Pauly 1995, Millar et al. 2007, Corlett 2016). Con-
\par
ceptually, the use of long-undisturbed reference states
\par
assumes that maximum biodiversity value occurs in
\par
undisturbed systems. However, maximum biodiversity
\par
Article e01970; page 1Article e01970; page 2 value can arise at intermediate levels of disturbance (e.g.,
\par
intermittent fire or livestock grazing; Shea et al. 2004).
\par
Best-on-offer biodiversity benchmarks
\par
An alternative to a long-undisturbed reference state is
\par
a ``best-on-offer'' reference state (Eyre et al. 2015) that
\par
represents the biodiversity values that exist under con-
\par
temporary conditions (Eyre et al. 2015). Our primary
\par
focus in this paper is the development and application of
\par
benchmarks that describe best-on-offer reference states.
\par
Specifically, we focus on a``more-is-better'' approach
\par
(e.g., more species or more habitat), wherein the distri-
\par
bution of available biodiversity states is used to set a
\par
minimum threshold for different biodiversity metrics.
\par
This approach draws on definitions of biological integ-
\par
rity, which often emphasize locations with many native
\par
species, few nonnative species, few overly dominant spe-
\par
cies, and high levels of functional diversity within or
\par
among species (Brooks et al. 1998, Oliver et al. 2014).
\par
Locations with high values of biodiversity metrics are
\par
expected to be associated with higher biodiversity values,
\par
increased resistance to external stressors, and reliable
\par
provision of key ecosystem services (Hobbs et al. 2010).
\par
Importantly, a focus on contemporary biodiversity states
\par
avoids the need to characterize past disturbances (natu-
\par
ral and anthropogenic) at large spatial extents.
\par
Best-on-offer reference states can be defined through
\par
expert elicitation, with experts commonly asked to iden-
\par
tify either the values of biodiversity metrics that collec-
\par
tively define a best-on-offer state or existing sites that
\par
reflect best-on-offer reference states (Hiers et al. 2012,
\par
Sinclair et al. 2015). Although expert elicitation can
\par
quantify abstract and complex reference states without
\par
knowledge of past disturbances, expert assessments are
\par
inherently subjective (Sinclair et al. 2015). Similarly, the
\par
identification of reference sites relies on expert experi-
\par
ence, knowledge, and ability to recognize when a site clo-
\par
sely resembles the desirable reference state (Eyre et al.
\par
2011).
\par
An alternative to expert elicitation is to use empirical
\par
data to characterize the distribution of available states,
\par
and to use value judgements to specify desirable regions
\par
of this empirical distribution. Calculating benchmarks
\par
from data defines contemporary best-on-offer reference
\par
states transparently and aligns benchmarks directly with
\par
field methods, which may not always match an expert's
\par
conceptual knowledge of biodiversity. Increasing avail-
\par
ability of archived data (Bruelheide et al. 2019) allows
\par
data-driven benchmarks to be calculated rapidly over
\par
large spatial extents, a process that would be expensive
\par
and time-consuming using expert elicitation.
\par
Estimating best-on-offer benchmarks over large spatial
\par
scales
\par
Estimating benchmarks over large spatial scales is
\par
challenging because most ecological systems are highly
\par
JIAN D. L. YEN ET AL. Ecological Applications
\par
Vol. 29, No. 7
\par
variable in space and time (Bull et al. 2013, Kirkman
\par
et al. 2013). Attempts to define a best-on-offer reference
\par
state should reflect climatic and biogeographical varia-
\par
tion at local and regional scales (Sinclair et al. 2002,
\par
Harris et al. 2006). Benchmarks that do not account for
\par
spatial or temporal variation risk under- or over-valuing
\par
particular reference states depending on the time of sam-
\par
pling and contemporary climatic conditions. Identifying
\par
spatial and temporal variation in reference states is espe-
\par
cially important given potential differences between
\par
past, contemporary, and future environmental condi-
\par
tions. The use of data-driven benchmarks is particularly
\par
valuable in this context because benchmarks can be
\par
updated as new data are collected or made available.
\par
Benchmarks could be estimated for many different
\par
biodiversity metrics, such as species richness, relative
\par
abundance or cover, habitat complexity, and functional
\par
diversity. Species richness is a common biodiversity met-
\par
ric because it is widely understood, easily measured for
\par
better known taxonomic groups, and has close associa-
\par
tions with measures of ecological integrity, such as resis-
\par
tance, resilience, and ecosystem functioning (Hooper
\par
et al. 2005). Relative abundance and cover are alterna-
\par
tives to species richness that account for differences in
\par
dominance and rarity among species or functional
\par
groups (Yen et al. 2017a). Differences in relative abun-
\par
dances and cover affect the functional roles performed
\par
by different species, and can influence the demographic
\par
processes (e.g., survival, reproduction) that determine
\par
long-term population or community persistence (Mace
\par
et al. 2008, Stuart-Smith et al. 2013). Together, species
\par
richness and cover or abundance data provide comple-
\par
mentary information on the nature of vegetation struc-
\par
ture, composition, and function and often are used as
\par
practical surrogates for biodiversity more generally
\par
(Quetier and Lavorel 2011).
\par
We sought to develop a statistical method to estimate
\par
spatially and temporally variable benchmarks from
\par
existing floristic plot data collected at a large spatial
\par
extent. We used a multivariate, hierarchical Bayesian
\par
model to estimate distributions of native species richness
\par
and summed cover (hereafter, cover) at\textasciitilde 35,000 sites
\par
spanning broad climatic, biogeographical, and distur-
\par
bance gradients in southeastern Australia (\textasciitilde 800,000
\par
km2). We used quantiles of these distributions to define
\par
best-on-offer benchmarks for each of six plant growth
\par
forms (grasses, forbs, ferns, shrubs, trees, not otherwise
\par
classified [e.g., twiners, grass trees, palms]; see Oliver
\par
et al. 2019) in each combination of vegetation type, bio-
\par
geographical region (bioregion), and month, for three
\par
prior rainfall amounts (below average, average, above
\par
average) based on the preceding 12 months (Dudney
\par
et al. 2017). This approach defines best-on-offer refer-
\par
ence states as those locations with high native species
\par
richness and cover relative to other locations within the
\par
same vegetation type and bioregion. The multivariate,
\par
hierarchical model structure accounted for dependencies
\par
among growth forms and allowed information fromOctober 2019 DYNAMIC BIODIVERSITY BENCHMARKS Article e01970; page 3
\par
extensively sampled vegetation types and bioregions to
\par
inform parameter estimates in sparsely sampled vegeta-
\par
tion types and bioregions. We compared our modeled
\par
benchmarks to existing expert-elicited benchmarks that
\par
were available for a small subset of the vegetation types
\par
included in our present study.
\par
METHODS
\par
Study region
\par
We used data spanning the state of New South Wales
\par
in south-eastern Australia. The study region contains
\par
eight major climate zones, including arid, temperate,
\par
subtropical, and alpine (Stern et al. 2000). Vegetation
\par
across New South Wales is classified into 99 vegetation
\par
classes nested in 16 vegetation formations (Keith 2004).
\par
Vegetation formations are primarily distinguished by
\par
structural attributes and vegetation classes are defined
\par
by shared floristic and structural features (Keith 2004).
\par
We had data from 95 vegetation classes in 16 vegetation
\par
formations. The study region also has been classified
\par
into 18 biogeographical regions (bioregions) as part of
\par
the Interim Biogeographic Regionalization of Australia
\par
(IBRA) (Thackway and Cresswell 1995). Bioregions dif-
\par
fer in climate, substrate, and soils but do not explicitly
\par
differ in vegetation type (Thackway and Cresswell 1995),
\par
so that a single vegetation class may occur in multiple
\par
bioregions. All vegetation survey plots used in this study
\par
had been previously allocated to a vegetation class as
\par
part of state-wide vegetation mapping and classification
\par
(available online).6
\par
using a subset (2,809 plots) of the 6,789 true cover esti-
\par
mates (McNellie et al. 2019), and transformed all BBCA
\par
data to quantitative estimates accordingly. Cover esti-
\par
mates of trees were based on crown cover, foliage cover,
\par
or projective foliage cover (Walker and Hopkins 1990).
\par
We included the method of cover assessment, recorded
\par
for 26,130 plots, as a covariate in preliminary models.
\par
However, we did not include cover assessment method in
\par
final models because it was unknown for 10,242 plots
\par
and 95\% credible intervals for the effects of cover assess-
\par
ment method overlapped zero.
\par
Prior to estimating species richness and cover, we allo-
\par
cated all native taxa to one of six growth forms: ferns,
\par
forbs, grasses and grass-like (hereafter, grasses), shrubs,
\par
trees, and not otherwise classified (hereafter, other; Oli-
\par
ver et al. 2019). Growth form was attributed based on a
\par
standardized species list for species endemic to New
\par
South Wales (Oliver et al. 2019). We calculated species
\par
richness and cover of each growth form within each plot
\par
by summing over all native species assigned to that
\par
growth form (Appendix S1: Figs S1--S2). This approach
\par
resulted in some summed cover estimates exceeding
\par
100\% due to species within growth forms having overlap-
\par
ping foliage. The majority of cover values were {$<$}100\%
\par
(Appendix S1: Fig. S2). We calculated summed percent
\par
cover of nonnative species, irrespective of their growth
\par
form, with this same approach.
\par
Vegetation data
\par
We used full species inventories from floristic plots
\par
surveyed between 1976 and 2016 to estimate species rich-
\par
ness and cover. From a data set of\textasciitilde 60,000 plots we iden-
\par
tified 35,615 plots for species richness and 35,493 plots
\par
for cover that had been sampled using a standard
\par
400 m2, typically 20 9 20 m quadrat. Some plots were
\par
surveyed multiple times, so that there were a total of
\par
36,543 surveys for richness and 36,372 surveys for cover.
\par
Of the 1,710 theoretical combinations of bioregion (18)
\par
and vegetation class (95), we had floristic plot data for
\par
469 combinations (27\%). Many of the unobserved com-
\par
binations do not exist and are unlikely to exist under
\par
current climatic conditions (e.g., subtropical rainforests
\par
in the Australian Alps bioregion).
\par
Cover was visually estimated for each species at each
\par
plot. Cover was estimated quantitatively (0--100\%) in
\par
6,789 plots and was recorded on various Braun-Blanquet
\par
cover-abundance (BBCA) ordinal scales for the remain-
\par
ing 29,583 plots. We previously used a beta regression
\par
model to estimate quantitative cover from BBCA data,
\par
Rainfall data
\par
We used gridded daily rainfall totals from the Aus-
\par
tralian Water Availability Project (Jones et al. 2009) to
\par
calculate accumulated rainfall in the 12 months prior to
\par
each survey (rainfall data available online).7 These data
\par
are at a resolution of 0.05{$^\circ$} 9 0.05{$^\circ$} (approximately 5 9
\par
5 km). Rainfall values were interpolated from gridded
\par
data to survey location and the rainfall time series was
\par
extracted for the 12 months prior to a given survey. The
\par
12-month accumulated rainfall was calculated at each
\par
plot location and date. This approach was also used to
\par
calculate 3-month and 36-month accumulated rainfall
\par
but we did not include these variables in our models
\par
because both were positively correlated with 12-month
\par
accumulated rainfall (r = 0.70 and 0.89, respectively).
\par
We used rainfall as a predictor variable in fitted mod-
\par
els and used estimates of below average (10th quantile),
\par
average (50th quantile), and above average (90th quan-
\par
tile) rainfall to estimate benchmarks at different rainfall
\par
levels (see Calculating species richness and cover bench-
\par
marks, below). Given that only 469 (27\%) of the theoreti-
\par
cal combinations of bioregion and vegetation class were
\par
observed, we needed to estimate rainfall levels in the
\par
remaining 1,241 combinations. We used rainfall data
\par
from 1900 to 2015 to calculate 10th, 50th, and 90th
\par
quantiles of rainfall in the 12 months prior to the survey
\par
month at each of the initial\textasciitilde 60,000 plots (prior to
