\par
We allowed rainfall effects to differ among bioregions.
\par
Although we expected generally positive effects of rain-
\par
fall, the magnitude of this effect on richness and cover
\par
might vary systematically owing to broad differences in
\par
climate and landform. For example, we expected that
\par
small increases in total rainfall would have larger effects
\par
in arid and semiarid regions than in coastal regions that
\par
generally experience higher rainfall amounts.
\par
We modeled monthly trends in two ways. First, we
\par
estimated smooth monthly trends and allowed these
\par
trends to differ among bioregions. This model form
\par
incorporates seasonal variation in species richness and
\par
cover and reflects our expectation that seasonal patterns
\par
differ throughout the study region due to broad differ-
\par
ences among arid, temperate, alpine, and subtropical cli-
\par
matic zones. Second, we included months as random
\par
intercepts in our model but did not allow monthly varia-
\par
tion to differ among bioregions. This model form is less
\par
ecologically realistic but has 186 fewer parameters to
\par
estimate than the dynamic model, which was expected to
\par
improve model predictions in bioregions with few plots.
\par
Data analysis: overview
\par
Our aim was to develop a statistical method to esti-
\par
mate benchmarks from existing floristic plot data.
\par
Specifically, we related data on species richness and
\par
cover of six growth forms to vegetation class, bioregion,
\par
month, rainfall, and nonnative cover to estimate distri-
\par
butions of species richness and cover in each month in
\par
each combination of bioregion and vegetation class at
\par
three rainfall levels. We used quantiles from this distribu-
\par
tion to define benchmarks, that is, best-on-offer biodi-
\par
versity states.
\par
We used a multivariate, hierarchical Bayesian model
\par
to model all growth forms simultaneously, but fitted sep-
\par
arate models for native species richness and cover. Our
\par
model accounts for correlations in species richness and
\par
cover among growth forms, and allows for differences in
\par
species richness and cover among vegetation classes,
\par
bioregions, and months. We assumed that vegetation
\par
class and bioregion had additive effects on species rich-
\par
ness and cover, and nested the effects of vegetation
\par
classes within those of the broader vegetation forma-
\par
tions. The model also included effects of rainfall and
\par
percentage cover of nonnative species, as well as stochas-
\par
tic variation in species richness among years and plots.
\par
Vegetation classes are partially based on systematic
\par
differences in the species richness and cover of different
\par
growth forms. We expected these systematic differences
\par
among vegetation classes to capture many of the correla-
\par
tions among growth forms. For example, shifts from tree
\par
cover in forests to grass cover in grasslands are reflected
\par
in different vegetation classes. Our multivariate model
\par
also accounted for residual correlations among growth
\par
forms. The model structure reflects our expectation that
\par
growth forms are not independent within vegetation
\par
classes. For example, species richness and cover might be
\par
positively correlated among growth forms due to shared
\par
responses to local environmental conditions (e.g., soil
\par
nutrient availability, recent rainfall). By contrast, species
\par
richness and cover might be negatively correlated among
\par
growth forms due to competitive interactions within a
\par
single vegetation type (e.g., trees and grasses; Scholes
\par
and Archer 1997).
\par
Our model included cover of nonnative vegetation as
\par
a predictor variable but did not explicitly include prior
\par
anthropogenic disturbance, for which reliable data were
\par
unavailable. Nonnative vegetation cover is often posi-
\par
tively correlated with the frequency and intensity of dis-
\par
turbances related to landscape transformations such as
