\par
We used an LKJ prior for the covariance matrix {$\Sigma$}
\par
(Lewandowski et al. 2009). The LKJ prior is governed
\par
by a single parameter a, whose value determines the
\par
prior weight assigned to different correlation matrices.
\par
Setting a equal to 1 assigns equal weight to all correla-
\par
tion matrices, while increasing this parameter above 1
\par
places more weight on the unit diagonal correlation
\par
matrix. Specifically, the density of the LKJ prior is pro-
\par
portional to the determinant of the correlation matrix
\par
raised to the power of the parameter a (i.e. p({$\Phi$}) = \textbar{$\Phi\vert$}a
\par
,
\par
where {$\Phi$} is the correlation matrix). The full covariance
\par
matrix {$\Sigma$} can be recovered by multiplying {$\Phi$} by a vector
\par
of standard deviations, rs. We set a equal to 4 and
\par
assigned rs a half-normal prior distribution with mean 0
\par
and standard deviation equal to 2.
