Computational details
\par
We fitted the above model in Stan version 2.12 (Car-
\par
penter et al. 2017, Stan Development Team 2017a) using
\par
the rstan R package (version 2.12.1; Stan Development
\par
Team 2017b) in R version 3.4.0 (R Core Team 2017).
\par
Stan is general purpose software for Bayesian models,
\par
and uses Hamiltonian Monte Carlo to generate samples
\par
from the posterior distribution more efficiently thanArticle e01970; page 6 JIAN D. L. YEN ET AL. Ecological Applications
\par
Vol. 29, No. 7
\par
many other MCMC methods (e.g., Metropolis-Hastings;
\par
Hoffman and Gelman 2014). All inferences were based
\par
on four chains of 5,000 iterations following a 5,000 itera-
\par
tion burn-in period. We assessed model convergence
\par
using Gelman-Rubin statistics (Gelman and Rubin
\par
1992) and the effective number of samples, neff. All Rhats
\par
were {$<$}1.01 and neff was greater than 1,000 for all param-
\par
eters. Links to model code and data are in Supporting
\par
information.
