\par
information). We note that expert-elicited benchmarks
\par
were not obtained for the purpose of validating our
\par
modeling approach but, rather, were used opportunisti-
\par
cally to assess whether modeled benchmarks were
\par
broadly consistent with expert knowledge of these vege-
\par
tation classes. There were several differences in defini-
\par
tions between elicited and modeled benchmarks. In
\par
particular, expert-elicited benchmarks were derived for
\par
spring (October) in a median rainfall year and cover esti-
\par
mates were defined as visual estimates of growth-form
\par
cover. Visual estimates of growth-form cover differ from
\par
the plot data used to derive modeled benchmarks, where
\par
cover was estimated from summed species-cover esti-
\par
mates. Also, rather than estimating the 75th percentile,
\par
experts were asked to estimate the range of values they
\par
would expect among sites that were in a best-on-offer
\par
reference state in the contemporary landscape. Expert-
\par
elicited benchmarks were available for Inland Flood-
\par
plain Woodlands in the Riverina bioregion, Sand Plain
\par
Mulga Shrublands in the Mulga Lands bioregion, Sub-
\par
tropical Rainforests in the South Eastern Queensland
\par
bioregion, and Western Slopes Grassy Woodlands in the
\par
Brigalow Belt South bioregion.
\par
Calculating species richness and cover benchmarks
\par
We used quantiles of marginal posterior distributions
\par
to estimate benchmarks. We note that any quantile could
\par
be used as a benchmark value; here, we estimated the
\par
55th, 65th, and 75th quantiles to represent lower
\par
through to higher benchmark values.
\par
Our multivariate model accounted for correlated
\par
residuals among growth forms in model fitting but our
\par
use of marginal posterior distributions assumed that the
\par
posterior quantiles of species richness or cover are inde-
\par
pendent among growth forms, conditional on vegetation
\par
class, bioregion, month, rainfall, and nonnative species
\par
cover. We did not use full posterior quantiles because
\par
multivariate quantiles have multiple dimensions (six
\par
dimensions in our case) and cannot be summarized as a
\par
single number for each growth form (Chaudhuri 1996).
\par
We calculated species richness and cover benchmarks
\par
in two ways. First, we used models with regionally vary-
\par
ing monthly trends to calculate dynamic benchmarks:
\par
one value for each growth form in each combination of
\par
vegetation class, bioregion, and month, at each of three
\par
rainfall levels (below average, average, above average).
\par
This approach resulted in 20,520 dynamic species rich-
\par
ness and cover benchmarks for each growth form and
\par
rainfall level (738,720 in total). Second, we used models
\par
with regionally consistent monthly trends to calculate
\par
static benchmarks: one value for each growth form in
\par
each combination of vegetation class and bioregion
\par
using the average rainfall for each combination of biore-
\par
gion and vegetation class. This approach resulted in
\par
1,710 static species richness and cover benchmarks for
\par
each growth form (20,520 in total). We assumed a best-
\par
on-offer reference state would exclude the presence of
\par
nonnative plant species and so all estimated benchmarks
\par
were based on 0\% nonnative plant species cover. In our
\par
data set, minimum nonnative cover was 0\% in 69\% of
\par
observed combinations of bioregion and vegetation
\par
class, and minimum nonnative cover was {$<$}5\% in 83\% of
\par
observed combinations.
\par
