RESULTS
\par
Patterns in modeled species richness and cover
\par
Estimated mean native species richness was highest in
\par
forbs, shrubs, and grasses and lowest in trees, ferns, and
\par
growth forms not otherwise classified (others; Table 1).
\par
Rainfall was positively associated with species richness
\par
of all growth forms (5--24\% increase in species richness
\par
for each standard deviation increase in rainfall) with the
\par
strongest associations with ferns, forbs, and others and
\par
the weakest with trees. The cover of nonnative species
\par
had a weak, negative association with species richness of
\par
all growth forms (1--12\% decrease in species richness for
\par
each standard deviation increase in nonnative cover;
\par
Table 1).
\par
Fitted models explained 20--55\% of the variation in
\par
observed species richness (links to model fit statistics are
\par
in Supporting information). There was substantial varia-
\par
tion in species richness among vegetation classes with
\par
less variation among bioregions and vegetation forma-
\par
tions (Fig. 1). Estimated mean native species richness of
\par
forbs and grasses differed more among years than
\par
among months (Fig. 1). Estimated associations were
\par
very similar in static and dynamic models of species rich-
\par
ness. Links to full parameter estimates are in Supporting
\par
information.
\par
Estimated mean cover was highest in shrubs and
\par
grasses and lowest in ferns and others (Table 1). Rainfall
\par
was strongly positively associated with cover of all
\par
growth forms (11--117\% increase in cover for each stan-
\par
dard deviation increase in rainfall) with strongest associ-
\par
ations with ferns, forbs, grasses, and others (Table 1).October 2019 DYNAMIC BIODIVERSITY BENCHMARKS Article e01970; page 7
\par
The cover of nonnative species also had a positive associ-
\par
ation with the cover of all growth forms with the excep-
\par
tion of shrubs (7--19\% increase in cover for each
\par
standard deviation increase in nonnative cover; Table 1).
\par
Fitted models explained 6--20\% of the variation in
\par
observed summed cover (links to model fit statistics are
\par
in Supporting information). There was substantial varia-
\par
tion in cover among vegetation classes with less variation
\par
among bioregions and vegetation formations (Fig. 2).
\par
Estimated mean native cover differed among years, with
\par
forbs and grasses differing more among years than other
\par
growth forms (Fig. 2). Estimated associations were simi-
\par
lar in static and dynamic models of cover.
\par
Comparison of modeled benchmarks with expert-elicited
\par
benchmarks
\par
Modeled benchmarks for both richness and cover
\par
aligned closely with expert-elicited benchmarks for all
\par
growth forms (Fig. 3). Differences between modeled and
\par
elicited benchmarks were most pronounced in forbs and
\par
grasses, where modeled benchmarks tended to be lower
\par
than elicited benchmarks, and in rainforest tree and
\par
shrub cover in Mulga Shrublands, where modeled
\par
benchmarks were higher than elicited benchmarks
\par
(Fig. 3). The number of plots underlying modeled
\par
benchmarks was not consistently associated with the dif-
\par
ference between modeled and elicited benchmarks
\par
(Fig. 3).
\par
Correlations among growth forms
\par
Residual species richness was positively correlated
\par
among all growth forms in static and dynamic models.
\par
In dynamic models, correlation coefficients (r) ranged
\par
from 0.12 to 0.95 (mean= 0.53; Table 2). The highest
\par
correlations were between forbs and grasses (0.95) and
\par
between others and ferns (0.94; Table 2). The lowest cor-
\par
relations were between shrubs and forbs (0.16) and
\par
between trees and grasses (0.12; Table 2). Tree species
\par
richness was strongly correlated with that of ferns (0.76),
\par
shrubs (0.76), and others (0.89; Table 2). Correlations
\par
among growth forms in static models did not differ from
\par
those in the dynamic model by more than 0.02.
\par
Residual cover was mostly positively correlated
\par
among growth forms in static and dynamic models. In
\par
dynamic models, correlation coefficients (r) ranged from
\par
--0.04 to 0.36 (mean= 0.12) (Table 2). Grasses were the
\par
exception, with negative correlations with trees (r =
\par
--0.01) and shrubs (r =--0.04). All other pairs of growth
\par
forms were positively correlated, with the highest corre-
\par
lations between grasses and forbs (r = 0.36) and others
\par
and forbs (r = 0.21; Table 2). Correlations among
\par
growth forms in static models did not differ from those
\par
in the dynamic model by more than 0.01.
\par
Patterns in modeled benchmarks
\par
Species richness benchmarks were highest in shrubs
\par
and forbs, followed by trees, others, grasses, and ferns
\par
(Appendix S2: Figs S1--S3). Cover benchmarks were
\par
highest in trees, shrubs, and other growth forms, fol-
\par
lowed by ferns, grasses, and forbs (Appendix S2: Figs
\par
S4--S6). We note that summed cover could exceed 100\%
\par
in our data set due to overlapping foliage among species
\par
within a growth form. Modeled cover benchmarks were
\par
highest and most variable in combinations of bioregion,
\par
vegetation class, and month for which we had few obser-
\par
vations, and this variability increased from lower to
\par
higher benchmark quantiles (Appendix S2: Figs S4--S6).
\par
Static and dynamic benchmarks were broadly similar
\par
in many cases. However, richness and cover were associ-
\par
ated with season and past rainfall in some vegetation
\par
classes, bioregions, and growth forms. The effects of sea-
\par
son and past rainfall differed among vegetation classes
\par
and bioregions, and were most pronounced in forbs
\par
(Fig. 4). For example, forb richness and cover peaked in
\par
the austral spring in southern and central bioregions
\par
(e.g., Riverina bioregion, Fig 4a, b) but in the austral
