\par
Although vegetation type explained much of the varia-
\par
tion in species richness and cover of shrubs and trees,
\par
temporally varying factors such as year and rainfall
\par
often explained substantial amounts of the variation in
\par
richness and cover of forbs, grasses, and growth forms
\par
not otherwise classified (others). Temporal variation in
\par
richness and cover often differed among vegetation
\par
classes and bioregions (e.g., spring vs. summer peaks at
\par
different latitudes), and accounting for these interactions
\par
in benchmark estimates is a key feature of our modeling
\par
approach. Dynamic benchmarks that accounted for
\par
monthly variation and recent rainfall amounts followed
\par
ecologically plausible trends in combinations of biore-
\par
gion and vegetation class. Substantial variation in
\par
benchmarks among vegetation types, bioregions, years,
\par
and rainfall levels reinforces the need for benchmarks
\par
that differ both spatially and temporally (Bull et al.
\par
2013, Kirkman et al. 2013).
\par
We estimated benchmarks for each of six growth
\par
forms: ferns, forbs, grasses, shrubs, trees, and others.
\par
Growth forms are an intermediate grouping that sim-
\par
plify the complexity of full species inventories without
\par
reducing these inventories to simple biodiversity metrics
\par
such as total species richness and total abundance or
\par
cover (Mayfield et al. 2010, Maseyk et al. 2016, Yen
