\par
cies richness and cover benchmarks among growth
\par
forms, which supports the use of disaggregated bench-
\par
marks in our study system. Calculating separate (disag-
\par
gregated) benchmarks for each growth form tracks
\par
compositional, structural, and functional components
\par
of biodiversity, and reduces the risk that one component
\par
can substitute or``eclipse'' another, for example the loss
\par
of forb species being substituted for by the addition of
\par
shrub species (McCarthy et al. 2004, Oliver et al. 2014).
\par
Accounting for variation among different components
\par
of biodiversity is critical to distinguishing sites that differ
\par
in composition, structure, or function but that otherwise
\par
have similar total numbers of species or relative cover
\par
(e.g., grasslands and rainforests).
