\par
richness and cover being overestimated in uncommon
\par
growth forms and underestimated in diverse or com-
\par
mon growth forms. Individual bias has been shown to
\par
affect elicited estimates of bounded values (Mon-
\par
tibeller and von Winterfeldt 2015), including elicited
\par
abundances of rare species (Lichtenstein et al. 1978,
\par
Farmer et al. 2012).
\par
Although it is difficult to validate biodiversity bench-
\par
marks without independent, a priori knowledge of refer-
\par
ence states, differences between elicited and modeled
\par
benchmarks indicate that these two approaches might be
\par
complementary rather than competing. Although a
\par
requirement for many benchmarks ({$>$}700,000 values in
\par
this study) precludes the elicitation of all benchmarks,
\par
targeted elicitation might inform benchmarks in loca-
\par
tions with few or no data or when modeled benchmarks
\par
have low precision. In these cases, elicited benchmarks
\par
might be used to define benchmarks directly or to
\par
inform prior distributions to be updated when data
\par
become available. An important consequence of model-
\par
ing benchmarks from empirical data is that these bench-
\par
marks can be based on the same field methods,
\par
measurement scales, and data types commonly used in
\par
contemporary environmental assessment and manage-
\par
ment applications. Aligning benchmarks with data col-
\par
lection is important because empirical data will not
\par
necessarily reflect an expert's conceptualization of a
\par
given vegetation type.
\par
Expert-elicited vs. data-driven benchmarks
\par
Although our estimated static benchmarks (averaged
\par
over months and rainfall amounts) were broadly con-
\par
sistent with expert-elicited benchmarks for four com-
\par
binations of bioregion and vegetation class, there were
\par
several differences between modeled and expert-eli-
\par
cited benchmarks. Specifically, elicited benchmarks
\par
exceeded modeled benchmarks for forbs, grasses, and
\par
ferns in several vegetation classes, and modeled
\par
benchmarks exceeded elicited benchmarks for shrubs
\par
and trees in some vegetation classes (Fig. 3). Within a
\par
vegetation type, elicited benchmarks tended to exceed
\par
modeled benchmarks in low diversity growth forms
\par
(e.g., grasses in rainforests), while the opposite was
\par
true of highly diverse growth forms (e.g., trees in rain-
\par
forests). These differences might be due to differences
\par
in data or differences in perceptions or interpretations
\par
of sampling scales. For example, modeled benchmarks
\par
were based on summed estimates of cover from spe-
\par
cies inventories, whereas experts were asked to con-
\par
sider visual estimates of growth-form cover, which do
\par
not necessarily include species with overlapping cover.
\par
Similarly, elicited benchmarks are subject to high
\par
levels of individual bias, with a tendency to avoid
\par
very low or high estimates possibly resulting in species
\par
Ecological inferences from estimated benchmarks
\par
Our method characterized variation in the richness
\par
and cover of plant growth forms among vegetation
\par
types, bioregions, seasons, and at multiple rainfall levels.
\par
Past rainfall and resulting changes in soil moisture are
\par
particularly important for fast-growing and shallow-
\par
rooted plants (e.g., grasses and forbs), and can substan-
\par
tially influence species richness and cover in these groups
\par
(Zavaleta et al. 2003). We observed strong seasonal pat-
\par
terns in some combinations of vegetation class and
\par
bioregion, but the nature of these patterns often differed
\par
among vegetation classes or bioregions. In many cases,
\par
benchmarks differed more among recent rainfall levels
\par
than among months, which indicates that recent rainfall
\par
explains more variation in cover and richness than sea-
\par
son. Failing to account for variation due to recent rain-
\par
fall and season could result in benchmarks that are
\par
either unattainable or too-readily attainable, which may
\par
lead to undesirable biodiversity outcomes (Kirkman
\par
et al. 2013). Close associations with rainfall suggest that
\par
benchmarks might be sensitive to changes in natural dis-
\par
turbance regimes, such as drought. An advantage of
\par
variable, data-driven benchmarks is their explicit incor-
\par
poration of changing environmental conditions, with the
\par
capacity to update benchmarks as new data become
\par
available.
