theoretical and empirical work on coexistence
\par
between grasses and woody vegetation (Walker et al.
\par
1986, Scholes and Archer 1997).
\par
Implications of variable, data-driven benchmarks
\par
We estimated benchmarks as the 55th, 65th, and 75th
\par
quantiles of the marginal posterior species richness orOctober 2019 DYNAMIC BIODIVERSITY BENCHMARKS Article e01970; page 13
\par
cover distribution. A remaining challenge for practical
\par
application of our method is the identification of the
\par
most appropriate quantile for use as the best-on-offer
\par
reference state. Our use of quantiles explicitly assumes
\par
that many native species and high cover are more desir-
\par
able than few native species and low cover relative to
\par
other locations within the same vegetation type and
\par
bioregion. We assume that more native species and
\par
higher cover are associated with higher biological integ-
\par
rity (Oliver et al. 2014). Under this assumption, the
\par
choice of the appropriate quantile is inherently subjec-
\par
tive but may be guided by four considerations. First, if
\par
there is a sampling bias towards relatively undisturbed
\par
(or disturbed) vegetation, a lower (or higher) quantile
\par
may be appropriate. Second, we found that small sample
\par
sizes and large amounts of residual variation widened
\par
posterior credible intervals and potentially inflated
\par
benchmarks, and this variability increased from lower to
\par
higher quantiles, which might suggest that a lower quan-
\par
tile is appropriate when few data are available or when
\par
residual variation is high. For example, residual varia-
\par
tion was higher for cover than richness in our case study,
\par
indicating that a lower quantile might be appropriate for
\par
cover benchmarks. Third, independent estimates of rich-
\par
ness and cover benchmarks might result in high quan-
\par
tiles being unattainable for richness and cover
\par
simultaneously, in which case a lower benchmark might
\par
be needed to ensure that current and future sites can fea-
\par
sibly achieve best-on-offer status. Last, biodiversity
\par
benchmarks used in biodiversity offset schemes can have
\par
significant implications for offset ratios. When used to
\par
assess a potential development site, low quantiles will
\par
generally result in higher offset requirements whereas
\par
high quantiles will result in lower offset requirements.
\par
Each of these four points requires careful consideration
\par
when selecting appropriate quantiles for use in a particu-
\par
lar benchmark application.
\par
A related challenge for practical applications is the
\par
choice between dynamic and static benchmarks.
\par
Although dynamic benchmarks capture important tem-
\par
poral variation in biodiversity states, estimating dynamic
\par
benchmarks reduces the amount of data underpinning a
\par
given benchmark, with consequences for the precision of
\par
benchmark estimates and the selection of benchmark
\par
quantiles. Knowledge of ecological dynamics can inform
\par
the choice of dynamic or static benchmarks. For exam-
\par
ple, one would expect substantial variation in biodiver-
\par
sity states in regions that experience large fluctuations in
\par
water availability or high levels of seasonal variation.
\par
Such associations were apparent in estimated bench-
\par
marks, where dynamic benchmarks diverged from static
\par
benchmarks in spring and autumn in a highly seasonal
\par
system (Riverina Floodplain Woodlands; Fig. 4a, b)
\par
and in dry and wet years in a system with highly variable
\par
rainfall (Nandewar Dry Sclerophyll Forests; Fig. 4c, d),
\par
but not in a more stable system (Sydney Basin Coastal
\par
Woodlands; Fig. 4e, f). These differences highlight situa-
\par
tions where dynamic benchmarks might be preferred
\par
over static benchmarks, following which the choice and
\par
implementation of dynamic or static benchmarks can be
\par
guided by data availability and knowledge of a specific
\par
benchmark application.
\par
Benefits of variable, data-driven benchmarks
\par
Our Bayesian modeling approach is easily updated,
\par
allowing benchmarks to be refined as new data become
\par
available. For example, we observed interannual varia-
\par
tion in species richness and cover of grasses and forbs,
\par
which suggests that benchmarks for these growth forms
\par
might need updating to capture long-term climatic
\par
trends (e.g., warming and drying climates; Parmesan
\par
and Yohe 2003). Existing benchmarks can be used to
\par
define prior distributions and newly collected data can
\par
be used to update benchmark estimates (Ellison 2004).
\par
This approach would substantially reduce the subse-
\par
quent computational demands of our modeling
\par
approach because the large data set used initially
\par
(\textasciitilde 35,000 observations for each of six growth forms) can
\par
be reduced to a relatively small set of prior distributions
\par
(\textasciitilde 5,000 parameters). An important consideration when
\par
collecting new data to update existing benchmarks is the
\par
additional information gain from new data versus the
\par
cost of collecting such data (Canessa et al. 2015).
\par
A focus on data-driven benchmarks that reflect con-
\par
temporary biodiversity states supports transparent and
\par
updateable benchmarks, which we believe will have
\par
broad application to conservation planning and manage-
\par
ment. Whereas benchmarks based on concepts of mini-
\par
mal disturbance emphasize divergences from idealized
\par
biodiversity states, our approach highlights contempo-
\par
rary states and existing data. The use of transparent
\par
benchmarks, linked to empirical data, is particularly
\par
beneficial in policy settings, where contentious decisions
\par
are best supported by defensible benchmarks that reflect
\par
empirical observations. The models outlined here were
\par
used to deliver benchmarks to the Biodiversity Assess-
\par
ment Method, which operates under the New South
\par
Wales Biodiversity Conservation Act 2016 (Office of
\par
Environment and Heritage 2018). Field testing of these
\par
benchmarks in real world applications, such as assessing
\par
biodiversity values to support biodiversity management,
\par
will provide valuable feedback on the validity of the esti-
\par
mated benchmarks and on key details of implementa-
\par
tion, such as the identification of appropriate quantiles
\par
to define best-on-offer reference states. We believe our
\par
approach is general and could be used broadly to calcu-
\par
late transparent and updateable biodiversity targets in
\par
dynamic environments, especially given unprecedented
\par
and global access to`big data' such as floristic invento-
\par
ries stored in data warehouses (Franklin et al. 2017, Bru-
\par
elheide et al. 2019).
