\par
Irrigation plays a signi cant role in achieving food and nutrition security in dry regions. However, detailed ex-
\par
ante appraisals of irrigation development investments are required to ef ciently allocate resources and optimize
\par
returns on investment. Due to the inherent system complexity and uncertain consequences of irrigation develop-
\par
ment interventions coupled with limited data availability, deterministic cost-bene t analysis can be ineffective in
\par
guiding formal decision-making. Stochastic Impact Evaluation (SIE) helps to overcome the challenges of evaluat-
\par
ing investments in such contexts. In this paper, we applied SIE to assess the viability of an irrigation dam con-
\par
struction project in northern Ethiopia. We used expert knowledge elicitation to generate a causal model of the
\par
planned intervention's impact pathway, including all identi ed bene ts, costs and risks. Estimates of the input
\par
variables were collected from ten subject matter experts. We then applied the SIE tools: Monte Carlo simulation,
\par
Partial Least Squares regression, and Value of Information analysis to project prospective impacts of the project
\par
and identify critical knowledge gaps. Model results indicate that the proposed irrigation dam project is highly
\par
likely to increase the overall bene ts and improve food and nutrition status of local farmers. However, the overall
\par
value of these bene ts is unlikely to exceed the sum of the investment costs and negative externalities involved in
\par
the intervention. Simulation results suggest that the planned irrigation dam may improve income, as well as food
\par
and nutrition security, but would generate negative environmental effects and high investment costs. The
