1. Introduction
\par
1.1. Background
\par
Many African farmers are poor, vulnerable and marginalized (UN,
\par
2015). Land degradation and frequent extreme weather events (e.g.
\par
droughts and oods) are among the many causes of low agricultural
\par
productivity, poverty and food insecurity on the continent (Brown
\par
et al., 2011; Lewis, 2017; Shiferaw et al., 2014; Solh and Van Ginkel,
\par
2014). Understanding local social, economic, and ecological systems
\par
will be necessary for increasing land productivity to meet food security
\par
needs and increase future resilience of agricultural systems in the region
\par
(Rosenstock et al., 2014; Solh and Van Ginkel, 2014). Irrigation is one
\par
option for improving national food and nutrition security and to in-
\par
crease farmers' resilience to climate change, especially in arid regions
\par
(Calzadilla et al., 2013; Gebregziabher et al., 2009; Hanjra et al., 2009a,
\par
2009b; Namara et al., 2010; Wossen et al., 2014). Effective agricultural
\par
water management also plays a considerable role in enhancing agricul-
\par
tural production and productivity. This in turn contributes to reducing
\par
poverty, stabilizing household income, and improving the nutritional
\par
and health status of the community (Gebregziabher et al., 2009;
\par
Hagos et al., 2009; Namara et al., 2010).
\par
In Africa, the dominant farming strategy is subsistence-oriented
\par
rainfed agriculture characterized by low-input/low-output practices
\par
(AGRA, 2014). Expansion of agricultural irrigation has been proposed
\par
as a potential strategy for reducing food insecurity (Xie et al., 2014;
\par
You et al., 2011) and increasing household income (Gebregziabher
\par
et al., 2009; Hagos et al., 2009; Hanjra et al., 2009b, 2009a). Despite
\par
the potential bene ts of irrigation in Africa, only around 7\% (\textasciitilde 15 million
\par
hectares) of the continent's total arable land is equipped for irrigation,
\par
far below the global average of 23.4\% (FAOSTAT, 2014). In recent de-
\par
cades, irrigation areas have been expanding at an average rate of 2.3\%
\par
annually (You et al., 2011), and they are expected to expand by around
\par
24 million hectares over the next ve decades (You et al., 2011). Xie
\par
et al. (2014) estimated that in Sub-Saharan Africa, 30, 24, 22, and 20
\par
million hectares of irrigable area could be developed thorugh motorized
\par
water pumps, treadle pumps, small reservoirs, and communal diver-
\par
sions, respectively.
\par
In Ethiopia, 5\% of the total irrigation potential is utilized
\par
(Awulachew et al., 2007; Xie et al., 2014; You et al., 2011). This is due
\par
to underperformace of operational irrigation schemes, resource con-
\par
straints, poor rural infrastructure, limited governmental commitment
\par
to the sector, lack of integration between different governmental sectors
\par
or stakeholders that share common irrigation development objectives,
\par
and a shortage of planned irrigation development projects (i.e. both in
\par
number of projects and targeted irrigable area) (Awulachew et al.,
\par
2010; You et al., 2011). Cognizant of the potential, the Ethiopian govern-
\par
ment is considering expansion of irrigation as a priority investment to
\par
reduce food insecurity and poverty (Awulachew et al., 2007; Hagos
\par
et al., 2009; MoARD, 2010).
\par
Irrigation potential can be expanded by investing in large-scale
\par
dams and/or small-scale irrigation developments (You et al., 2011).
\par
Dam-based irrigation expansion is a common practice in Ethiopia. For
\par
example, over the last two decades, about 100 dams were constructed
\par
in the Tigray region, with more than three quarters of the structures
\par
dedicated exclusively to irrigation (Berhane et al., 2016). However,
