\par
researchers disagree which kind of irrigation development delivers the
\par
highest return on investment. Some expect greater impacts on food se-
\par
curity from dams, since they allow establishment of large cropping
\par
areas (e.g. Strobl and Strobl, 2011), while others have argued that
\par
small-scale structures are more effective and sustainable, as well as
\par
less harmful to the environment (e.g. Dile et al., 2013; You et al., 2011).
\par
1.2. Stochastic impact evaluation of irrigation development projects
\par
Agricultural development intervention decisions are mostly based
\par
on perceived economic and technical viability (e.g. Balana et al., 2012;
\par
Dadaser-Celik et al., 2009), yet they have social, political, and environ-
\par
mental impacts (Luedeling et al., 2015). In addition, the complex nature
\par
of agricultural development interventions such as irrigation dams and
\par
their long-term consequences (Kalra et al., 2014), coupled with uncer-
\par
tainty and variability of important parameters, limits decision-makers'
\par
ability to clearly anticipate project impacts (Li et al., 2016; Xie et al.,
\par
2014; You et al., 2011). Furthermore, in developing regions such as
\par
Sub-Saharan Africa, data scarcity limits decision makers' ability to
\par
make informed decisions (Luedeling et al., 2015; Luedeling and
\par
Shepherd, 2016; Shepherd et al., 2015). The uncertainties and variabil-
\par
ities in an irrigation dam range from the hydrological and agronomic pa-
\par
rameters to socioeconomic, environmental and social dimensions
\par
(Graveline et al., 2012; Li et al., 2016). These uncertainties and variabil-
\par
ities are rarely considered in development project evaluations
\par
(Luedeling et al., 2015; Rosenstock et al., 2014), but they substantially
\par
limit the usefulness of the commonly used approach of deterministic
\par
cost-bene t analysis, which cannot easily handle imprecise information
\par
(Luedeling et al., 2015; Luedeling and Shepherd, 2016). Failure to com-
\par
prehensively consider all project outcomes and their inherent uncer-
\par
tainty and variability can jeopardize project success or lead to
\par
undesirable outcomes (Luedeling et al., 2015; Rosenstock et al., 2014).
\par
Therefore, a detailed ex-ante appraisal is required to allocate resources
\par
ef ciently and to optimize the returns of agricultural development
\par
investments.
\par
Stochastic Impact Evaluation (SIE) is an ex-ante appraisal approach
\par
that allows integration of different impact dimensions by considering
\par
the intervention as a business case and by capturing the variability
\par
and uncertainty of all input variables (Luedeling et al., 2015; Luedeling
\par
and Shepherd, 2016). SIE is a probabilistic decision modeling approach
\par
(Wafula et al., 2018), which is suitable for evaluating the outcomes of
\par
development projects, even when data availability is limited (cf.
\par
Luedeling et al., 2015; Rosenstock et al., 2014; Whitney et al., 2017).
\par
However, despite its potential, it has rarely been applied to agricultural
\par
development interventions (Luedeling et al., 2015). We applied the de-
\par
cision analysis tools Monte Carlo simulation, Partial Least square regres-
\par
sion, and Value of Information analysis (Luedeling et al., 2015; Whitney
\par
et al., 2017) to project the outcome of the proposed intervention, iden-
\par
tify the sensitive variables that in uence the distribution of the outcome
\par
and to identify critical knowledge gaps.
\par
Our aim was to apply SIE to assess the viability of an irrigation devel-
\par
opment project in northern Ethiopia in terms of monetized overall ben-
\par
e ts, as well as food and nutrition security indicators for all major
\par
stakeholder groups and the environment both with and without catch-
\par
ment restoration practices. We use the value of information concept to
\par
identify critical uncertainties that strongly in uence the model and may
\par
be targeted for further decision-supporting research (Luedeling et al.,
\par
2015; Whitney et al., 2017).
\par
2. Methods
\par
2.1. The study area
\par
Our study area is located in the Tigray region of Ethiopia, where we
\par
investigated the regional government's decision to build an irrigation
\par
dam at Ebo village, in the Raya-Azebo Wereda (district) of Southern
\par
Tigray. The proposed dam construction site is located at 39.63{$^\circ$} to
\par
39.66{$^\circ$} E and 12.86{$^\circ$} to 12.89{$^\circ$} N (Fig. 1). The planned dam is expected
\par
to be 35 m high and 6 m thick, with a crest length of 700 m. It is expected
\par
to hold up to 6.3 million cubic meters of water and irrigate around
\par
405 ha of farmland (Tesfay, 2017). Considering the average size of
\par
land holdings in the study area (0.5 to 0.6 ha; Yazew et al., 2014), the
\par
dam could support an irrigation scheme large enough to provide
\par
water to about 675 to 810 farmers.
\par
Fig. 2. Causal impact pathway model representing the structure of the decision to implement an irrigation dam project in Tigray, Ethiopia. The model represents important costs, bene and risks identi ed by local experts. It is structured as an impact pathway from the decision to build the dam (top) through all estimated and risk-adjusted costs and bene ts to the model outputs in the form of Net Present Value (NPV).
\par
