\par
The study area is semi-arid with a bimodal rainfall distribution, char-
\par
acterized by a short rainy season between March and April and a long
\par
rainy season from July to September (Hagos, 2010). The altitude of the
\par
study area ranges from 1370 m in the lowlands up to 3600 m a.s.l. on
\par
the region's mountain peaks and plateaus (Fig. 1). Due to these differ-
\par
ences in elevation, mean annual rainfall ranges from 350 mm in the
\par
lowlands to 800 m in mountainous areas (Hagos, 2010; Haile et al.,
\par
2013; Yazew et al., 2014). The study region has no permanent rivers,
\par
so the proposed reservoir will rely on intermittent oods created by
\par
heavy rains in the catchment area.
\par
The majority of the local population relies on crop and livestock
\par
farming for their livelihoods (Hagos, 2010; Yazew et al., 2014). Raya
\par
Valley, which includes the proposed dam area, is considered a high-
\par
potential location for irrigation expansion by the regional government
\par
(Hagos, 2010; Haile et al., 2013; Yazew et al., 2014).
\par
2.2. Participatory model development and parametrization
\par
To develop and parametrize a model of the dam construction deci-
\par
sion, we followed a decision analysis approach (Luedeling et al.,
\par
2015). The approach seeks to actively involve local experts in develop-
\par
ment of both an impact pathway and a nal decision model. In this
\par
case, experts refers to local knowledge holders with in-depth under-
\par
standing of the local context and the potential bene ts, costs and risks
\par
of dam construction.
\par
Model development began with an interview with the head of the
\par
region's irrigation development program, the of cial who would be
\par
the coordinator of the intended project. This coordinator helped to iden-
\par
tify ten experts with knowledge of similar local systems: two experts in
\par
engineering projects (hydraulic and design), one in socioeconomic sys-
\par
tems, two in agronomic systems, three in watershed and environmental
\par
management, and two in water supply systems. These ten experts were
\par
asked to consider the dam construction project decision and identify the
\par
main effects of the proposed project, which they grouped into factors of
\par
relevance to the local community (hereafter referred to as stake-
\par
holders), the environment, and the implementer. Based on location
\par
and access to the irrigation scheme (i.e. land with or without access to
\par
water from the irrigation project), the stakeholders were further classi-
\par
ed into four groups: (i) downstream irrigators (farmers who would
\par
use the water from the dam for agriculture in an irrigation scheme),
\par
(ii) downstream non-irrigators (farmers downstream who could be af-
\par
fected by the dam but do not have access to the irrigation water), (iii)
\par
displaced farmers (those who currently live in the dam construction
\par
area but will need to be relocated and compensated, both nancially
\par
and with a parcel of land in the irrigation scheme), and (iv) upstream
\par
non-irrigators (those who live upstream of the dam, close enough to ac-
\par
cess water for domestic use and/or livestock consumption).
\par
We generated a preliminary qualitative impact pathway model
\par
based on local expert knowledge, including important costs, bene ts,
\par
and risks of the project for the stakeholders, the implementer and the
\par
environment. This preliminary model was then shared with the experts
\par
for review and re ned based on their feedback. Moreover, the identi ed
\par
environmental impact was further re ned based on existing literature.
\par
We consolidated all inputs into a nal model capturing the impact path-
\par
way of the proposed intervention, which re ected the current state of
\par
understanding and knowledge of all experts (Luedeling et al., 2015).
\par
Experts provided quantitative estimates for all model variables cov-
\par
ering the bene ts, costs and risks of the dam construction. We used an
\par
expert knowledge elicitation approach (Luedeling et al., 2015; Tamene
\par
et al., 2011; Whitney et al., 2018, 2017) to collect estimates for model
\par
variables. All experts were familiarized with estimation techniques to
\par
overcome bias, allowing them to provide quantitative estimates in the
\par
form of distributions representing their subjective 90\% con dence inter-
\par
vals for model variables (Luedeling et al., 2015; Rosenstock et al., 2014;
\par
Wafula et al., 2018). Initial monetary estimates were collected in
\par
Ethiopian currency (i.e. Birr) and converted to USD at the Ethiopian Na-
\par
tional Bank's October 10, 2017 rate of \$1 = 23.3908 ETB (NBE, 2017).
\par
The resulting model was scripted in the R programming language (R
\par
Core Team, 2018) and run 10,000 times as a Monte Carlo simulation to
\par
randomly select values from de ned distributions for all input variables
\par
(Arnold and Yildiz, 2015) using the decisionSupport package
