\par
(Luedeling and G\"ohring, 2018). The intervention was modeled in two
\par
ways: 1) the dam construction is complemented with catchment resto-
\par
ration; 2) the dam construction is implemented without catchment res-
\par
toration. The model was used to provide forecasts of the costs, bene ts
\par
and risks of the irrigation project for each stakeholder, the implementer,
\par
and the environment. The forecasted marginal bene ts/losses were
\par
discounted according to an estimated discount rate to compute the
\par
monetized net bene t, expressed as net present value (NPV),
\par
representing the sum of discounted projected net bene ts over the ex-
\par
pected life span of the project (Luedeling et al., 2015). More information
\par
about the computation of the marginal bene ts/losses is provided in the
\par
supplementary material (Yigzaw et al., in press). In order to guide the
\par
decision, we also computed the total project outcome (i.e. the sum of
\par
NPVs for all stakeholders, the implementer and the environment).
\par
2.2.1. Nutrition analysis
\par
We used the model to forecast the additional total energy and pro-
\par
vitamin A that may become available for local people (cf. Whitney
\par
et al., 2017). Energy and pro-vitamin A outcomes were expressed ac-
\par
cording to the number of people whose annual needs could be covered
\par
by the additional agricultural production expected within the proposed
\par
irrigation scheme. These were computed by dividing the total energy
\par
and pro-vitamin A by the annual per capita requirements. Values for en-
\par
ergy (in Kcal) and pro-vitamin A content (in mcg Retinol equivalent)
\par
per ton of yield as well as the per capita requirements of these measures
\par
were obtained from Whitney et al. (2017). Note that these calculations
\par
only consider the gross nutrient production, without accounting for fac-
\par
tors such as food distribution or access.
\par
2.2.2. Sensitivity analysis
\par
Sensitive parameters within the model were identi ed by applying
\par
Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression (Luedeling et al., 2015; Wold
\par
et al., 1993), in which the NPV for each dimension of the model outcome
\par
was regressed against latent factors consisting of linear combinations of
\par
the input variables (Luedeling and Gassner, 2012). The selection of the
\par
most sensitive variables was done based on variable importance in the
\par
projection (VIP) calculation (Farr\'es et al., 2015; Wold et al., 1993). The
\par
VIP score measures the in uence of an individual variable on the NPV
\par
of the proposed intervention (Akarachantachote et al., 2014; Farr\'es
\par
et al., 2015). Variables with a VIP score above 0.8 (Luedeling et al.,
\par
2015) were considered as in uential variables. For these variables, we
\par
also determined whether they had a positive or negative in uence on
\par
project outcomes.
\par
2.2.3. Value of information analysis
\par
We identi ed the variables with highest value of information (i.e.
\par
variables that most in uence the sign of the project outcome) using
\par
Value of Information (VoI) analysis (Tuffaha et al., 2016). These are
\par
the variables for which further measurement (reduction of uncertainty)
\par
could improve certainty about whether model outcomes are positive or
\par
negative (Luedeling et al., 2015). We used the expected value of perfect
\par
information (EVPI) procedure described by Wafula et al. (2018), to cal-
\par
culate the monetary value of additional information for the decision-
\par
making process. EVPI indicates the maximum value that a decision
\par
maker should be willing to pay in order to reduce uncertainty about
\par
which decision alternative promises the greatest returns (Whitney
\par
et al., 2017).
\par
3. Results
\par
Expert inputs were consolidated into a decision model with 175 var-
\par
iables (i.e. estimates and distributions of the bene ts, costs and risks of
\par
the proposed intervention and some other nutritional and technical
\par
model parameters), which illustrated the expected impact pathway
\par
for the dam intervention (overview in Fig. 2). The experts identi ed
\par
nine bene ts, fourteen costs, and ten risks of the proposed dam inter-
\par
vention, when it is complemented with catchment restoration
\par
(Table 1). The plausible cost and bene t components for each of the
\par
stakeholders, the implementer, and the environment are illustrated in
\par
the supplementary material (Table S1) (Yigzaw et al., in press). Descrip-
\par
tions of all input variables used in the study and their estimates are pro-
\par
vided in the Data in Brief journal (Yigzaw et al., in press).
