Experts identi ed irrigation as the primary purpose of the dam, in-
\par
cluding fully irrigated areas, as well as rainfed areas that may bene t
\par
from supplemental irrigation during dry spells. The dam is also ex-
\par
pected to create employment for the local community. The irrigation
\par
area will be planted with fruit trees, vegetables, groundnuts, and
\par
maize with cereal crops grown intensively in the rainy season (except
\par
in the area under fruit trees).
\par
The proposed irrigation dam construction intervention will create
\par
both positive and negative secondary effects, and the impact depends
\par
on whether the dam is constructed with or without catchment restora-
\par
tion. The positive environmental effects (i.e. bene ts) are the ecosystem
\par
services obtained from an increase in vegetation cover around the
\par
dam and within the catchment area (i.e. if dam construction is
\par
complemented with catchment restoration). Environmental costs are
\par
ecosystem services that will no longer accrue because of the proposed
\par
intervention. These environmental costs arise from reduction of vegeta-
\par
tion cover further downstream from the dam (i.e. land degradation) and
\par
from ecosystem services currently produced by the area that will be
\par
submerged. The environmental impact of the intervention is thus com-
\par
puted based on the ecosystem value of the change in vegetation cover,
\par
which is composed of provisioning services (e.g. production of grass,
\par
fuelwood, and medicinal plants), regulating and maintenance services
\par
(e.g. improved microclimate, nutrient cycling, erosion control and sedi-
\par
ment retention, carbon sequestration and habitat conservation), and
\par
cultural services.
\par
The implementer of the project (i.e. the government) will cover the
\par
costs of constructing the dam and the canals, managing the catchment
\par
area through construction of soil conservation structures, reforestation,
\par
and area exclosure, and providing repair and maintenance. The resi-
\par
dents of one village, called Mahgo, would need to be relocated because
\par
their homes are within the ooded area. The implementer will compen-
\par
sate the farmers for the loss of their homes and farming plots and after
\par
completion of the dam, the farmers will receive a plot within the pro-
\par
posed irrigation scheme. We modeled the net value (i.e. the difference
\par
between the compensation and the value farmers can generate without
\par
the dam) of the compensation as income.
\par
