4. Discussion
\par
The proposed dam construction intervention affects the composition
\par
and con guration of the landscape, which in return in uences the local
\par
biodiversity, ecosystem functions, and the provisioning of ecosystem
\par
services (Bai et al., 2018). In Tigray, sedimentation is one of the main
\par
problems that in uence the effectiveness of an irrigation dam
\par
(Berhane et al., 2016). Sedimentation could be strongly reduced
\par
through restoration of degraded catchment area (Alemayehu et al.,
\par
2009; Balana et al., 2012; Mekuria et al., 2011). Thus, we considered
\par
catchment restoration as a way of complementing the intervention
\par
and evaluated the outcomes with and without catchment restoration.
\par
4.1. Projected outcomes of the dam intervention
\par
In general, the proposed project is expected to have a positive NPV
\par
for all stakeholders in the community, but the high investment cost of
\par
the structure leads to negative total project outcomes. The negative pro-
\par
ject outcome could be reduced by complementing the intervention with
\par
catchment restoration, but the overall prospects remain negative even
\par
then. Thus, based on overall cost-bene t considerations, construction
\par
of an irrigation dam in the study area is not advisable. This supports re-
\par
sults by Petheram et al. (2016) in a semi-arid tropical catchment of
\par
northern Australia, who found that construction of water storage to irri-
\par
gate perennials as well as year-round cropping was prohibitively
\par
N. Yigzaw et al. / Science of the Total Environment 685 (2019) 1209 1220
\par
expensive and generated low return on investment. Manikowski and
\par
Strapasson (2016), also found a negative NPV for a large irrigation
\par
dam in the Senegal River Valley in Senegal.
\par
Net bene ts were greatest for downstream irrigators, followed by
\par
displaced farmers, upstream non-irrigators, and downstream non-
\par
irrigators. Farmers with access to the proposed irrigation scheme
\par
bene ted most. As with past studies, our ndings show that the bene ts
\par
from irrigation are mostly attributed to increasing numbers of crop
\par
harvests per year and reducing crop failure during dry spells
\par
(Gebregziabher et al., 2009; Hagos et al., 2009; Mengistie and Kidane,
\par
2016). Among farmers who do not have access to irrigation, upstream
\par
non-irrigators are expected to bene t more from the proposed project
\par
than downstream stakeholders. A major reason for this is that develop-
\par
ment of the dam may jeopardize the option of downstream non-
\par
irrigators to reduce the incidence of crop failure by diverting ash oods
\par
into their farming plots (i.e. spate irrigation), which is a common prac-
\par
tice in the study area (Haile et al., 2013; Yazew et al., 2014). Upstream
\par
villagers will save signi cant amounts of time by harvesting water
\par
from the dam for domestic and livestock consumption, because in the
\par
absence of the dam, they have to travel long distances to fetch water.
\par
In general, the use of the dam for either irrigation or domestic and live-
\par
stock consumption is bene cial. The magnitude of the bene t is related
\par
to the degree of extraction (i.e. those who extract most, in this case
\par
downstream irrigators, who have the largest share of irrigable area in
\par
the scheme, stand to bene t most).
\par
The proposed intervention is expected to lead to land use and cover
\par
changes, which in return will affect the provisioning of ecosystem ser-
\par
vices. Thus, the environmental impact of the proposed intervention is
