\par
the net change in the ecosystem services. Comparing the environmental
\par
costs and bene ts, the cost of the proposed dam (i.e. without catchment
\par
restoration) outweighs the bene ts. This is mainly due to the loss of
\par
vegetation, such as shrubs, wetlands, and forest areas further down-
\par
stream, caused by retaining water in the dam (Amdihun, 2008;
\par
Manikowski and Strapasson, 2016). The value of ecosystem services
\par
provided by the area expected to become submerged is likely to out-
\par
weigh additional provisioning of ecosystem services from the newly
\par
established land use system. However, when the dam construction in-
\par
tervention is complemented with catchment restoration, the environ-
\par
mental bene ts exceed the costs.
\par
The implementer may also incur losses from the proposed project,
\par
but since this implementer is the government, generating pro ts may
\par
not be a primary objective. However, the government might bene t
\par
from the political stability, poverty reduction, and other human devel-
\par
opment outcomes created in the area, which we did not monetize. Con-
\par
sidering these effects, which should be highly desirable for a
\par
government serving the public interest, could improve the total project
\par
outcome.
\par
The proposed irrigation project has the potential to improve food
\par
and nutrition security, both of which are highly associated with low ag-
\par
ricultural productivity and dependency of smallholder farmers on
\par
rainfed production in which small weather shocks can have signi cant
\par
effects (Amede et al., 2008; Hagos et al., 2012; Smajgl et al., 2016).
\par
Model results concur with other studies showing that access to irriga-
\par
tion can be key to reducing food insecurity by increasing agricultural
\par
productivity and stabilizing farming outputs in the face of climate
\par
change and weather variability (Burney and Naylor, 2012; Clothier
\par
et al., 2010; Dile et al., 2013; Gebregziabher et al., 2009; Hanjra et al.,
\par
2009b; Namara et al., 2010; Ringler et al., 2016). In this study, we con-
\par
sidered only the additional energy and pro-vitamin A produced from
\par
the dry season irrigation together with the damage prevention during
\par
dry spells. However, households may also supplement and improve
\par
their diet using on-farm employment income related to the dam inter-
\par
vention, which could in turn improve the food and nutrition outcomes
\par
of the project.
\par
4.2. Sensitive variables
\par
4.2.1. Variables related to the cost of construction
\par
The construction-related variables (i.e. the initial estimated cost of
\par
constructing the structure, the likelihood and rate at which construction
\par
cost increases, and delay in the completion of the structure) were nega-
\par
tively correlated with the total project NPV. This is in line with the global
\par
assessment of large dams report by the World Commission on Dams, in
\par
which the high construction cost of large dams and additional cost over-
\par
runs incurred by construction delays were mentioned as raising the
\par
likelihood of negative project outcomes (WCD, 2000). Increases in a sin-
\par
gle or combination of all these variables reduce the overall project out-
\par
comes. This suggests that it may not be advisable to invest in areas
\par
where the investment cost is high and delays can easily occur.
\par
4.2.2. Variables related to farm revenue
\par
The price and maximum yield of vegetables, maximum irrigable area
\par
under the scheme and the fruit price are positively associated with the
\par
total project outcome. This complements the results of Hagos et al.
\par
(2009) and Amede et al. (2008), who reported that the national econ-
\par
omy is more strongly impacted by changes in the prices of irrigated
\par
