\par
vegetables and fruits than by the same changes in the price of other
\par
crops. While an increase in fruit price increases the expected NPV,
\par
greater number of years until rst fruit harvest and greater area allo-
\par
cated to fruit reduces the total project outcome. This suggests that irri-
\par
gation development interventions should target high-yielding
\par
vegetable farming.
\par
4.2.3. Variables related to reservoir capacity and water use
\par
Irrigation inefficiency in uenced the distribution of the overall pro-
\par
ject outcome negatively. The water loss due to inef ciency varies with
\par
the irrigation technology or method used (Asres, 2016; Brouwer et al.,
\par
1989). The proposed intervention relies on a furrow irrigation system,
\par
which is relatively inef cient. Adoption of more advanced irrigation
\par
methods could improve water use ef ciency (Ki e et al., 2017), which
\par
could in turn allow increasing the irrigated area. It is important, how-
\par
ever, to ensure adequate drainage to prevent salinity build-up, as well
\par
as suf cient groundwater recharge to avoid undesirable downstream
\par
consequences. These considerations are beyond the scope of this
\par
study, but should be factored into decisions on which irrigation tech-
\par
nique to adopt.
\par
Reservoir capacity was positively associated with total project out-
\par
come, with an increase in this variable resulting in a greater command
\par
area, which in turn increases the irrigation revenue. The importance of
\par
this parameter is underscored by ndings from a large irrigation dam
\par
at Kogo, Ethiopia, where the irrigable area was reduced by almost 20\%
\par
compared to initial expectations due to a reduction in reservoir volume
\par
(Asres, 2016). Therefore, irrigation development interventions should
\par
consider the uncertainty in the reservoir's capacity and irrigation inef -
\par
ciency to accurately anticipate irrigation bene ts.
\par
4.2.4. Variables related to employment and relocation
\par
Irrigation practices are often labor-intensive (Gebregziabher et al.,
\par
2009), which implies that they can generate employment. The total
\par
amount of labor created (quanti ed in man-days per hectare in this
\par
study) positively in uences the total project outcome. While this gener-
\par
ates costs for the irrigators, the income generated by such employment
\par
could improve the social wellbeing of the non-farming members of the
\par
community.
\par
Irrigation dams often lead to the displacement of farmers who previ-
\par
ously cultivated the area that is ooded. The government already nego-
\par
tiated the issue of displacement, and the farmers agreed to be relocated
\par
to areas with better infrastructure. The government will pay compensa-
\par
tion for the houses destroyed and farming plots inundated. Even though
\par
this compensation is vital to overcome some livelihood problems, loss of
\par
homes and social bonds have negative implications. Considering such
\par
costs reduces the total project outcome. Therefore, the net bene ts of
\par
an irrigation dam project could be improved by selecting an appropriate
\par
location that leads to displacement of fewer farmers.
\par
4.3. Methodological limitation
\par
The development of the impact pathway model was done by the ex-
\par
perts, and there is no guarantee that all important aspects of the pro-
\par
posed intervention and their estimates are accurately captured
\par
(Luedeling et al., 2015). In addition, the ecosystem services obtained
\par
from the intervention are considered only for the expected life of the
\par
dam (i.e. 30 years, including construction), yet the services could be
\par
provided for more than the expected life of the dam, which might lead
\par
to positive project outcome.
\par
