\par
The ndings of this study revealed that investment in an irrigation
\par
dam could improve household incomes, as well as food and nutrition se-
\par
curity, albeit at the cost of environmental externalities and high invest-
\par
ment costs. However, complementing the dam construction
\par
intervention with catchment restoration could reduce the expected
\par
project losses, because the ecosystem value obtained from catchment
\par
restoration is enough to cover some of the costs of the intervention.
\par
Thus, the dam construction intervention should be complemented
\par
with the restoration of degraded catchment areas. Moreover, the invest-
\par
ment outcomes can be improved through reduction of water losses due
\par
to inef ciency, targeting high-value crops, and controlling construction
\par
cost overruns. Furthermore, the study revealed that the social and cul-
\par
tural costs to the displaced farmers and the wider impact on farmers
\par
further downstream, which are often neglected during impact assess-
\par
ments, should be considered in irrigation development interventions.
\par
The study demonstrates that a decision analysis approach can help
\par
overcome some of the weaknesses of commonly used deterministic,
\par
ex-ante project evaluation techniques (i.e. cost-bene t analysis) by
\par
thoroughly capturing all relevant impact dimensions and their inherent
\par
uncertainties, addressing system complexity, and confronting data scar-
\par
city. The approaches demonstrated in this study can support decision
\par
making for agricultural development investments and provide guidance
\par
on resource allocation.
