\par
5. Conclusion
\par
We evaluated an irrigation dam construction project in the Tigray
\par
region of Ethiopia by capturing the variability and uncertainty of impor-
\par
tant decision-relevant variables. Model results suggested that the pro-
\par
posed irrigation dam construction bene ts all the community, in
\par
terms of both overall monetized bene ts and improved food and nutri-
\par
tion status. The magnitude of the net bene t was positively correlated
\par
with the degree of water extraction, which was highest for downstream
\par
irrigators, followed by displaced farmers, upstream non-irrigators, and
\par
downstream non-irrigators. However, the proposed investment is ex-
\par
pected to have negative impacts on the environment and to generate
\par
high costs to the funder of the project, which exceed the total bene ts
\par
across all stakeholders. Complementing the dam construction interven-
\par
tion with catchment restoration generates positive environmental im-
\par
pacts, improves the household income, and reduces the expected
\par
project loss, though the expected overall outcomes remain negative.
\par
Overall net bene ts of the project are thus almost certainly negative,
\par
so that construction of the dam in the study area at this time should
\par
not be recommended.
\par
Model results suggested that the variables which most in uenced
\par
overall project outcomes were the high construction costs, the reservoir
\par
capacity, water use, fruit and vegetable price, employment creation, social
\par
and cultural value of displacement, the ecosystem value obtained from re-
\par
stored catchment area, and the farmers' discount rate. Value of informa-
\par
tion results illustrate high values for social and cultural costs of
\par
displacement and for the bene ts foregone for those downstream
\par
when water is held in reservoirs. However, measuring these parameters
\par
is unlikely to change the emerging recommendation. Thus, based on the
\par
current model, no further research is needed for providing decision ad-
\par
vice to the implementer.
\par
Through the example of evaluation of the Tigray dam intervention,
\par
the study demonstrates the applicability of SIE to guide decision-
\par
making for agricultural development interventions where little data is
\par
available, and a lot of uncertainty exists.
