\par
Methods: Patients with relapsed or refractory MF/SS with \$1 prior therapy were
\par
randomised to mogamulizumab (1.0 mg/kg weekly for a 28 day cycle, then on days 1
\par
and 15 of subsequent cycles) or vorinostat (400 mg daily). Vorinostat patients with
\par
con rmed disease progression or intolerable toxicity following at least two treat-
\par
ment cycles could cross over to mogamulizumab treatment. Time to next treatment
\par
((TTNT) de ned as time from randomisation to date of rst new systemic therapy),
\par
overall response rate ((ORR) de ned as rate of con rmed complete response and
\par
partial response), and progression-free survival (PFS) were examined. PFS was based
\par
on Kaplan-Meier estimates, and hazard ratios on the Cox proportional hazards
\par
model. Results: In total 287 stage IIB+ MF and SS patients were included (moga-
\par
mulizumab, IIB n=32, IIIA n=9, IIIB n=13, IVA1 n=73, IVA2 n=19, IVB n=4; vorinostat,
\par
IIB n=23, IIIA n=9, IIIB n=7, IVA1 n=82, IVA2 n=12, IVB n=4). Baseline characteristics
\par
were similar between cohorts. Median TTNT was 11.00 months and 3.47 months for
\par
mogamulizumab and vorinostat, respectively (hazard ratio 0.36 [95\% CI, 0.27-0.48;
\par
P,0.0001]). ORR evaluated by investigator assessment was 30.0\% (45/150) and 2.9\%
\par
(4/137) for mogamulizumab and vorinostat, respectively (rate difference 27.1 [95\% CI,
\par
19.1-35.3; P,0.0001]). Median PFS was 9.40 months and 3.07 months for moga-
\par
mulizumab and vorinostat, respectively (hazard ratio 0.43 [95\% CI, 0.31-0.58;
\par
P,0.0001]). Conclusions: In this post-hoc analysis, mogamulizumab increased TTNT,
\par
ORR, and PFS in stage IIB+ MF and SS patients compared with vorinostat, demon-
\par
strating a clinical bene t for more advanced MF and SS patients.
