\par
NETWORK META-ANALYSIS COMPARING PALBOCICLIB
\par
PLUS FULVESTRANT WITH EVEROLIMUS PLUS
\par
EXEMESTANE FOR HR+/HER2- ADVANCED BREAST
\par
CANCER THAT HAS BECOME RESISTANT TO PREVIOUS
\par
ENDOCRINE THERAPY
\par
LeReun C,1 Gentilini A,2 Large S,3 Chowdhury M,3 Smith A,4 Rinciog C2
\par
1Independent, Sainte Anne, Guadeloupe, 2Symmetron Limited, London, UK,
\par
3Pfizer UK, Tadworth, UK, 4Pfizer Inc, New York, NY, USA
\par
Objectives: Fulvestrant (FUL) and everolimus plus exemestane (EVE+EXE) are com-
\par
mon therapies for advanced breast cancer resistant to prior endocrine therapy.
\par
PALOMA-3 found the new regimen palbociclib plus fulvestrant (PAL+FUL) was
\par
associated with clinically meaningful improvements in overall survival (OS) versus
\par
FUL, despite narrowly missing statistical signi cance. As no direct data are available
\par
for PAL+FUL versus EVE+EXE, this study's objective was to indirectly estimate
\par
comparative OS between PAL+FUL and EVE+EXE. Methods: A systematic literature
\par
review of clinical evidence was conducted. Two separate network meta-analyses
\par
(NMAs) were formed using a network of studies linking OS evidence from PAL+FUL
\par
to EVE+EXE: fractional polynomial (FP) methodology was used to capture a multi-
\par
dimensional treatment effect, and a proportional hazards (PH) NMA was carried out
\par
assuming constant treatment effects over time. The PH assumption was tested using
\par
cumulative log-log and Schoenfeld residual plots. Results: A linear network
\par
including ve studies was used. PH tests showed the assumption did not hold for OS
\par
in most studies in the network. In the PH-based NMA, PAL+FUL showed OS bene t
\par
over EVE+EXE with a median hazard ratio (HR) of 0.74 (95\% credible interval [CrI]:
\par
0.51, 1.09), not statistically signi cant. In the FP NMA, the median HRs decreased over
\par
time from 0.94 at 6 months (95\% CrI: 0.48, 1.89), to 0.90 at year 1 (95\% CrI: 0.53, 1.54),
\par
and 0.76 at year 5 (95\% CrI: 0.49, 1.20). The results were aligned with clinical trial
\par
data. Conclusions: PALOMA-3 was the rst trial to report OS for a CDK 4/6 inhibitor
\par
in this population. Using these data, the FP found PAL+FUL was associated with
\par
greater OS than EVE+EXE, ndings backed by the PH-based NMA. While the results
\par
were not statistically signi cant, this could be due to the clinical trial results, studies
\par
not powered to detect differences in OS, and between trial variance.
