\par
Background: In a single-payer setting,the payer aims to maximize the net-mone-
\par
tary-bene t(NMB)given a cost-effectiveness(CE) threshold,whilst the manufacturer
\par
aims to maximize the expected discounted-cash- ow(DCF)resulting from the sales of
\par
that technology. Managed entry agreements(MEAs) are tools that are used to
\par
improve access to expensive technologies that would otherwise not be deemed to be
\par
cost-effective to payers. While simple discount on the list-price is the most
\par
commonly applied MEA type,there are different forms,each having different impact
\par
on the cost-effectiveness of the technology,on the lifetime DCF-per-patient and on
\par
the decision uncertainty. We aim to analyze the sequential decision-making of
\par
different MEAs (i.e. simple discount,free treatment initiation,lifetime/cycle treatment
\par
acquisition cost-capping[LTTACC/CTACC],performance-based risk sharing
\par
[PBRSA],etc.)at the manufacturer and at the payer level,respectively. Methods: A UK-
\par
based cost-utility analysis using three-state,partitioned-survival-model was
\par
VALUE IN HEALTH - NOVEMBER 2019
\par
constructed to determine the cost-effectiveness of regorafenib versus best-sup-
\par
portive-care for the second-line treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma.The optimal
\par
agreement terms that would maximise the lifetime DCF-per-patient for each
\par
MEA,whilst remaining below the CE-threshold(\pounds 50,000/QALY gained)were obtained
\par
in the deterministic base-case.Robustness for each optimized MEA was then assessed
\par
using probabilistic sensitivity and scenario analyses,value of information(VoI),and
\par
HTA-risk analyses. Results: As expected,the introduction of all MEAs improved the
\par
probabilistic ICER and NMB values to (almost)acceptable levels,compared to``no-
\par
MEA''case(ICERw\pounds 78,000/QALY-gained).The expected DCFs across the explored
\par
