\par
competing models. However, substantial discretion remains for the analysts in
\par
selecting the parametric distributions while avoiding over tting the data. Our study
\par
characterises the impact of decision-impacting errors which result from reliance on
\par
these criteria alone within simulated trial data with varying degrees of follow-up. We
\par
de ned errors as selection of models that did not contain the true mean within the
\par
95\% con dence interval of the predicted mean. Methods: We simulated data with
\par
sample sizes comparable to phase 3 oncology trials (N = 300). Time-to-death was
\par
drawn randomly from prespeci ed distributions, and maximum follow-up period
\par
was varied, within the simulation, relative to the true median of the distribution.
