Maximum-likelihood models, assuming a range of potential distributions were tted
\par
to the data. Model selection was based upon minimum Akaike Information Criterion
\par
(AIC). Errors were measured relative to the underlying true distribution.
\par
Results: Increased follow-up resulted in decreased rates of error across all under-
\par
lying true distributions. Focusing on data derived from a Weibull distribution, the
\par
95\% CI for the mean of the minimal AIC model contained the true mean in
\par
approximately 50\% to 19\% of cases for follow-up times of 75\% to 200\% respectively of
\par
the median survival time. Decreasing error was associated with increasing shape
\par
parameter. Conclusions: This study demonstrates the challenges faced by analysts
\par
and decision-makers when relying on information criteria alone in the selection of
\par
parametric distributions for extrapolation of time-to-event outcomes in HTA.
