\par
parametric network meta-analysis (PNMA) is commonly used to assess their effec-
\par
tiveness compared to other therapies in the absence of head-to-head trials. However,
\par
NMA based on standard parametric curves does not adequately capture cure. The aim
\par
of this study is to explore three methods for extrapolating survival: PNMA, spline
\par
NMA, and a novel mixture-cure PNMA (MCPNMA) methodology. Methods: To
\par
compare the methodologies, two melanoma trials including potentially curative
\par
treatments were used; 1) nivolumab versus dacarbazine (Ascierto, 2018), and 2)
\par
ipilumumab + dacarbazine versus dacarbazine (Robert, 2011). Weibull distribution
\par
was tted on the trial data using the standard PNMA, spline NMA and MCPNMA
\par
methodologies. The survival extrapolations were compared in terms of t and mean
\par
(incremental) survival. Results: MCPNMA and spline NMA had better t over the
\par
trial data than PNMA. For dacarbazine, PNMA, spline NMA and MCPNMA resulted in
\par
mean survival of 2.01, 3.72, and 5.75 years, respectively. For ipilumumab + dacar-
\par
bazine, the three methodologies resulted in 2.76, 5.64, and 7.93 years, respectively.
\par
For nivolumab, these resulted in 5.24, 9.81, and 11.17 years, respectively. Cure rates
\par
predicted by the MCPNMA were 20\%, 28\% and 41\%. Conclusions: MCPNMA and
\par
spline NMA both provided a good visual t on the observed data, but resulted in
\par
different lifetime (mean and incremental mean) survival estimates. The MCPNMA
\par
predicts longer mean survival, and the predicted mortality hazards cannot go lower
\par
than the general population mortality (GPM), as it is incorporated in the t of the
\par
cure fraction. Spline hazards, on the other hand, may become lower than GPM over
\par
time. The choice between the models should be informed by the long-term survival
\par
observational data and clinical expert opinion on clinical plausibility of long-term
\par
survival and cure.
