will be. The intuitive explanation for this result is that acquiring information of the effort becomes
\par
important for the MTO if the carriers become more conservative.
\par
5. Conclusion and Future Research
\par
In this paper, we addressed the uncertain incentive contract design problem with an MTO and three
\par
carriers working in sequence in a WRR intermodal transportation setting. For modeling travel time
\par
uncertainty, we formulated an uncertain bi-level programming model for the incentive contract design
\par
problem under the expectation and the entropy decision criteria based on the uncertainty theory. Taking
\par
advantage of the structural characteristics, we first divided the original model into three sub-models and
\par
then used an iterative algorithm to derive closed-form expressions for the optimal time-based incentive
\par
contracts. To broaden the scope of application of this model, we implemented a simulation example in a
\par
WRR intermodal transportation setting. The computational results show that the MTO is more willing to
\par
acquire the carrier's effort level information under the uncertain travel time environment by using the
\par
numerical analysis.
\par
Finally, we conclude by providing some directions for future research. In this paper, we assumed that
\par
the travel times of the transportation stages are mutually independent. A further research path could be to
\par
study the case where the travel times are correlated. Another alternative setting is one where the carrier
\par
may wish to utilize her resources more effectively by varying her effort level over time, which might
\par
extend our static model to the dynamic environment. Last but not least, the other heuristic algorithms for
\par
increasing the performance of achievements could also be considered in future research.
