Appendix A. Why is Fuzzy Variable not Suitable for Uncertain Travel Time?
\par
A fuzzy variable is a function from a possibility space to the set of real numbers. Some people think
\par
that fuzzy variable is a suitable tool for modeling uncertain travel time. Is it really true? Unfortunately,
\par
the answer is negative.
\par
When asked about the travel time, we can expresses the subjective judgment by the terms ``about
\par
24 h''. If ``about 24 h'' is regarded as a fuzzy concept, then we may assign it a membership function, say
\par
\textmu (x) =
\par
(x-22)
\par
2 , if 22 {$\leq$}x {$\leq$}24
\par
25-x, if 24 {$\leq$}x {$\leq$}25.
\par
This membership function represents a triangular fuzzy variable (22, 24, 25). We choose such a
\par
membership function because it is not important for the focus of debate. Based on the membership
\par
function \textmu{} and the definition of possibility measure
\par
Pos\textbraceleft B\textbraceright = sup
\par
x{$\in$}B
\par
\textmu (x),
\par
the fuzzy theory will immediately conclude the following three propositions:
\par
(a) (b) (c) the travel time is ``exactly 24 h'' with possibility measure 1;
\par
the travel time is ``not 24 h'' with possibility measure 1;
\par
``exactly 24 h'' and ``not 24 h'' are equally likely.
\par
However, it is doubtless that the belief degree of ``exactly 24 h'' is almost zero. On the other hand,
\par
``exactly 24 h'' and ``not 24 h'' have the same belief degree in possibility measure. Thus, we have to regard
\par
them as ``equally likely''. It seems that no human being can accept this conclusion. This paradox shows that
\par
this imprecise travel time like ``about 24 h'' cannot be quantified by fuzzy variable because the possibility
\par
measure has no self-duality property. From the above discussion, we can see that the fuzzy variable is not
\par
suitable for modeling uncertain travel time in mathematics and may lead to wrong results in practice.
