\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Inspection and maintenance are important means to validate or recover reliability of metallic structural
\par
systems, which usually degrade over time due to fatigue, corrosion and other mechanisms. These
\par
inspection and maintenance actions generally account for a large part of lifetime costs, which
\par
necessitate an efficient maintenance strategy to satisfy the requirements on reliability and costs. Most
\par
often, an optimum maintenance/repair crack size criterion is derived by probabilistic cost-benefit
\par
analysis, e.g. by minimization of expected lifetime costs, which are assessed based on cost models.
\par
This paper proposes an integrated approach to derive an optimum range for maintenance criterion
\par
using both reliability-based and cost-based optimization. It is found that an optimum repair (crack size)
\par
criterion exists which leads to the maximum lifetime fatigue reliability. A smaller repair criterion than
\par
the reliability-optimum do not lead to a higher lifetime fatigue reliability but leads to higher lifetime
\par
costs. Hence, a limit for repair criterion is defined by the reliability-optimum criterion, which can be
\par
obtained without cost models. The reliability-optimum criterion is found to be smaller than the cost-
\par
optimum criterion and thus an optimum range between the reliability-optimum and cost-optimum
\par
criterion is established.
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Structural systems with a substantial number of welded structural components are at high risk of loss
\par
of structural integrity. The issue can be partially addressed in the design stage by adopting a
\par
conservative approach that will increase structural resistance against fatigue. However, direct and
\par
indirect costs can become very high in excessively conservative designs, that aim to cover for the
\par
numerous sources of uncertainty affecting fatigue loads, fatigue capacity and deterioration process.
\par
In addition, there are inevitable initial cracks or flaws in welded structures after construction, which
\par
typically decrease fatigue lives of structural details and cause crack growth or even fracture. Hence,
\par
maintenance of these fatigue-prone components during operation is an important means of risk
\par
reduction to ensure structural integrity for many industries, e.g. ship structures, offshore installations,
\par
nuclear plants, etc. Cracks can be identified and repaired timely during maintenance interventions
\par
and disastrous failures can be avoided, which if happen would result in significant losses, not only
\par
financially, but also socially and environmentally (Frangopol and Soliman 2016).The maintenance costs do not only include direct costs of manpower, materials and instrumentation,
\par
but also indirect costs, such as transportation, work preparation and planning, downtime loss, etc
\par
(Soliman, Frangopol, and Mondoro 2016). To reduce maintenance costs, it is important to develop a
\par
cost-efficient maintenance plan well in advance. The effectiveness of a maintenance plan depends
\par
on factors such as an accurate modelling of fatigue loading, a good understanding of the fatigue
\par
deterioration process, the choice of inspection and repair methods, the characterization of inspection
\par
quality and maintenance effect, and the selected inspection time and repair criterion. Some of the
\par
uncertainties associated with these factors have been reduced via probabilistic methods. For example,
\par
crack growth under wave loading has been the topic of many studies in the context of marine
\par
engineering (Moan and Ayala-Uraga 2008, Lotsberg et al. 2016, Garbatov and Soares 2001, Doshi,
\par
Roy, and Parihar 2017), and several approaches have been developed to characterize the inspection
\par
qualities of non-destructive testing (NDT) methods, taking uncertainties associated with detection and
\par
sizing of a crack into account (Ali et al. 2018, Schoefs, Cl\'ement, and Nouy 2009, Rouhan and Schoefs
\par
2003). The effects of repair, e.g., the fatigue strength of repaired components, have also been
\par
investigated with simulation and experimental methods (Akyel, Kolstein, and Bijlaard 2017, Abdullah,
\par
Malaki, and Eskandari 2012). This literature contributes to an improved understanding and a broader
\par
knowledge of each of the factors, on which a theoretical basis for developing maintenance
\par
optimization methods can be built.
\par
A complete maintenance plan comprises of a number of maintenance interventions in lifetime,
\par
inspection times and methods, and repair criteria and methods. These maintenance decisions
\par
determine the maintenance costs. The determination of an optimum maintenance strategy that is
\par
optimum strictly in mathematical format and optimum from the perspective of the whole life cycle, is
\par
a complex problem due to numerous challenges. I.e., the possible combinations of maintenance
\par
decisions are substantial, both structural damage states and maintenance activities are subjected to
\par
uncertainties, inspection results are unknown in the planning stage yet influential in optimum decision-
\par
making, optimum maintenance decisions are also dependant on how maintenance practices in reality
\par
are modelled and how the optimization problem is formulated, etc. Thus, appropriate approximations
\par
and assumptions are necessary for developing maintenance decision models or optimization methods.
\par
While some researchers put effort in modelling and comparing the effects of specific maintenance
\par
strategies (Zio and Compare 2013, Huynh, Grall, and B\'erenguer 2017),others intend to utilize
\par
available inspection data to update structural deterioration condition and optimize maintenance plans
\par
accordingly (Dong and Frangopol 2016, Moan and Ayala-Uraga 2008, Soliman, Frangopol, and
\par
Mondoro 2016). Among the maintenance decisions, inspection times are a major concern in many
\par
published research (Lotsberg et al. 2016, Chen, Wang, and Guedes Soares 2011, Straub and Faber
\par
2005, Doshi, Roy, and Parihar 2017, Beaurepaire et al. 2012, Valdebenito and Schu\"eller 2010), as
\par
the lifetime fatigue reliability and expected lifetime maintenance costs are very sensitive to the
\par
scheduled inspection times. Planning methods have been developed with respect to inspection times
\par
based on a given reliability or risk acceptance level (Dong and Frangopol 2016, Chen, Wang, and
\par
Guedes Soares 2011), and optimization methods have been proposed for both inspection times and
\par
qualities based on life cycle costs analysis (Beaurepaire et al. 2012, Valdebenito and Schu\"eller
\par
2010). Repair criterion have also been optimized with the objective of minimizing total lifetime costs
\par
outside the optimization with respect to inspection times (Kim, Soliman, and Frangopol 2013, Soliman,
\par
Frangopol, and Mondoro 2016, Madsen, Torhaug, and Cramer 1991, Straub 2004, Gomes and Beck2014) or with multi-objectives coupled with the optimization with respect to inspection times (Kim,
\par
Soliman, and Frangopol 2013, Soliman, Frangopol, and Mondoro 2016, Madsen, Torhaug, and
\par
Cramer 1991, Straub 2004, Gomes and Beck 2014). Basically, most existing studies derive an
\par
optimum repair criterion adopting the metric of cost, when the optimum repair criteria will provide the
\par
best trade-off between expected maintenance costs and expected failure costs. This paper finds that
\par
an optimum repair (crack size) criterion exists which leads to the maximum lifetime fatigue reliability.
\par
A smaller repair criterion than the reliability-optimum do not lead to a higher lifetime fatigue reliability.
\par
Hence, a limit of repair criterion is defined by the reliability-optimum criterion. An integrated approach
\par
using both reliability-based and cost-based optimization is proposed to derive a range for optimum
\par
repair criterion.
\par
The modelling of fatigue, maintenance strategies, inspection methods, and repair effects are
\par
introduced first to then explain how to derive an optimum maintenance plan and to finally test it. More
\par
specifically, Section 2 simulates fatigue crack growth allowing for probabilistic modelling of the
\par
uncertainties related to the initial damage state, material property, modelling of structural response,
\par
etc., in a consistent way. Section 3 introduces the five maintenance strategies that will be tested. An
\par
equivalent repair criterion is defined to reflect the differences between the conditions of each strategy
\par
to carry out repair. For comparison purposes, time-based and detection-based maintenance
\par
strategies are analysed in addition to condition-based maintenance strategy. Section 4 establishes a
\par
probabilistic model for the quality of an inspection method in terms of crack detection considering
\par
inspection uncertainties. A simplified model is proposed for perfect inspection quality. Both imperfect
\par
and perfect inspection models are integrated into the optimum maintenance planning approach to
\par
assess the influence of inspection uncertainties. Section 5 reviews the methods for characterizing and
\par
modelling repair effect. A widely-used model considering that a structural detail is brought to initial
\par
damage state is adopted. Section 6 presents the proposed probabilistic maintenance optimization
\par
approach based on a physical-based probabilistic crack growth model, probabilistic models for
\par
inspection quality, decision tree analysis, reliability theory, cost modelling and life cycle analysis. The
\par
latter is employed to derive a range for optimum repair criterion. Section 7 illustrates the impact of the
\par
proposed optimum maintenance plans on a typical fatigue-prone detail, and investigates the influence
\par
of inspection uncertainty, the relationship between reliability-optimum and cost-optimum repair criteria
\par
with inspection times, and optimum plans under different strategies. Section 8 highlights the main
\par
contributions of the paper.
