\textbf{2.2 Probabilistic modelling}
\par
Although the FM approach is well suited for fatigue deterioration modelling in support of maintenance
\par
planning, it is generally acknowledged that compared with the S-N approach, the modelling
\par
uncertainty associated with the FM approach is high, as a result of the large number of factors
\par
affecting the complex fatigue deterioration process is considered, and the shortage of validation data
\par
for the FM approach, compared to the S-N approach (Lassen and Recho 2013). Therefore, the results
\par
of the FM approach are sensitive to input parameters, that may lack sufficient statistical information.
\par
Probabilistic modelling based on Paris' law provides an explicit and rational approach for dealing with
\par
uncertainties in fatigue deterioration that is preferred here following (Moan and Ayala-Uraga 2008,
\par
Lotsberg et al. 2016, Chryssanthopoulos and Righiniotis 2006), although there are other approaches
\par
available in the literature such as random process (Gomes and Beck 2014), Markov process (Sheils
\par
et al. 2010, Lassen and S\o rensen 2002b, a), etc.The accuracy of using Paris law for crack growth modelling is believed to mainly depend on the
\par
accuracies of the input parameters: initial crack size {$a$}0, material parameter {$C$} and stress range ∆{$\sigma$}.
\par
In this paper, the initial crack size {$a$}0 and the material parameter {$C$} are modelled as variables. Figure
\par
1 provides a schematic representation of probabilistic crack growth, where {$f$} 1 is probability density
\par
function of {$a$}0, {$f$} 2 is probability density function of crack size at the end of service life {$a$}({$TR$}), and {$P$} {$f$} is
\par
failure probability within the required service life {$TR$}. Uncertainties associated with the calculation of
\par
stress range ∆{$\sigma$} are modelled as an additional variable {$B$}. The mean value and standard deviation
\par
(SD) for the variables are inputs for reliability analysis and maintenance decision-making.
\par
