\textbf{3. Maintenance strategy}
\par
Structural performance usually degrades over time under environmental loads and hazards. A
\par
maintenance strategy is a plan about when and how (the times, conditions, means and actions) to
\par
intervene a structural system to maintain or recover structural performance to a targeted level. An
\par
efficient maintenance strategy should be aimed at maximizing the potential benefits that scheduled
\par
maintenance actions would bring while minimizing the costs associated with the maintenance actions.
\par
For some non-critical structural components, reactive maintenance strategy may be feasible, i.e. a
\par
repair action is carried out after failure occurs. However, this strategy can be risky when the failure
\par
can cause serious consequences, e.g. failure of the whole system, causing pollution to the
\par
environment, danger to crew, etc. Also, the maintenance strategy may not be cost-effective if the
\par
structural system is comprised of many components with high rates of failure. Nowadays proactive
\par
maintenance strategy has been adopted extensively in many engineering fields. Maintenance
\par
interventions are scheduled and optimized with respect to the times, conditions, means and actions
\par
well in advance, and implemented at the scheduled times or under the optimized conditions before
\par
failure occurs. Proactive maintenance is also known as preventive maintenance, since components
\par
are repaired or replaced before failure occurs. Typical preventive maintenance strategies are time-
\par
based preventive maintenance and condition-based maintenance.Table 1 lists the maintenance strategies investigated in this paper. The baseline case is the strategy
\par
MS1, in which no maintenance action is carried out during the lifetime. Time-based maintenance,
\par
labelled MS2, is used to describe maintenance activities which are carried out at specific times or
\par
after a fixed passage of service time (S\'anchez-Silva et al. 2016). The times or intervals for
\par
maintenance actions can be determined by engineering experiences or expert options, or be
\par
optimized based on cost-benefit analysis (Huynh et al. 2012), etc. In the case of a condition-based
\par
maintenance strategy, maintenance actions are carried out only when a specific criterion is met, e.g.,
\par
measured crack size exceeding a repair (crack size) criterion. This strategy requires measurement of
\par
crack size, and then to evaluate whether a specific repair criterion is met or not.
\par
This paper proposes a probabilistic approach to determine the repair (crack size) criterion in a
\par
condition-based maintenance strategy (MS4) allowing for uncertainties in material property, initial
\par
crack size, modelling and inspection. The case of perfect information (provided by inspections),
\par
labelled MS3, is also examined in order to investigate the influence of inspection uncertainty on
\par
optimum maintenance decisions. Additionally, a detection-based maintenance strategy (MS5) is
\par
analysed for comparison purposes.
\par
