\textbf{4. Probabilistic inspection modelling}
\par
Under the condition-based maintenance strategy, inspections are carried out to provide additional
\par
information on crack damage states, that together with the modelling results from Paris' law, provide
\par
basis for maintenance decision-making. Commonly-used inspection methods for fatigue crack
\par
detection are (close) visual inspection and NDT methods, e.g. liquid penetrant, ultrasonic, magnetic
\par
particle, and acoustic emission inspection methods. However, the information provided by an
\par
inspection is typically imperfect. Crack detection by NDT methods is inherently probabilistic, as there
\par
are many factors that can affect inspection results. Existing cracks may sometimes go unnoticed.
\par
Conversely, a positive indication may be false due to the absence of a crack. It is also often found
\par
that an existing crack can be detected by one inspector but missed by other inspectors. Generally,
\par
NDT results depend on the reliability of the specific instrument-human system. The following factors
\par
can influence the chance of crack detection (Georgiou 2007, Wall, Burch, and Lilley 2009):
\par
- Crack characteristics (sizes, shape, location, etc.);
\par
- The reliability of instrumentation;
\par
- The environment where inspection is carried out;
\par
- Inspection procedure;
\par
- Human factors associated with inspector.
\par
The reliability of the instrument-human system and confidence level on inspection results must be
\par
adequately demonstrated in terms of the level of accuracy that can be reached on the true crack
\par
characteristics. The reliability of an inspection method is often characterized by a probability of
\par
detection (PoD) curve. A PoD is defined as the probability that a given crack of a fixed size can be
\par
detected by a given inspection method (Georgiou 2007, Keprate and Ratnayake 2015). PoD curves
\par
for inspection methods are traditionally obtained by inspection experiments on structural details of a
\par
range of crack sizes. Based on inspection results, an appropriate function is assumed for the PoD
\par
curve, and parameters of the function are estimated by statistical methods. The confidence range on
\par
the PoD can be specified based on the estimated parameters. The experimental approach is usually
\par
very expensive and time-consuming as there are so many factors that can affect the probability of
\par
detection. Alternatively, simulation approaches are also developed to obtain PoD curves (Wirdelius
\par
and Persson 2012, Rentala, Mylavarapu, and Gautam 2018, Ali et al. 2018).
\par
This paper models inspection information as imperfect (MS4 in Table 1) or perfect (MS3) in
\par
maintenance modelling and optimization to investigate the influence of inspection uncertainty on
\par
optimum maintenance decisions. Two different PoD functions are applied, as shown schematically in
\par
Figure 2. `Inspection uncertainty' and `Perfect inspection' mean that inspection uncertainty is
\par
considered and neglected respectively.\textbf{Figure 2: Probability of detection (PoD) curves}
\par
For the imperfect inspection, the exponential PoD function given by Equation (6) is employed (Chen,
\par
Wang, and Guedes Soares 2011, Lassen and Recho 2015, Moan and Song 2000, Dong and
\par
Frangopol 2016, Moan et al. 1997, Moan and Ayala-Uraga 2008, Beaurepaire et al. 2012, Valdebenito
\par
and Schu\"eller 2010).
\par
