\par
constant value {$E$}({$ad$}).
\par
Due to inspection uncertainty, there are probabilities of missed repair and fault repair in condition-
\par
based maintenance. A missed repair means that a crack larger than the repair criterion is not detected
\par
by the inspection method, while a fault repair means that a crack smaller than the repair criterion is
\par
detected and believed to exceed the repair criterion. It must be noted that fault repair is not considered
\par
in this paper, and that the probabilities of missed repair under different repair criteria and at different
\par
inspection times are discussed based on the illustrative example of Section 7.\textbf{5. Maintenance effect modelling}
\par
It is the repair or replacement actions that ultimately improve fatigue reliability. A condition-based
\par
maintenance strategy can improve fatigue reliability only when repair actions are carried out following
\par
negative inspection result and exceedance of a repair criterion. A model for the effect of repair action
\par
needs to be developed before the impact of maintenance strategies and maintenance actions on
\par
fatigue reliability can be evaluated and quantified. Commonly-used repair methods for cracks in
\par
structural engineering include:
\par
- Drilling a stop hole;
\par
- Welding;
\par
- Welding plus post-weld treatment;
\par
- Replacement;
\par
- Grinding.
\par
These methods are used to remove cracks, to decrease crack size, and to stop or slow down crack
\par
growth, by which a structural system is physically changed, as well as structural performance and
\par
fatigue reliability. The effects of repair lie in changes in crack size, deterioration rate, fatigue life etc.
\par
Fatigue strength of repaired structural details is investigated by experimental (Akyel, Kolstein, and
\par
Bijlaard 2017, Abdullah, Malaki, and Eskandari 2012, Kudryavtsev, Kleiman, and Knysh 2008) and
\par
numerical (Ayatollahi, Razavi, and Chamani 2014, Achour, Bouiadjra, and Serier 2003, Schubbe and
\par
Mall 1999) methods and some useful conclusions are drawn based on statistical analysis on the
\par
experimental or simulated data. Depending on the study areas and objectives, different repair effect
\par
models have been developed.
\par
{$\bullet$} (Ma and Bea 1995) studies the repair methods used for ship structures and proposes to designate
\par
different classes of S-N curve for details repaired by different methods.
\par
{$\bullet$} Some probabilistic maintenance modelling and optimization methods are developed using time-
\par
based safety margins, and the effect of repair on a structural detail in terms of extension of fatigue
\par
life is modelled as a variable defined by a distribution (Kim, Soliman, and Frangopol 2013, Barone,
\par
Frangopol, and Soliman 2013, Kim and Frangopol 2010, Soliman, Frangopol, and Mondoro 2016).
\par
{$\bullet$} On the other hand, many publications on probabilistic maintenance modelling and optimization
\par
adopt crack size-based safety margins and consider the effect of repair on a structural detail in
\par
terms of mitigation of crack damage.
\par
{$\bullet$} (Straub 2004, Faber, Straub, and Goyet 2003, Straub and Faber 2005, Valdebenito and Schu\"eller
\par
2010, Beaurepaire et al. 2012, Gomes and Beck 2014) assumes that a detected crack is fully
\par
removed by a repair, and that the crack size is zero from the time of repair until the end of lifetime.
\par
The assumption indicates that after repair, the fatigue life of structural detail is sufficiently long,
\par
and that the failure probability caused by fatigue is zero.
\par
{$\bullet$} Most of maintenance planning models to date have adopted the so-called `as good as new'
\par
maintenance model, which means that after repair a structural detail is brought to its initial as-
\par
built state (Garbatov and Soares 2001, Eltaief et al. 2015, Huynh, Grall, and B\'erenguer 2017,
\par
Kulkarni and Achenbach 2007, Madsen, Torhaug, and Cramer 1991, Sheils et al. 2010, Zitrou,
\par
Bedford, and Daneshkhah 2013).{$\bullet$} Under the minimum maintenance strategy, a minimum repair is carried out and a structural
\par
system returns to its previous damage state, i.e., the structural deterioration is improved by just
\par
one stage, which is nearly as bad as the old state (Huynh et al. 2012, Bad\'ia et al. 2018, Aven
\par
and Jensen 2000). The minimum repair model is often used in maintenance planning methods
\par
based on discrete structural deterioration models.
\par
{$\bullet$} Some more sophisticated repair effect models have also been developed based on the idea that
\par
after repair a structural system may be brought to states between the as good as new and the as
\par
bad as old, e.g. the state after repair is a variable with a distribution (Wu, Niknam, and Kobza
\par
2015, Van and B\'erenguer 2012), the effect of repair is linked with deterioration rate of the system
\par
rather than with the deterioration state (Zhou, Xi, and Lee 2007, Zhang, Gaudoin, and Xie 2015),
\par
etc. However, the applications of these sophisticated repair effect models have been very limited.
\par
Figure 3 illustrates the potential impact of four repair effect models following an intervention at time
\par
{$ti$}. This paper employs the commonly-used `as good as new' model (model 4 in Figure 3), i.e. after
\par
repair a structural detail is brought to its initial as-built state (Garbatov and Soares 2001, Eltaief et al.
\par
2015, Huynh, Grall, and B\'erenguer 2017, Kulkarni and Achenbach 2007, Madsen, Torhaug, and
\par
Cramer 1991, Sheils et al. 2010, Zitrou, Bedford, and Daneshkhah 2013). This is a relatively
\par
reasonable model, as it can model the small failure probability of a repaired structure detail. In
\par
practical, fatigue cracks are usually not easy to prevent, and periodical inspections are necessary. It's
\par
often found that cracks propagate again in original areas, and thus there is a small possibility that a
\par
repaired structural detail fails. However, (Straub 2014) has performed comparative study and
\par
concluded that the influence of maintenance model on reliability results is negligible.
\par
