\textbf{6.5 Probabilistic maintenance optimization approach}
\par
The chart in Figure 7 and 8 shows the flow of the probabilistic maintenance optimization approach for
\par
reliability-based and cost-based optimizations. The optimization approach needs to take probabilistic
\par
fatigue crack modelling, probabilistic inspection modelling, reliability theory, decision tree analysis,
\par
cost modelling and life cycle analysis into account. The theoretical basis for the approach is a physical
\par
model for fatigue crack growth, that projects crack size over time probabilistically. Maintenance
\par
interventions are scheduled to renew the structural system upon damage condition, crack detection
\par
or just time, depending on the adopted maintenance strategy. The event margins for failure, detection
\par
and repair are formulated based on the crack size over time. The crack size and inspection result at
\par
an intervention are probabilistic and thus unknown at the planning stage, but the possible inspection
\par
results and repair actions can be obtained from decision tree analysis. The probabilities of inspection,
\par
repair and failure are formulated based on the corresponding event margin. Optimization objectives,
\par
e.g. lifetime fatigue reliability and expected lifetime costs, are formulated based on the probabilities
\par
of inspection, repair and failure, as well as cost ratios and annual discount rate of money. The
\par
reliability-based and cost-based optimum criteria and inspection times are derived for the
\par
corresponding optimization objective. The optimization problem can be solved numerically with the
\par
aid of certain optimization techniques, e.g. genetic algorithms (Kim, Soliman, and Frangopol 2013,
\par
Kim and Frangopol 2011) and subset simulation (Valdebenito and Schu\"eller 2010, Beaurepaire et al.
\par
2012). Alternatively, with the increasing capacity of computational power, an exhaustive searchalgorithm can be used if the number of potential solutions is not very substantial. Practical constraints
\par
can also be applied to limiting the possible solutions. For example, from the perspective of logistics
\par
and management, it may be more convenient to adopt an equal inspection interval, e.g. to carry out
\par
inspections at equal-distant times, as adopted by (Zio and Compare 2013, Huynh, Grall, and
\par
B\'erenguer 2017, Scarf, Wang, and Laycock 1996, Mendes, Coit, and Ribeiro 2014, Cronvall et al.
\par
2012, Zou et al. 2018, Tolentino and Ruiz 2014). With this constraint, the possible solutions are greatly
\par
reduced. Based on the probabilistic optimization approach, a reliability-based repair criterion
\par
{$ar$},{$opt$}
\par
{$Bl$} can be obtained, as well as a cost-optimum criterion {$Cl$}
\par
{$ar$},{$opt$}
\par
. Next, the above approach is applied
\par
to a typical fatigue-prone structural detail of ship structures to determine the recommended range
\par
{$Bl$}
\par
[{$ar$},{$opt$}
\par
, {$ar$},{$opt$}
\par
