\textbf{7. An illustrative example}
\par
A stiffened plate (Figure 9) of a ship structure is chosen to test the proposed probabilistic approach.
\par
Ship structures typically comprise of a substantial number of plates, stiffeners, pillars, and welding
\par
joints. One of the most common fatigue-prone details in ship structures is the welded T joint, which is
\par
also common in other steel structures, e.g. offshore installations, bridge decks, and wind turbine
\par
foundations, etc. Although the stability of the plate is greatly improved by stiffeners, fatigueperformance of the welded joints is likely to be a problem due to initial flaws introduced during welding
\par
processes. Welded joints represent weak areas in structural integrity. Under fatigue loading, failure
\par
can occur under a stress level much lower than material tensile strength and eventually lead to rupture
\par
of the entire cross-section. In order to prevent the latter, integrity and reliability of welded joints should
\par
be examined, assessed and maintained during the operational stage.
\par
\textbf{Figure 9: The studied fatigue-prone structural detail}
\par
\textbf{7.1 Probabilistic model}
\par
The required service life of the ship is 20 years. The ship is trading in the sea environment, in which
\par
the frequency of wave loading is about 0.16Hz, which corresponds to approximately 5 \texttimes{} 106 cycles
\par
per year (Lotsberg et al. 2016, DNVGL 2015). The fatigue resistance of the joints is classified as F
\par
class and given by a two-segments S-N curve, formulated by Equation (3). Values for the parameters
\par
of the S-N curve obtain from rules of ship classification societies e.g. (DNV 2014). In consideration of
\par
inspection accessibility and failure consequences, the joints have been designed with a fatigue design
\par
factor ({$FDF$} = 8), and the allowed maximal equivalent stress range is {$\sigmae$} = 17.28MPa. The design
\par
plate thickness is {$T$} = 25mm. Failure of the structural detail is defined as occurrence of through-
\par
thickness crack. A summary for all parameters is provided in Table 2.
