\textbf{8 Conclusions}
\par
An efficient maintenance strategy is important for lifetime integrity, safety, reliability and cost
\par
management of structural systems with a substantial number of fatigue-prone details. This paper has
\par
proposed an integrated management method incorporating probabilistic crack growth, probabilistic
\par
inspection modelling and probabilistic planning approaches to derive an optimum range for the repair
\par
criterion in a condition-based maintenance strategy which meets requirements on lifetime fatigue
\par
reliability and expected lifetime costs simultaneously. While the repair criterion is almost exclusivelypre-determined or derived based on minimization of expected lifetime costs in existing studies on
\par
probabilistic maintenance optimization, herein it is emphasized that there is an optimum repair
\par
criterion based on maximization of lifetime fatigue reliability. A smaller repair criterion does not
\par
necessarily result in a higher lifetime fatigue reliability. A smaller repair criterion than the reliability-
\par
optimum can lead to a lower or the same lifetime fatigue reliability, but much higher lifetime costs, and
\par
thus should be avoided. The contribution of this paper can be summarized in the following points:
\par
{$\bullet$} An exponential probability of detection (PoD) function has been used to consider uncertainties
\par
associated with the detectability of magnetic particle inspection (MPI), in comparison with a
\par
piecewise function proposed as the PoD for perfect detectability. Both PoD functions have been
\par
employed in optimum maintenance planning. It has been found that the influence of inspection
\par
uncertainty in MPI on lifetime fatigue reliability is marginal. When the repair criterion is set to be
\par
large, e.g. {$ar$} {$>$} 3 mm, a missed repair is unlikely. Missed repair could occur when {$ar$} is very
\par
small, e.g. {$ar$} {$<$} 2 mm. However, the influence of missed repair on lifetime fatigue reliability is
\par
limited if an inspection is scheduled at an early stage of service life. A missed repair can hardly
\par
affect lifetime fatigue reliability if an inspection is scheduled at a late stage of service life.
\par
{$\bullet$} The repair criterion in a condition-based strategy has been derived from maximization of lifetime
\par
fatigue reliability {$Bl$}, based on decision tree analysis, consideration of possible damage states,
\par
inspection results and repair actions at a specific intervention time. A smaller repair criterion than
\par
the reliability-optimum one (i.e., {$ar$}
\par
{$Bl$}) can lead to a lower or the same lifetime fatigue reliability,
\par
but much higher lifetime costs, and thus is consider as ineffective. The reliability-optimum repair
\par
criterion is dependent on the scheduled inspection time, e.g. the reliability-optimum repair criteria
\par
for inspections scheduled at later times are larger than that at earlier times.
\par
{$\bullet$} The repair criterion has also been derived based on the conventional approach of minimizing
\par
expected lifetime costs {$Cl$} , and the reliability-based and cost-optimum repair criterion for a
\par
condition-based maintenance strategy have been compared under the same conditions. It has
\par
been found that for a specific inspection time, the reliability-optimum criterion {$ar$}
\par
{$Bl$} is smaller than
\par
the cost-optimum criterion {$ar$}
\par
{$Cl$}, which indicates that the reliability-optimum requirement is more
\par
difficult to fulfil as more repair efforts are required. When the same repair criterion is adopted, the
\par
reliability-optimum inspection time takes place at a later stage than the cost-optimum inspection
\par
time.
\par
{$\bullet$} The range [{$ar$}
\par
{$Bl$}
\par
, {$ar$}
\par
{$Cl$} ] has been proposed for repair criterion. While a repair criterion smaller than
\par
{$ar$}
\par
{$Bl$} has been considered as ineffective, a repair criterion larger than the {$ar$}
\par
{$Cl$} has been
\par
considered as insufficient, given that the return of maintenance in terms of risk reduction is not
\par
fully reaped by adopting the criterion, although the return rate was higher. A suitable repair
\par
criterion can be selected from the proposed range accordingly to the specific target on fatigue
\par
reliability and constraint on expected lifetime costs, e.g. available budgets.
\par
{$\bullet$} The time-based and detection-based maintenance strategies have also been optimized based
\par
on maximization of {$Bl$} and minimization of {$Cl$} respectively, and the optimum strategies have
\par
been compared with the condition-based maintenance strategy. The results have shown that the
\par
condition-based strategy with a reliability-optimum repair criterion, e.g. {$ar$}
\par
{$Bl$} = 2 mm in theillustrative example, can help to achieve a higher {$Bl$} than the time-based and detection-based
\par
strategy at all intervention times. The time-based and detection-based strategies can be
\par
understood as condition-based strategy with very small repair criterion, and it is known that a
\par
smaller repair criterion that the reliability-optimum one can't lead to a higher {$Bl$}. A condition-based
\par
strategy with a cost-optimum repair criterion has helped to achieve a lower {$Cl$} than the time-
\par
based and detection-based strategies.
\par
{$\bullet$} If multiple maintenance interventions are scheduled, it is not optimum in terms of both {$Bl$} and {$Cl$}
\par
to use the same repair criterion for all maintenance interventions in lifetime. Both the reliability-
\par
based and cost-optimum repair criterion have increased with longer inspection times. Hence, the
\par
optimum repair criteria, whether based on {$Bl$} or {$Cl$}, for maintenance interventions scheduled at
\par
later times are expected to be larger than those scheduled at earlier times.
\par
This paper has developed an integrated reliability and cost-based maintenance optimization method
\par
for one-component structural system (e.g. for one critical hotspot area) and reached some concisions
\par
on the reliability and cost-optimum maintenance (crack size) criterion, ineffective and insufficient
\par
maintenance and time-variant maintenance criteria for multiple maintenance interventions. The
\par
method and conclusions provide fundamental basis for developing optimal maintenance strategies
\par
for multi-component systems, e.g. multiple hotspots. A maintenance strategy for a one-component
\par
system can be applied to a multi-component system in which components are independent by defining
\par
a one-dimensional performance indicator of the system (Grall et al. 2002). Future work on system
\par
level maintenance planning needs to consider dependencies. These dependencies can be properly
\par
modelled and exploited to develop sampling inspection and opportunistic maintenance strategies,
\par
which are promising approaches to efficient allocation of maintenance resources among components
\par
in a multi-component structural system.
