\textbf{Abstract}
\par
This study aimed at identifying the level of the sustainability practices disclosure of Jordanian commercial banks
\par
listed on the Amman Stock Exchange, as well as explore the value relevance of sustainability practices
\par
disclosure and verify its role in shares prices and market returns of Jordanian commercial banks listed on the
\par
Amman Stock Exchange. This study proposes to present the diverse instrument's measurement of the variables
\par
proposed by the literature. In the Price and Return models, stock prices and stock returns are considered as the
\par
dependent variables in order to measure value relevance, while the level of the independent variable is
\par
sustainability practices disclosure. Data were collected from the annual reports of the banks included in the study
\par
sample during the period from 2008 to 2019.
\par
The results indicate that there is a wide gap between Jordanian banks in the level of disclosure of sustainability
\par
practices. The results of the valuation analysis revealed that investors positively appreciate the disclosure of the
\par
sustainability practices of Jordanian commercial banks listed on the ASE. The results of the analysis revealed
\par
that the explanatory power of all models of the study increases significantly when the variable related to the level
\par
of disclosure of sustainability practices is included in the model.
\par
\textbf{Keywords: }sustainability, sustainability practices disclosure, value relevance, shares prices, market returns,
\par
Jordanian Commercial Banks
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
\emph{1.1 Study Background}
\par
Companies are increasingly concerned about their responsibilities to society and the environment in which they
\par
operate because of global changes, as the pressures that companies face from consumers and society drive them
\par
to adopt sustainable business strategies (Li et al., 2019). This has led companies to increase the scope of
\par
sustainability practices disclosure in many countries around the world tremendously, as companies began to
\par
issue independent sustainability reports from their annual report (Mohammadi et al., 2018). When the concept of
\par
sustainability is applied to banks it implies the incorporation of the objectives of social equality, economic
\par
efficiency, and environmental performance into the banks operating practices (Miralles-Quir\'os et al., 2018).
\par
Corporate disclosures about sustainability practices for companies are consistent with the interests of various
\par
parties including investors, managers, employees, clients, regulators, and government (Clarkson et al., 2013). In
\par
2000, a joint reporting framework on sustainability was developed by the Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) in
\par
the Netherlands, where it included the Sustainability Reporting Guide, which consists of reporting principles and
\par
specific content indicators. In 2006, GRI released a new version of the sustainability reporting guide called G3
\par
(third generation) and the latest is the fourth generation that has been effectively implemented since the
\par
beginning of 2015. In line with this, the studies provide evidence of the increasing importance of sustainability
\par
practices disclosure (Li et al., 2019). By implementing the GRI guidelines, companies show a strong
\par
commitment to continuously improve their disclosures about sustainability practices. These developments about
\par
the disclosure of sustainability practices make it necessary to examine their implications as to the value that they
\par
can add to the company.
\par
75mas.ccsenet.org Modern Applied Science Vol. 13, No. 9; 2019
\par
\emph{1.2 Study Problem}
\par
Contemporary debate on sustainable enterprise development has raised awareness of sustainability practices.
\par
This poses emerging challenges for companies to do their business in a more ethical and responsible manner. At
\par
the same time, successful disclosure of corporate efforts with regard to corporate sustainability practices to
\par
stakeholders is another challenge for managers (Qiu et al., 2016). As the disclosure requires companies to make
\par
great efforts and high costs (Garc\'ia-S\'anchez et al., 2019). This is accompanied by uncertainty by companies and
\par
management as to whether the reports meet the objectives required by the stakeholders and meet their
\par
requirements, and provide useful rules for investment decisions, which must have a bearing on the market value
\par
and stock returns of the company (Brooks \& Oikonomou, 2018).
\par
Despite the great scientific interest in the issue of value-relevance in various areas of business research, the
\par
results are still inconclusive, the empirical literature did not reach to achieve consensus on the economic
\par
consequences of disclosure of sustainability practices (Baboukardos, 2018). Several studies have indicated that
\par
more empirical studies should be conducted on the value of disclosure of sustainability practices, both for the
\par
company and for stakeholders (Clarkson et al., 2013).
\par
The laws and regulations did not oblige Jordanian companies to disclose sustainability practices. Therefore, all
\par
corporate sustainability reports are voluntary disclosures by companies based on their strategic orientations, their
\par
understanding of the importance of sustainability reports, and the level of disclosure of sustainability practices
\par
among Jordanian companies in general, and in banks, In particular, this study conducted to identify the value
\par
relevance of sustainability disclosure practices in Jordanian commercial banks
\par
\emph{1.3 Study Objectives}
\par
The objective of this study is to identify the level of sustainability practices disclosure of Jordanian commercial
\par
banks listed on the Amman Stock Exchange, as well as to:
\par
1. Explore the value relevance of sustainability disclosure and verify its role for different
\par
stakeholders.
\par
2. Identify the impact of disclosure of sustainability practices on the shares prices of Jordanian
\par
commercial banks listed on the Amman Stock Exchange.
\par
3. Identify the impact of disclosure of sustainability practices on the market returns of Jordanian
\par
commercial banks listed on the Amman Stock Exchange.
\par
4. Provide a set of recommendations that can contribute to enhancing corporate practices in relation
\par
to the disclosure of sustainability practices
\par
\emph{1.4 Study Importance}
\par
The theoretical importance of this study is highlighted in its support for theoretical studies and research on the
\par
importance of the level of disclosure of sustainability practices in Jordanian commercial banks and their role in
\par
enhancing value for different stakeholders.
\par
The importance of this applied study stems from the importance of disclosing the sustainability practices of the
\par
various stakeholders' bodies, as well as the importance of the Jordanian banking sector. , And conducting such a
\par
pilot study is critical to improving the current understanding of market reactions to the disclosure of
\par
sustainability practices in Jordanian commercial banks, where different stakeholders such as the state and
\par
investors in the market Securities great interest in strengthening the role of companies in sustainable
\par
development and improving corporate transparency.
\par
\textbf{2. Theoretical Background \& Experimental Studies}
\par
\emph{2.1 Theoretical Background}
\par
Increasing corporate disclosure of information, including sustainability information, enables stakeholders to
\par
collect information about the company easily and at a lower cost, increasing their ability to predict the future
\par
income of the company, which will be reflected in market share prices (Song, 2015). There are many theories
\par
and assumptions that explain the level of disclosure of information on sustainability practices with investor
\par
behavior and market reactions to this information.
\par
Under the efficient market hypothesis, optional disclosures, including disclosure of sustainability practices,
\par
affect equity prices equally for all market participants (Kim et al., 2014). Hence, it can be assumed that
\par
disclosure of sustainability practices will be valuable to stakeholders and will affect stock prices and directly
\par
reflect on market share prices and returns (Aerts et al., 2008). The random walk theory assumes that prices
\par
76mas.ccsenet.org Modern Applied Science Vol. 13, No. 9; 2019
\par
change and move randomly, depending on new market information (Didar et al., 2018). Therefore, the volatility
\par
of stock prices will reflect both previous information and any future market events, so disclosure of sustainability
\par
information will be closely related to adding value to investors as it will increase the ability of investors in
\par
financial markets to predict future events. Thus, an increase in the level of voluntary disclosure will increase
\par
equity price volatility and affect market returns. The Signaling theory emphasizes the importance of the
\par
information that companies disclose in the investment decisions of the external parties of the company (Elfeky \&
\par
Nasiri, 2017). The Signaling theory assumes that information is inconsistent between the management of the
\par
company and the external parties that are interested in the information (Ratnatunga \& Alam 2011). The
\par
information disclosed, including sustainability practices, and current and future, and helps them in the process of
\par
investment decision-making (Agle et al., 2008). This theory suggests that companies aim to refer to the quality
\par
and real value of companies by providing more information to parties that lack information by increasing the
\par
level of disclosure they provide to different stakeholders (Healy \& Palepu, 2001).
\par
Increased corporate disclosure also provides a signal that helps financial analysts predict corporate earnings,
\par
assess their performance, and provide financial market customers with reports about corporate quality and value
\par
(Lang et al., 2004), Hamrouni et al. (2015) argues that the increased circulation of information about companies
\par
in the media and financial analysts' reports increases the level of trading of shares in the financial markets, which
\par
has an impact on the stock market return.
\par
According to the agency theory, the asymmetry of information between management and managers increases
\par
agency costs. In this case, disclosure of sustainability practices is one way to ensure that agency problems are
\par
minimized and costs are minimized (Jo \& Harjoto, 2014). Owners therefore seek to increase the level of
\par
disclosure of sustainability practices to protect their interests and maximizing their wealth. (Freeman, 1984)
\par
Managers are also motivated to provide more reliable information and to increase the level of disclosure of
\par
sustainability practices given the fact that organizations are constantly seeking additional funds from capital
\par
markets at the lowest possible cost. Improving the level of disclosure of sustainability practices helps to reduce
\par
the cost of the company's capital in order to obtain financing, which is reflected in the performance of the
\par
company and the market returns of its shares (Garc\'ia-S\'anchez et al., 2019).
\par
According to the legitimacy theory, there is a general perception or assumption that the actions of any entity are
\par
desirable, valid or appropriate under a system of rules and values based on social (Suchman, 1995). It, therefore,
\par
focuses on the relationship between the company and its operating environment and recognizes groups of
\par
stakeholders at the conceptual level (Ioannou \& Serafeim, 2015). According to the legitimacy theory,
\par
stakeholders assess companies based on their perceptions of their values and organizational value, and that the
\par
survival of the organization will be threatened if society realizes that the organization has violated its social
\par
contract (Luo et al., 2015). The legitimacy theory has been widely used in relation to social, economic and
\par
environmental disclosures (Garc\'ia-S\'anchez et al., 2019), indicating that companies do not work for the benefit
\par
of their investors only, but must ensure that their actions do not adversely affect the environment in which they
\par
operate and thus avoid pollution and other illegal activities (Oliveira et al., 2013). Therefore, the disclosure of
\par
sustainability practices is a means by which management can influence the perceptions of society in the
\par
environment in which it operates, increasing the acceptance of the community, thus improving its financial
\par
performance and increasing the liquidity of its shares, which will positively affect its market returns.
\par
Stakeholder theory is closely linked to the legitimacy theory, where stakeholder's actions are expected to affect
\par
the company's ability to successfully implement its strategies and achieve its objectives, Examples of primary
\par
stakeholders include shareholders, creditors, employees, customers, suppliers, and regulators (Freeman, 1984).
\par
Stakeholder theory suggests that companies are not only responsible for their shareholders, but must make
\par
decisions for all stakeholders and encourage stakeholder management theory The integration of personal values
\par
in the company's strategy and constitute a more ethical practice, and emphasizes the importance of the
\par
relationships that the company needs to establish with stakeholders (Freeman \& Parmar, 2004).
\par
\emph{2.2 Experimental Studies}
\par
Studies on corporate disclosure began in the 1960s. Cerf (1961) study was the first study measure the level of
\par
disclosure in annual reports, the study found a relationship between the level of disclosure and profitability of the
\par
company. Benjamin \& Stanga (1977) study found that there was an impact for the level of disclosure provided
\par
by companies on investment decisions in the financial markets, indicating the value relevance of disclosure.
\par
Spero (1979) argues that the need for finance is one of the most important drivers of companies to increase their
\par
level of voluntary disclosures, as this affects investor decisions and increases the liquidity of their shares and
\par
reduces lenders' terms and interest rates. Al-Issa (1988) was the first study in Jordan that examined the value
\par
77mas.ccsenet.org Modern Applied Science Vol. 13, No. 9; 2019
\par
relevance of disclosure, pointing out that there is an impact of the disclosures made by companies in investment
\par
decisions. Similarly, Abu-Nassar and Rutherford (1996) found that the information expressed by companies in
\par
Jordan is very important to the users' decisions. The results of the study show that annual reports are considered
\par
as a major source of information, followed by visits to companies and communication with management.
\par
Eizentas et al. (2012) also found value relevance for information in the investment decisions of traders in the
\par
financial markets, and the information of the announcement and the volume of information disclosed are
\par
important factors affecting the prices of shares and their market returns.
\par
With regard to sustainability disclosure, it began late with the 1987 Report Brundtland Report (Jones, 2011).
\par
McGuire et al. (1988) found that the company's previous performance which is assessed through both the stock
\par
market returns and measures based on accounting is linked to corporate social responsibility. McWilliams \&
\par
Siegel (2001) found a neutral relationship between corporate social responsibility and financial performance.
\par
Hassel et al. (2005) noted that there is an impact of environmental performance on the market value of Swedish
\par
companies. Murray et al. (2006) did not notice any correlation between stock returns and the release of corporate
\par
social responsibility. In contrast, Jones et al. (2007) found a strong correlation between disclosure of
\par
sustainability and stock returns, through a detailed review of the literature found in accounting literature.
\par
Schadewitz and Niskala (2010) findings suggest that the sustainability report can play an important role in
\par
addressing the market value of the company. Similarly, De Klerk and De Villiers (2012) concluded that
\par
corporate social responsibility disclosure is strongly and positively correlated with equity prices. Moneva and
\par
Ortas (2008) did not find a direct correlation between sustainability performance and stock valuation for a
\par
sample of European companies. In contrast, Lourenco et al. (2012) reveal that corporate sustainability has
\par
material explanatory power in stock prices.
\par
The results of the Reverte (2016) showed a strong and consistent correlation between stock prices and CSR
\par
reports through stock price fluctuations in response to CSR disclosure reports In Jordan, Abu Farha and
\par
Alkhalaileh (2016) found that there is no statistically significant relationship between the level of disclosure of
\par
social responsibility and market returns. Miralles-Quir\'os et al. (2018) results support the value enhancing theory,
\par
as the study concluded that the market appreciates positively and large environmental practices implemented by
\par
companies Mohammadi et al. (2018) found sustainability reporting is linked to market assessments in which
\par
companies active in environmentally sensitive industries with sustainability reports have higher market ratings
\par
than firms in non-sensitive industries with sustainability reports. Abu Karsh and AL-Deek (2019) did not find a
\par
relationship between disclosure of social responsibility and corporate profitability measured by (ROA, ROE, NP,
\par
and EPS) Garc\'ia-S\'anchez et al. (2019) found that providing more information about CSR initiatives for the
\par
company is particularly valuable for those who finance companies, as more disclosure of CSR practices
\par
facilitates access to finance.
\par
\textbf{3. Study Methodology}
\par
This study proposes to present the diverse instrument's measurement of the variables proposed by the literature.
\par
In the Price and Return models, stock prices and stock returns are considered as the dependent variables in order
\par
to measure value relevance, while the level of the independent variable is sustainability practices disclosure.
\par
Data were collected from the annual reports of the banks included in the study sample.
\par
\emph{3.1 Study Sample and Population}
\par
The study population consists of all 13 Jordanian commercial banks listed on the Amman Stock Exchange,
\par
during the period from 2008 to 2018. The study sample has represented of all banks.
\par
\emph{3.2 Sources of Data}
