value of the F statistic (F=10.839) is (Prob F = 0.000) less than 0.05, which means that the effect of independent
\par
variables aggregated is significant. This refers to the relevance of information implied in sustainability disclosure,
\par
and it reflect on stock market and return of bank (R).
\par
The relationship between the level of sustainability disclosure and the market returns can be explained by several
\par
assumptions and theories. Asset prices in the financial markets under the efficient market hypothesis reflect all
\par
relevant information about these assets (Fama, 1970). Therefore, information disclosed by companies will affect
\par
stock prices and is reflected directly in their market returns. In view of the Random Walk Theory, fluctuations in
\par
stock prices will reflect both previous information and any future market events, so market returns are closely
\par
linked to the level of sustainability disclosure provided by companies. The increase in sustainability disclosure
\par
will increase the ability of investors in financial markets to predict future events, which will neutralize the
\par
systematic behavior in the price chain, so the actual prices of securities will be randomly centered around
\par
intrinsic value (Thicke, 2017). Hamrouni et al. (2015) argue that the increased circulation of corporate
\par
information including sustainability information in the media and financial analysts' reports increases the level of
\par
trading of shares in the financial markets, which has an impact on its stock market returns..
\par
82mas.ccsenet.org Modern Applied Science Vol. 13, No. 9; 2019
\par
The relationship between sustainability disclosure and market shares returns can be explained in the according to
\par
Legitimacy Theory. This theory considers that sustainability disclosure is a means by which management can
\par
influence the perceptions of society in the environment in which it operates, increasing community acceptance
\par
and thus improving its financial performance, and increase the liquidity of its shares, which is reflected in their
\par
market returns (Moerman, L \& Van der, 2005). Which is also linked to Stakeholder theory. Stakeholders have a
\par
great deal of control over the company's resources. Increasing the level of sustainability disclosure may improve
\par
communication with stakeholders and improve their financial performance, reducing funding costs in relation to
\par
Reduce staff costs for owners, increase customer loyalty, assist them in obtaining production inputs at the lowest
\par
cost by improving their relationship with suppliers, and reduce the costs associated with regulators, which will
\par
affect the revenue streams And the strength of its shares (Nielsen \& Madsen 2009).
\par
This finding is in line with what McGuire et al. (1988) arguing that the company's previous performance, which
\par
is assessed by both stock market returns and accounting measures, is linked to CSR. And Jones et al. (2007),
\par
which concluded that there is a strong correlation between disclosure of sustainability and equity returns. As well
\par
as Mohammadi et al. (2018) who pointed out that the sustainability report is linked to market valuation of stock
\par
prices, which is reflected in market returns.
\par
\textbf{5. Conclusions}
\par
There is considerable debate in the academic realm about whether corporate behavior in disclosing sustainability
\par
practices is in line with shareholder interests. The interest of companies, shareholders and various stakeholders in
\par
sustainability practices and their role in maintaining corporate growth and prosperity has increased, which is why
\par
academic studies have grown that will decompose the impact of sustainability practices in stock markets.
\par
However, most of the literature on corporate sustainability practices and value creation has been conducted in
\par
developed countries and little is known about the impact of sustainability on equity prices and market returns in
\par
emerging markets, including Jordan. It is, therefore, necessary to close this gap in the literature on sustainability
\par
practices.
\par
In this sense, the Jordanian situation was analyzed. Given its unique economic, natural and human resources, it is
\par
particularly important to examine how investors assess the sustainability performance of Jordanian banks. More
\par
specifically, we have contributed to increasing understanding of the advantages and disadvantages of investors to
\par
consider corporate disclosure of sustainability practices by analyzing the impact of the disclosure of
\par
sustainability practices in stock prices and market returns of Jordanian commercial banks during the period 2008
\par
to 2018.
\par
The results of the valuation analysis revealed that investors positively appreciate the disclosure of the
\par
sustainability practices of Jordanian commercial banks listed on the Amman Stock Exchange. The analysis of the
\par
study models (1) and (2) related to the prices of bank shares in the study sample revealed that the disclosure of
\par
sustainability practices increases the explanatory power of the model from (21.0\%) to (30.8\%). The analysis of
\par
the study models (3) and (4) related to the market returns of the banks in the study sample revealed that the
\par
disclosure of sustainability practices increases the explanatory power of the model from (15.8\%) to (30.6\%).
\par
These results have important implications for investment decisions in the ASE, as well as for the managers of
\par
these banks and policymakers. This provides relevant information on the implications of share prices and market
\par
returns as a result of the disclosure of sustainability practices in Jordanian commercial banks for consideration
\par
by investors when making their investment decisions. Furthermore, we should note that investors are crucial
\par
stakeholders and can exert a significant influence on the sustainability strategy of the companies they own.
